1250	Thomas Aquinas, the famous Theologian held the view that the literal sense of the scripture was foundational to all its other senses. He insisted that every legitimate spiritual interpretation was rooted in the literal sense of the passage. He also taught that nothing necessary to faith was communicated as a spiritual meaning without being taught elsewhere In scripture in spiritual sense. He insisted in principle that every sense of a passage of scripture must be tied to its literal meaning. But this view was not adopted by every one. \nUnfortunately this method was misused by medieval Church and asserted that they had special God given insights in to spiritual meanings to support many doctrines. \nBut renaissance scholars began to study classical, literary, philosophical and religious texts in their original language. They began to interpret the text apart from the authority of the Church. The protestant Scholars believed that scripture's literal sense is both singular and plural. They saw the literal sense as a complex meaning that included historical, doctrinal, moral, and eschatological aspects. \nThe most responsible approach to the richness of the scripture is to ensure that everything we say about a biblical passage is tied to its grammar set within the historical context of the ancient world. Then we will be able to discover the complex meaning that God and his inspired authors intended to communicate to the original audience of the scripture.	8	2019-09-18 15:13:39+05:30	2019-10-22 19:09:04+05:30	\N	319	687
1251	Biblical elaborations are places where one part of scripture directly or indirectly comments on an aspect of the meaning of another passage in scripture. \nThe Bible elaborates the commandment against murder in many places. It is recorded first in Exodus 20: 13. The first elaboration of this passage we see a repetition in Deuteronomy 5:17, where Moses reminded people of Israel about the law. Repetition confirmed the command and reminded the terms of covenant. \nThe second type of elaboration we see as a clarification of the command in Numbers 35:20-25, where Moses distinguished between murder and accidental man slaughter. \nThe third type of biblical elaboration was expansion, in which scripture provides additional information about the passage. We see this in Matthew 5: 21-22. There Jesus criticized the Rabbis of his day for wrongly limiting the scope of the commandment. Here the command is expanded beyond physical act of unlawfully taking a life. According to Jesus' elaboration, unrighteous anger violates the same principle that murder does. \nThe biblical elaborations confirm other parts of the Bible by repeating them, by clarifying them and even by expanding on their original meaning.	9	2019-09-18 15:25:19+05:30	2019-10-22 19:09:04+05:30	\N	319	688
1252	The interpretation of the scripture can be interpreted relevantly without misapplying them by considering the following key aspects in interpretation.\n1. The original meaning. The original meaning of a passage means the concepts, behaviors and emotions that the divine and human writers jointly intended the document to communicate to iys first audience. The original meaning is always multi faceted and there is complexity with it. So the reformers looked in to both the grammatical and historical setting. The original meaning contributes to the full value of the scripture. \n2. The Biblical Elaboration. It is places where one part of scripture directly or indirectly comment on an aspect of the meaning of another passage. These elaborations always confirm the original meaning. Scripture is repeated, clarified and expanded. \n3. Legitimate Applications. It is the impacts that the original meaning and biblical elaborations of a passage should have on their audience. All legitimate applications of scripture must be derived from and consistent with the Bible's original meaning and elaborations. \nIn the complexity of the meaning, the scripture can be interpreted effectively and relevantly by giving heed to the original meaning, biblical elaborations and legitimate applications.	8	2019-09-18 15:37:26+05:30	2019-10-22 19:09:04+05:30	\N	319	689
1253	The following are the intentional fallacies that can be made by interpreters. \n1. Over emphasizing the Author. We might make un warranted assumptions about an authors' intentions by speculating about what he was thinking when he wrote. \n\n2. Over Emphasizing Document. This error is known as a graphic fallacy. This over emphasizes the document itself to the relative exclusion of contextual considerations like the writer and the audience. We might commit this fallacy by thinking that we can sufficiently grasp the meaning of passage merely by analyzing its vocabulary, grammar and structure without author or original audience. \n3.  A third type of interpretive mistake we can make is over emphasizing audience. This is often called an affective fallacy because it focuses too heavily on how scripture affects its audience. \n\nThese fallacies mostly happen when our information  about the writer, document and the audience are limited. When our information more limited, its generally wise for our interpretations to be more generalized. We need to properly account for the interdependence of the guidance we receive from the writer, the document and the audience, to avoid these fallacies.	9	2019-09-18 15:50:33+05:30	2019-10-22 19:09:04+05:30	\N	319	690
1254	Making multiple summaries of the same passage is a meaningful method to extract the true meaning of the scripture passage. Multiple legitimate summaries bring us closer to the passage's full meaning. \n\nAll interpreters come to biblical writings with different sets of concerns, assumptions, background and questions. Each of us read scripture differently because God has gifted us differently. \nPeople's gift and backgrounds lead them to summarize biblical passages in distinct ways. The personal strength of the interpreters lead each to bring out important but different aspects of passage's original meaning. \n At the same time, both types of summaries cam also be hindered by interpreter's' weaknesses. \nInterdependence on other interpreters is a way beneficial for better understanding. We need to learn from what others have seen from the scripture than we study the scripture by ourselves only. \nGod intended the Bible and biblical interpretations to be understood in a context of fellowship with other believers. \nDifferent people receiving insights of the scripture according to their gifts and back grounds can help each other in knowing the truths and bringing out more relevant and legitimate applications to the context.	8	2019-09-18 16:05:02+05:30	2019-10-22 19:09:04+05:30	\N	319	691
