1267	It is recognized by the theologians that the progressive nature of God's rule over creation has resulted in periodic developments that create discontinuity between the various epochs mentioned in the scripture. Perhaps the most obvious epochal development occurred between the old and New Testaments. \nTheologians commonly identified epochs according to God's various covenants through out Bible. The laws regarding atoning sacrifice required different things at different points of redemptive history. In patriarchal period, it was family altars; Moses' period, the Tabernacle; Solomon's period, the temple; early NT period Jesus's death on the cross and in the later N T, they ceased to be offered altogether. \nIt is true that the Old testament and New Testament laws differ in their context and content but they are not contradictory. The animal sacrifice of the OT are not relevant to us, but they are fulfilled in Christ. \nBiblical history is like a growing  tree, grows from seed to a sapling and to a fully grown tree. Everything that the tree will become is contained in the seed. But over time tree has to grow and develop to full maturity. \nSimilarly redemption has grown and developed through out biblical history. This developmental modal teaches that the whole Bible is relevant and authoritative for us. The older revelations must be applied in the light of the later revelations.	9	2019-10-22 15:43:18+05:30	2019-10-22 19:11:49+05:30	\N	331	694
1268	Many theological changes occurred in OT history where God altered his expectations regarding the concepts, behaviors and emotions of his people. When the changes were significant enough, they became basis for identifying epochal divisions. Each epoch is a period of time characterized by substantial and long lasting transitions in OT theology. \nThe following are the variety of ways theologians divided OT history. Theologians tend to use theological criteria to describe the epochs. Just as the growth of a tree, some parts of the OT theology advanced slowly, others at a medium pace and others rapidly. \nOn the basis of the genealogy of Matthew, the OT history is divided in terms of Abraham to David, David to exile and Exile to Christ. Also on the basis of Romans 5 and 1 Corinthians 15, OT history divided as the period before the law and after the law. \nOne of the most popular ways to divide OT history is by associating each epoch with one of God's covenants. God's covenant with his people always entailed significant theological shifts and there fore provide helpful boundaries for epochal divisions. The covenants are associated with Adam, Moses, David and finally with the New covenant in  Christ. \nThese covenants represents times when God moved mightily in history and introduced long lasing theological emphases. Each of God's covenant established different ways in which he related to human beings, and each provided his faithful people with new principles to follow in His service.	9	2019-10-22 15:58:13+05:30	2019-10-22 19:11:49+05:30	\N	331	695
1269	Old Testament divisions of history shows that God wanted his people to understand and apply theological themes in different ways in different times. Just as it was in the OT period, the NT believers also should never apply the scriptures as if they live in earlier period of history. \nA person living in Mosaic period is not supposed to go back to patriarchal period and build altars. A person from Solomon's time is not to go back to Mosaic time of Tabernacle. Once God commanded a new way for sacrifice in worship, he expected his people never to return to the old ways. \nAs we live in the epoch of New Covenant, we are not supposed to go back to the sacrifice and worship system of the OT. All the OT rituals and practices fulfilled in Jesus Christ and his atonement.\nHebrew 8:13 says that the arrival of the New Covenant made the old ways Obsolete, using the Greek term 'palaioo' which may also be translated ' made old' or out dated. \nThis does not mean that Christians should discard the OT and pay no attention to its teaching. The author of the Hebrews was not telling the OT is irrelevant rather he was saying that we live in a different epoch. we don't ignore the old ways, but we should never try to serve God as if we still lived in the old days. \nAlso in every age, people need to follow the military leadrership God established for the age. Eg. Judges , Kings etc. We as Christians follow the king Jesus and practice the NT principles of spiritual warfare fighting against the evil forces.\nGod has always expected his people to serve him in ways that were appropria	8	2019-10-22 16:13:16+05:30	2019-10-22 19:11:49+05:30	\N	331	696
