1295	The identity of the Authorship of Hebrews is a difficulty as the it is not revealed in the letter. Earlier most of the Scholars believed of Paul's authorship. But Scholars also suggested Barnabas, Luke, Apollos and even Clement of Rome as authors. Origen commented' only God knows the truth of the matter'. \nDue to this fact , Hebrews was avoided from Marcionite canon and Muratorian canon. But by the end of patristic period the majority of the interpreters in the eastern and western Church come to recognize Hebrews as part of Canon. They generally agreed Pauline authorship. \nTheme like Melchizedek, Tabernacle, Christ as High priest distinguishes Hebrews from other Pauline letters.  Tee strong reason for doubting Pauline authorship is the way of Hebrews distanced himself from the first generation of Jesus' followers. Paul mentions in letters that he received the gospel from the Lord. Gal 1:11,12. But in Hebrews 2:3 contrasts this thought and says the salvation was announced by the Lord and confirmed by those who heard him. Also it is the opinion of the Scholars that it was a Hellenistic Jew who wrote it because of the quotes from Septuagint. The vocabulary and style shows the evidence of a mastery of the language. So Pauline authorship is questioned by most of the critical Scholars.	8	2020-01-08 15:13:43+05:30	2020-01-10 19:07:57+05:30	\N	363	776
1296	The Book of Hebrews does not identify the audience by name, city or region, but he wrote to an audience familiar to him. He shows his intent to visit them. 13:19-24. Following are the five important factors about the audience. \n1. Jewish: A good portion of the original audience were Jewish. Hebrews 1:1. Here the author referred, how God revealed himself to Israel in the Old Testament. \n2. Hellenistic: The content of Hebrews indicates that the audience was familiar with theological teachings that were more common among Jews living outside of Palestine. People like Clement referred the place as Rome on the basis of Hebrews 13:24. \n3. Immature: Hebrews 5 : 12 address them as spiritual babes; though they were supposed to be teachers. They need to be taught the elementary truths of the word. \nHow ever the book contains most advanced , sophisticated theological teachings in the entire NT. \n4. Persecuted: It become evident in the book that the original audience had already faced persecution in the past. Some were suffering in the present. 10:32-35. Also there is a warning of expected persecution in future. \n5. Near Apostacy: They were in danger of turning away from Christ. 10: 26-27. They were warned of Apostacy and judgement if they turn away from Christ.	9	2020-01-08 15:25:29+05:30	2020-01-10 19:07:57+05:30	\N	363	777
1297	The author of Hebrews had many literary devices to express his exhortations of which Rhetorical device appears common. \n1. Synkrisis: this is a rhetorical device used in Hebrews 7:111-28 where a detailed comparison between two or more things expressed. The author described an eight point comparison between Melchizedek and Christ: their parentage, genealogy, birth, death, office, actions, status and achievements. \n2. Exempla: These are list of examples or illustrations that follow onw after another to build arguments. This is seen in Hebrews 11. \n3. Qolwahomer: This is a Hebrew expression which is well known in Greeco - Roman and Rabbinical traditions. It can be translated ' light t heavy or ' less to great' or 'simple to complex'. An example of this thought is  seen in Hebrews 10:28-29. \nWith these literary styles the author was expressing the seriousness of the situation and convincing them to make right decisions.	8	2020-01-08 15:35:40+05:30	2020-01-10 19:07:57+05:30	\N	363	778
1298	The original audience of the book of Hebrews were believers of long enough for the author to say ' by this time you ought to be teachers". But they needed to be taught elementary truths of God's word all over again. \nHowever the book contains some of the most advanced, sophisticated theological teachings in the entire NT. Balancing these two facts is a difficult task. The early Christians adopted a practice of reading and explanation of the scripture from synagogue practices of first centaury. Just as in the synagogue, elders used to teach the scripture to the congregations.\nSo the author of Hebrews wrote some of the most theologically sophisticated NT writings because he expected Church leaders to teach this to the congregation. \nAccording to Hebrews 5:11, it is vivid that the original audience were slow in understanding the truths of the scripture and obeying their leaders Hebrews 13:17 underlines this fact.	9	2020-01-08 15:43:41+05:30	2020-01-10 19:07:57+05:30	\N	363	779
1299	To challenge the false doctrine and to accomplish the goal of calling his audience to faithful service to Jesus, the author organized his exhortations to five major divisions. \n1. The supremacy of Christ over Angelic Revelations. 1: 1-2:18. Refuting all Jewish false teachings about the angels as superior beings, the author was establishing the superiority of Christ over them.\n2. Jesus is above Moses and his authority. 3:1- 4:13. The local Jewish community called for Christians to return to Moses' teachings and his ways. Here the author affirmed his point that Jesus was even greater than Moses, the faithful servant, because he was God's royal son. \n3. Melchizedek's priesthood 4:14 -7:28. Here the author argued that Jesus was the supreme royal priest after the order of Melchizedek. Local Jewish community had the idea that Melchizedek will appear the great high priest in the last days. So the author presented Jesus as the true royal priest appeared in the last days. \n4. Supremacy of the New covenant in Jesus 8: 1- 11:40. The teachings of the local Jewish community raised doubt about the Christian claim of new covenant made by Jesus. The author affirmed that Jesus is the mediator of the New covenant promised by Jeremiah. \n5. Practical perseverance. 12:1- 13:25. In the light of so many challenges to their from the local Jewish community and else where, the author wrote to inspire and energize his audience. Exhorted them to remain faithful, reminding promises and blessings in Jesus.	9	2020-01-08 15:59:17+05:30	2020-01-10 19:07:57+05:30	\N	363	780
