1329	Authority -dailog Objective acknowledges that objective truth can be found in the text of the scripture . the Bible is GOd,s word and revelation to us , and everything its says is objectively true and meaningful. And the methods of interpretation can help us to understand this revelation as long as the methods comply with biblical standards. \nAuthority dialog and subjective: it acknowledges that we all come to the Scriptures with perspectives and beliefs that influence the way we interpret biblical passages\n. It agrees that the personal and subjective input  we bring is valuable.\nit indicates that investigating the Bible is a life long process ij which Scripture changes us and causes us to grow and mature in our Christian Faith	8	2020-03-26 07:32:12+05:30	2020-03-27 09:25:25+05:30	\N	395	686
1341	The famous theologian Thomas Aquinas insisted that the literal sense of Scripture was foundational to all its other senses. Unlike his other contemporaries , he insisted that every legitimate spiritual interpretation was rooted in the literal senses of a passage. He also taught that nothing necessary to faith was communicated as a spiritual meaning without being taught elsewhere in Scripture in the literal sense. Although Aquinas efforts to anchor spiritual meanings in the literal meaning of the Bible may seem like common sense to us ,his pint of view wasn't adopted by everyone. The church authorities asserted that they had special God -given insights into every spiritual meanings that had no connection to the literal meaning f the Bible. Not all scholars would agree that Aquinas always followed these principles as he interpreted the scriptures.	8	2020-03-31 06:11:58+05:30	2020-04-01 22:54:28+05:30	\N	398	687
1342	Biblical elaborations are places where one part of Scripture directly or indirect,y comments on an aspect of the meaning of another passage in scripture. These elaborations always record with and confirm original meaning as all Scripture is inspired and infallible.\nThree types of elaborations used are :\n1: as repetition of a facet of the original meaning\n2: as a clarification of things that weren't entirely apparent  or well understood\n3:as an expansion of the meaning of  particular passage.\nThe first Biblical elaboration is primarily a repetition of the words as in You shall not murder (Exo20:13) and in Deu5:17 again You shall not murder. This repetition confirmed  the commandment and reminded God; people of the terms if his covenant.\nThe second type of elaboration was clarification as we see in numbers 35:20-25. Here we see a clarification that provides information that was important to understand the commandment against murder.\nThe third type of elaboration was expansion , in which Scripture provides addition all information about the passage or topic it references.	9	2020-03-31 06:27:15+05:30	2020-04-01 22:54:28+05:30	\N	398	688
1343	The more we study the Scriptures , the more we see that the Bible elaborates on Itself over and over. The prophets and Psalmists regularly refer back to Moses, law. Jesus continually referred back to the old testament. And New testament writers did it the same again and again. But Biblical elaborations confirm other parts of the bible by repeating them, clarifying them and expanding them under the inspiration of the Holy Spirit.  so, as we explore the meaning of Scriptures , we must acknowledge and submit ourselves to all places where the Scripture elaborate on themselves. And the legitimate applications of Scripture must be derived from and consistent with the Bible's original meaning and elaborations. As our applications are not inspired by God, we can make mistake s, so our applications must be subject to improvement and modification.	8	2020-03-31 06:41:56+05:30	2020-04-01 22:54:28+05:30	\N	398	689
1559	All true moral goodness is rooted in God Himself. God's attributes are inseparable from his person : they define who he is. All God's essential attributes are immutable - they can never change.\nGoodness refers to moral purity and perfection . God is the ultimate standard of morality. There is no external standard of goodness by which he or we can be judged.\nHumanity was created to reflect God's goodness.Whatever God blesses and approve is good,and whatever God curses and condemns is evil\nGod blessed Adam and Eve, because they had innate attribute of goodness.\nGod appointed humanity to be his vassal kings on the earth and to fill , subdue and rule it for his glory.	8	2020-06-01 01:01:54+05:30	2020-06-02 07:34:40+05:30	\N	444	854
1344	The writer, the document and the audience are three  interdependent guides to meaning. If we fail to account for their interdependence , its very easy to fall into error.\n 1: INTENTIONAL FALLACY-If we overemphasize the author, we often fall into intentional fallacy. An intentional fallacy relies too heavily on what we think we know about a writer and his intentions , and de-emphasizes the things we learn about the document and the audience.\n2: GRAPHIC FALLACY: when we overemphasize the document to the reclusion of contextual considerations of document and audience. the word graphe means writing.this is a fallacy because the same document can mean very different things on who wrote it or for whom  it was written\n3: AFFECTIVE FALLACY: A third type of interpretive mistake we can make is over emphasizing the audience. It focuses too heavily on the how Scripture effects  the audience. This happens when an interpreter speculates too much about the mindset of the audience and fails to pay attention to the writer and audience.	9	2020-03-31 06:57:02+05:30	2020-04-01 22:54:28+05:30	\N	398	690
1345	All interpreters come to  Biblical writings with different sets of concerns , assumptions, background, and questions. Each of us read the Scriptures differently because God has gifted each of us differently. We all  have strengths and weaknesses ,and we process information  in a variety of ways, based on our knowledge and experience, God has designed this church so that we each can benefit from the strengths of others. Peoples gifts and backgrounds leads them to summarize Biblical passages in distinctive ways. A historian may summarize differently from an  artist. A historian describe s about the order and an artist describes about the beauty. SO the personal strengths of interpreters lead each to bring out the important but different aspects of the passages original meaning.\nMaking multiple summaries helps us to focus on the individual's aspects of meanings and it also allows us to learn more and more of the passages s full meaning. There can be many legitimate ways to summarize the meaning of the passage in the Bible. and these summaries cans help us better understand the aspects of its meaning. And taken together, multiple legitimate summaries bring us closer to the passages's full meaning.	9	2020-03-31 07:14:00+05:30	2020-04-01 22:54:28+05:30	\N	398	691
