1367	The word synchronic derives from two Greek words the preposition sun which means 'with' or 'together with' and the noun' chronos' which means 'time'. When the word synchronic is applied to historical events, it describes occurances that took place 'together in time' or 'at the same time.' We will use the term synchronic to indicate how biblical theologians often explore sets of events in old testament history that happened at the same time. \njust as a movie director conveys his whole film by breaking it down into small series which tells a portion of old story although it being all one unit. Similarly synchronic studies takes an approach in synchronic synthesis biblical theologians concentrate their attention on particular  periods of time in the old testament as if they were scenes in a movie rather than on the flow of its entire history.	8	2020-04-05 21:06:40+05:30	2020-04-10 21:53:07+05:30	\N	403	660
1368	Synthesis means combining different components of something into a whole. We describe the ways different components of theology revealed in a particular period of history fit together in a coherent logical structure. Critical theologians believed that logic was a primary feature of what they call the Greek mindset, but it was foreign to the Hebrew mindset. Based on a number of linguistic and cultural assessments, they argued that the Greek focused on abstraction and logical order, much like systematic theology. and by contrast they suggested that the Hebrew mindset looked at everything in terms of historical dynamics. from this point of view, the old testament did not focus on logical systems or on theological relationships between beliefs. and for the this reason, to synthesize old testament theology was to misread the Hebrew bible and to force it into a Greek framework.	8	2020-04-05 21:12:24+05:30	2020-04-10 21:53:07+05:30	\N	403	661
1369	Poets of the old testament were interested in two different worlds that tell us about history. Old testament poetry often conveyed information about the revelatory acts and words of God in the past, present and future. Old testament poets also focused on the world of their readers which we will call 'their world'. They focused on their world by designing their texts to influence the lives of their original readers in particular ways. \nOld testament narratives were designed to have many different influences. They were doxological, leading readers to praise and worship God. They were theological, explaining truth about God. Some were political, focusing on current national events as well as polemical, opposing false teachings. They were moral, explaining how God's people should leave. They were motivational, encouraging every kind of faithful response. They were designed to teach early readers about their lives.	7	2020-04-05 21:19:45+05:30	2020-04-10 21:53:07+05:30	\N	403	662
1370	Modern evangelical often make the mistake of expecting Old testament narratives to be like modern journalistic historical writings. Old testament stories were only as comprehensive as suited the didactic purposes of their writers. They did not include every significant fact. Consider an example from the book of Chronicles when the writer of Chronicles composed his history of Solomon's life in 2 Chronicles 1-9, he followed the record of 1 kings 1-11 fairly closely. But he omitted every negative dimensions of Solomon's Reign. In  the second place, Old Testament authors were only as precise as their didactic purposes required. They were only as precise as they needed to be to reach their didactic goals like in example, Gen 1;7, where Moses wrote about earth's atmosphere.\nOld testament narratives are not objective by Modern Standards.	8	2020-04-05 21:28:13+05:30	2020-04-10 21:53:07+05:30	\N	403	663
1371	We must say that trustworthy historical accounts must be comprehensive, precise and objective. that is to say that it will include every significant fact about a situation to give a balanced account. They will report details with exacting precision, or will at least acknowledge that they haven't. and they will avoid all subjective evaluations that may prejudice readers. Yet, the authors of Old Testament narratives did not completely follow these modern ideals. Now they did not propagate religious fantasies. Nor did they present historical errors or fabrication as fact. But they did write in ways that where determined largely by their didactic purposes and not by our modern sensibilities.	7	2020-04-05 21:40:57+05:30	2020-04-10 21:53:07+05:30	\N	403	664
