1372	English term "diachronic" derives from the two Greek words; first, the preposition "dia" which often means 'through' or 'throughout' and second the Greek noun "chronos" which means time. Diachrony is a concern with the passage of time. In the case of biblical theology the term 'diachronic' points to the ways Old Testament theology transformed, changed, or developed through time. It will help to see how Diachronic approaches to the Old Testament relate to synchronic synthesis, how it stands in contrast with synchronic synthesis. A diachronic approach however, is much more concerned with changes that occurred in theological structures. It asks questions like, ''what changes in theology occurred as God acted and spoke in different ways during this period of time?"	8	2020-04-07 21:07:08+05:30	2020-04-10 21:52:39+05:30	\N	413	665
1373	The term "development" instead of simply speaking of diachronic changes to suggest two main ideas. First, changes in Old testametical structures always move toward fulfilling God's purposes for history. and Second, we speak of development because theological changes only occur through God's providential involvement in history. The scriptures affirm that everything in history including shifts in theology always accomplish unfailing purposes for his creation. \nDivine purposes from one end to another the scriptures affirm that everything in history including shifts in theology always accomplish God's unfailing purposes for his creation. \nDivine Providence; while are right to speak of changes in Old testament theology as developmental because they fulfill God's purposes, we must also keep in mind how complex this development was. God revealed himself through His actions and His words in many different ways and for this reason developments in old testament theology also took place in many different place. \nAt the same time, some theological developments that took place in old testament often appear to us as random or inexplicable.	9	2020-04-07 21:15:45+05:30	2020-04-10 21:52:39+05:30	\N	413	666
1375	The six Old testament covenants fall into three main categories; First the universal covenants with Adam and Noah, second the national covenants with Abraham, Moses and David and third, the new covenant predicted by old testament prophets.\nUniversal Covenant is the covenant as ''universal'' because they were made between God and all of humanity. The covenant with Adam refers to the governance of divine- human relations established in the opening chapters of Genesis. \nNational covenant; God established national covenant with his special people Israel the covenants with Abraham, Moses and David. The covenant with Abraham emphasize promises for numerical promise of Israel and possession of promise Land, from which Israel was to spread God's blessings to the entire world. and for this reason Abraham's covenant maybe covenant of promise. Similarly, covenant with Moses was known as covenant of law and covenant with David was known as covenant of Kingship.\nNew Covenant: The new covenant was designed to govern God's people when God fulfilled promises to reestablish his people after exile and to spread his kingdom through them to the ends of the earth. For this reason we may speak of the new covenant as ''the covenant of fulfillment''.	8	2020-04-07 21:25:30+05:30	2020-04-10 21:52:39+05:30	\N	413	667
1376	Biblical typology is the study of diachronic developments between the theological structures closely associated with significant persons, institutions and events in scripture. Typology is the study of types. the word"type" derives from the Greek word "tupos". In three important passages New Testament writers spoke of particular theological structures in the Old testament as types of other theological structures in New testamental faith. \nIn Romans 5:14, Paul declared Adam was a pattern was the one to come. From the larger context we know that the one to come is Christ, so in this case Paul observed that Adam was a type of Christ. \nIn 1 Peter 3:20,21- the new testament counterpart to an Old Testament type is designated the "anti type'' so in this example christian baptism is presented as a new testament counterpart to Noah's flood.\nIn Colossians 2:17-  the apostle Paul once spoke of Old Testament ceremonial law with a significant variation in terminology.\nJesus spoke a typological connection between |Moses' bronze serpent and himself in John 3:14,15. Jesus compared the bronze serpent with his crucifixion without any special terminology. Serpent was the type of crucifixion and crucifixion was the Anti type of serpent.	8	2020-04-07 21:35:00+05:30	2020-04-10 21:52:39+05:30	\N	413	668
