1400	Jesus redeeming work: his inauguration of the kingdom of God; his obedience to the Father; his resurrection; Inauguration of Kingdom; God's people longed for the day when God would bring his kingdom to earth. Jesus brought the kingdom of God to earth in his day. This was the day when humanity's original mandate would finally be fulfilled. God would completely restore his creation, and his will would be done on earth just as perfectly as it is already done in  heaven. \nObedience; Jesus lived a life of perfect obedience to the father, culminating his death on the cross. And by his obedience, he won the blessing that Adam had lost. \nResurrection; Jesus' bodily Resurrection was critical to his work of redemption. By raising from the dead, Jesus conquered death itself and secured the everlasting physical life of everyone that has faith in Him.	8	2020-04-17 22:24:05+05:30	2020-04-21 10:27:02+05:30	\N	420	764
1401	General Resurrection: At Christ's return, everyone that has ever died, will be resurrected. Both the wicked and the righteous will be given new bodies that will endure forever. This is clearly taught in John 5;28,29, where Jesus spoke these words:\nA time is coming when all who are in their graves, will hear the Son's voice and come out- those who have done good will rise to live and those who have done evil will rise to condemned. Believer's bodies will be free from corruption and presence of sin. Bodies of unbelievers will continue to be plagued by the effects of sin.\nLast Judgement; Immediately after general resurrection, Jesus will exercise his authority and power as king by destroying all his enemies and blessing all his faithful people at the last judgement. Every human being will be included in the last judgement.\nRenewal of Creation: Just as Jesus will judge humanity and purge the unbelievers from it, he will also purge and renew the creation itself.	8	2020-04-17 22:30:46+05:30	2020-04-21 10:27:02+05:30	\N	420	765
1402	Peter's apostolic confession confirmed that Jesus was the Christ. His affirmation of Jesus' anointing to the office of Christ was direct revelation from God. Peter's confession as the spokesman for the apostles was more than that. It was an authoritative prophetic revelation from God. As such, it was an infallible confirmation of that fact that Jesus really was the Christ.\nTransfiguration: Jesus took 3 disciples to a mountain to pray while Moses and Elijah appeared to Jesus. God's voice was heard from heaven affirming that Jesus was his son. And when Peter suggested that the disciples build shelters for them singled out Jesus as being worthy of the greatest honor and obedience.This was significant because Moses was the lawgiver and Elijah was the faithful prophet, it meant Jesus  stood in continuity with the law and prophets and that He was fulfilling the expectations set by the greatest leaders of Israel's past. it also meant that he was the final Davidic heir who was bringing God's kingdom to the earth.	10	2020-04-20 22:07:35+05:30	2020-04-20 23:46:21+05:30	\N	423	766
1403	Jesus fasted for 40 days in the desert before being tempted by Satan. Jesus overcame temptation to bring salvation to his people.\n1] Obedience: As we see in the temptation he suffered, Jesus obeyed God both by keeping the requirements of his law and by submitting the Father's plan for His life. And his obedience prepared him for his work as Christ. \n2] Sympathy: Jesus didn't give into temptation but still felt it acutely. He recognized that the things Satan offered him, where desirable and weakened condition from fasting must have amplified his longing for them. This experience caused him to grow in compassion and understanding for us as we suffer and struggle with temptations.\n3] Impeccability: means inability to sin. Jesus never sinned. He never succumbed to temptation, or has a evil thought or desire or said a sinful word. because Jesus is impeccable, his obedience and sympathy are increased.	9	2020-04-20 22:14:31+05:30	2020-04-20 23:46:21+05:30	\N	423	767
1404	Crucifixion was a form of death penalty used in the ancient roman empire. Jesus' crucifixion was unique, of course, because it also served as an atoning offering for sin. \nWhen we speak of the imputation of our sin to Jesus on the cross, God counted Jesus as if he had personally committed every sin imputed to him. Once our sin was reckoned to his account, God viewed him as being guilty of all the sins that were assigned to him. Christ's role as our substitute is reflected in the fact that bible often refers to him as our sacrifice. It's also why he is called our ransom. \nHuman death is always a divine judgement against sin. Jesus' death was also a divine judgement against sin. Before laid our guilt on Him, Jesus couldn't' die. But once our sin was imputed to him on the cross, his death became not only possible but necessary.	9	2020-04-20 22:20:57+05:30	2020-04-20 23:46:21+05:30	\N	423	768
1405	Plan of redemption: God's plan to redeem humanity and the rest of creation depend on him, fulfilling his covenant promises to establish his kingdom on the earth under the kingship of a descendant of David, otherwise known as the Christ.\nJesus resurrection was a critical step that enabled God to fulfill his covenant promises.\nBlessings of Salvation: The new testament associated Jesus resurrection was a wide variety of blessings we receive as a part of salvation. It results in our justification, which is the forgiveness of our sins. Its the source of regeneration of our spirit, and it opens the door for our eternal inheritance. It produces good works and a true witness to Christ in pour bodies and lives. And it's the source of future bodily resurrection of believers, when will have glorified bodies just like the one Jesus has.	9	2020-04-20 22:26:50+05:30	2020-04-20 23:46:21+05:30	\N	423	769
