1488	The letter clearly identifies a man named 'James' as the author. Two men named James had enough authority in the early church to write a letter like this: James, son of Zebedee and James the brother of Jesus- leader of the early church in Jerusalem. Critical scholars still offer at least three arguments for this objection. Three arguments by scholars for\n 1) Psuedonymity: No relation to Jesus- there is no mention of the author's relation to Jesus but Jude never mentioned his blood ties in his epistle either. \n2) Hellenistic culture- the book gives evidence that the author was aware of Greek culture. Many Jews in Palestine had knowledge of Hellenistic philosophy and religion. \n3) Inconsistencies- some ideas in the epistle don't match James' theological outlooks in other new Testament books(Acts 21:17-25, Galatians 2:12). James position on the law is actually consistent with his theology in Acts and Galatians.	8	2020-05-12 22:08:29+05:30	2020-05-19 07:11:20+05:30	\N	447	771
1490	James lived and ministered in Jerusalem until he was martyred in AD 62. His audience was most likely scattered throughout Judea and Samaria, as far as Phoenicia, Antioch and Cyprus. Date- The earliest likely date for the letter's composition is AD 44. He became a significant leader of the early church in Jerusalem, by the time Peter was released from prison in AD 44. The latest possible of composition for the epistle is AD 62, when he was matryed. Two reasons to think that the date of composition was earlier in this timeline: James' use of the word: 'Synagogue"- James may have written before Christians were forced out of the synagogues. \nPurpose- No mention of Jewish- Gentile controversies- it's likely that they hadn't yet become a factor for James' audience. He called his audience to pursue wisdom from God, so that they would joy in their trials. These trials appear to have led some to abandon their loyalty to Christ and pursue "friendship with the world".	8	2020-05-12 22:21:58+05:30	2020-05-19 07:11:20+05:30	\N	447	773
1491	James' epistle reflects a long history of wisdom literature stemming from the Old Testament; Job, Ecclesiastes, Proverbs."wisdom psalms", prophetic wisdom sayings. James also reflects the contents of influential wisdom books outside of Scripture: (5:11) to promote perseverance. Beyond this, James touched on topics such as speech, the treatment of widows and orphans, poverty, and favoritism. These topics reflect numerous parallels with the content of the book of Proverbs. \nThe wisdom of Sirach (e.g James 1:5, and Sirach 1:26). The wisdom of Solomon. In addition to these types of wisdom literature, much of Jesus' instruction recorded in the Gospels is characteristic of wisdom teaching in Israel. And interpreters have noted a number of similarities between James' writing and Jesus' instruction.	8	2020-05-12 22:28:51+05:30	2020-05-19 07:11:20+05:30	\N	447	774
1492	First words about the wisdom began with a call to joy. James 1:2- Consider it pure joy, my brothers and sister, whenever you face trials of many kinds. This passage may seem odd to us, especially because it addresses people who were facing " trials of many kinds". But James' appeal to consider this pure joy is not as unusual as we might think. The phrase "pure joy" comes from the Greek expression pasan charan that may be translated unmitigated joy." This kind of encouragement fits well with other wisdom literature of James' day. Many times wisdom writings encouraged those who suffered to consider themselves blessed. Jesus, for instance, closed the beatitudes in Matthew 5:12 with the call to "rejoice and be glad" in the face of persecution. It's often difficult to see the benefits of suffering so James told his readers to pursue wisdom from God(1:5). Wisdom strengthens our confidence that God has ordained for us the blessing of eternal salvation.	9	2020-05-12 22:38:06+05:30	2020-05-19 07:11:20+05:30	\N	447	775
