1496	Jesus redeeming work for sinners, consisted of his appearance in the flesh as the redeemer of mankind, in full obedience to the Father's will and accomplishing everything in accordance with the Father. Mainly can be categorized as follows :\n\nInauguration of Kingdom : Jesus brought the Kingdom of God on this earth during His days. Jesus taught and preached about the kingdom of God, sayinv that the Kingdom of God is come unto all, and also invited everyone to be partakers of the kingdom of God. \n\nJesus fulfilled all the requirements of the law in obedience to the Father, As the first Adam sinned due to disobedience, the second Adam, Jesus through obedience full filled the task of redemption. Jesus life on earth was in perfect obedience to the Father's will, even unto death on the cross. And by His obedience, Christ gained the lost blessings back to mankind. \n\nBible speaks about the passsive obedience wherein his submission  to a life of humiliation and suffering, culminating to His death, fulfilled God's requirement as punishment for the sins of mankind. Through the life of Jesus on this earth, His obedience to God, His sufferings and death fulfilled God's mandate of judgement for all of mankind. \n\nAlso there is the Active Obedience - wherein Jesus obeyed the laws of God. Jesus completely obeyed God's commandments, life was without sin. Fulfilled God's requirement of righteousness, called as forensic righteousness. Hence gave an opportunity for mankind to approach heavenly Father, who looks allows us to approach the throne of grace through His Son Jesus.	8	2020-05-15 21:14:47+05:30	2020-05-19 07:12:30+05:30	\N	448	764
1497	The consummation of the heavens and the earth, during Jesus return to the earth, the following will happen with regards to the last judgment :\n\nDestruction of all God's enemes, the blessings of His peop.le and the complete renewal of creation itself, where God's redeemed people will dwell forever. \n\nJesus Return : \nJesus return will both be a physical and personal return. He will return the ame way as He ascended to heaven, just like the angels had advised. \nIt will be both a public and visible return. Everyone will see Him.\nJesus second coming will be triumphant. He will return as a conqueror, as a King of Kings & Lord of Lords. \nJesus return will be sudden, like a thief in the night. Only the Father in heaven knows when Christ will return.\n\nThe events that will take place during Jesus returns.\n\nGeneral Resurrection : \nAll of mankind, who has died will all be resurrected. Both the wicked and righeous. The righteous will be transformed into heavenly bodeis and will live for ever. . But the wicked and unrigheous will face judgement. No one will escape the process. The righteous will receive a uncorruptible body, to live with our God for ever. \n\nLast Judgement\nAll of mankind - wicked and unrigheous will stand before the seat of Judment and will have to give account of their life on the earth. Every aspect & deed will be judged as per the standards of God. \nBut those of mankind who received forgiveness by grace will escape the seat of judgement, and they will receive their rewards according to their works during their sojourn on the earth. \n\nRenewal of Creation \nThe present world and creation will be purged of all sins and and will be destroyed with fire. Thereafter God will bring about the New Jerusalem adorned from heaven. There will no more be any sorrow, sickness or pain.  God Himself will be the light. \nAll of creation will be restored back to God's original plan and everyone will live for ever with God. Everyone will partake from the Tree of Life and worship God forever and ever.	9	2020-05-15 21:36:02+05:30	2020-05-19 07:12:30+05:30	\N	448	765
1498	The majority of interpreters reject Pauline authorship. \nFirst, Anonymity: this book is anonymous and it was Paul's practice to name himself in his epistles. In fact, as 2 Thessalonians 2: 2 makes clear, Paul was deeply concerned that forgeries had spread under his name. So, it seems unlikely that he would have failed to identify himself had he written Hebrews. \nSecond. Themes, the book of Hebrews emphasizes subjects that don't receive much, if any, attention in Paul's letter. For instance, author of Hebrews mentioned Melchizedek three times. He drew attention to the Old Testament tabernacle. And he dealt at length with Christ as the high priest. Taken together, these themes distinguish the book of Hebrews from books that we know were written by Paul. \nThird. Generation: the strongest reason for doubting Pauline authorship is the way the writer of Hebrews distanced himself from the first generation of Jesus' followers.	9	2020-05-17 22:07:04+05:30	2020-05-19 07:11:47+05:30	\N	449	776
1499	The author wrote to a specific audience with whom he was personally familiar. \n1. Jewish- the author referred to the Old Testament Israelites as "our forefathers". It's no wonder, that from as early as the time of tertullian, who lived from AD 155-230, the title attached is "Pros Hebraious","For the Hebrews". \n2. Hellenistic- Hebrews contains theological teachings that were common among Jews living outside of Palestine. The content of Hebrews indicates that the audience was familiar with theological teachings that were more common among Jews living outside of Palestine than among more traditional Jewish circles within Palestine.\n3. Immature- The original audience may have remained theologically immature because they didn't respect their leaders. In heb 5.11- the author reprimanded his audience for being ''slow to learn''. \n4. Persecuted- As we read, it is evident that original audience has already faced persecution in the past, some in the present, perhaps more severely in future.\n5. Near Apostasy- as the audience faced persecution, some of them were near apostasy. Rather than simply being discouraged or weakened by sufferings, they were in danger of turning away from Christ entirely.	9	2020-05-17 22:18:13+05:30	2020-05-19 07:11:47+05:30	\N	449	777
1500	Hebrews employs many literary devices that were associated with persuasive oratory or urgent debate in 1st century:\n1. One rhetorical device called synkrisis is a detailed comparison between two or more things designed to convince audience to affirm speaker's point of view, For eg, Heb 7:11-28. Rather than asserting his belief, author gave a compelling eight point comparison between Melchizedek and Christ. \n2. Another one is exempla. Exempla are lists of illustrations or examples that follow one after the other to build a persuasive argument for a paticular point of view. This technique appears in familiar list of faithful in Heb.11\n3. Third is known as Hebrew expression qol wahomer. This expression is well known from both Greco- Roman and rabbinical traditions and can be translated "light to heavy" "less to great" etc. This argument says that because simple premise is true then surely the more difficult conclusion must also be true.	8	2020-05-17 22:26:32+05:30	2020-05-19 07:11:47+05:30	\N	449	778
