1525	We have many commitments in our lives.We are loyal to various people such us or families, friends and fellow Christians. and we are also committed to organizations  such us churches , companies etc. We are not committed to all these things to the same degree. And for us , one commitment should stand above all others  which is our commitment to God.This commitment should govern the fundamental decision of our entire life and all our other commitments should serve this most basic one. \nin Luke 6:45 Jesus referred to our commitments as the things that are store up in our hearts . And our commitments always express themselves in our works. So we express our commitments to God in good works and we express our commitments to sin in evil works. I am fully committed and dedicated to the expansion of the Kingdom of God by giving my time , energy and all resources that I have in my life for His glory.	8	2020-05-25 01:03:28+05:30	2020-06-02 07:32:05+05:30	\N	453	865
1508	1. Factual Backgrounds- the book of Hebrews quotes refers to or alludes to the old testament nearly 100 times. The author of Hebrews incorporated historical facts into his presentation of the christian faith. (Hb 7:2). \n2. Theological outlooks- the author focused on theological beliefs affirmed by the Hebrew scriptures. God's son, angels are serving spirits, lower than angels, God's oath to Abraham, God as a consuming fire.\n3. Moral Obligation- Old testament moral requirements were to remain as standards in New Testament times: not to rebel, not to be discouraged, adhere to righteousness, confidence in God( Hb 13:6)\n4. Eschatological Predictions- the author used several old testament eschatological predictions to show that God's final judgement and blessings are fulfilled in Christ: angels will worship, current arrangement destroyed, universal sovereignty, royal priesthood from God, no further sacrifices etc.\n5. Dynastic ideals- the author referred to a number of dynastic ideals that were established for David's lineage in the Psalms. Jesus is the perfect fulfillment of the ideas of David's house, royal son to rule nations, vindication shared with Israel, devotion of body to God. (Hb10.5, Ps 40.6-8)	8	2020-05-20 22:11:18+05:30	2020-06-02 07:26:32+05:30	\N	454	781
1509	The author of Hebrews wrote that his audience should follow Jesus because Jesus is God's supreme source of divine revelation. The author acknowledge that God spoke through angels throughout Old Testament history. But Jesus brought revelation that was far superior to any revelation given through angels. In Hb 1:5-14, Jesus is greater than angels because He alone is God's messianic son. Jesus fulfilled ideals for David's house, fulfilled David's prediction of victory of God's messianic son over all God's enemies. By Contrast, he noted that angels are nothing more than spirit sent to serve those who inherit salvation in Christ. \nIn 2:1-4, the author exhorted of the great message of salvation by Jesus and reminded them that violating messages from angels received punishment from God. \nHb. 2:5-9, supports belief in the supremacy of Christ by explaining that Jesus is now ruler over angels and in the future all believers will reign over them with him. And finally, Heb 2:10-18, author demonstrated that Jesus is Abraham's descendant. And Jesus will share his Glory with Abraham's descendants not with angels.	8	2020-05-20 22:19:55+05:30	2020-06-02 07:26:32+05:30	\N	454	782
