1526	Ethics is the study of moral right and wrong, the study of what is good and evil. Christian ethics is; theology viewed as a means of determining which human persons acts and attitude receive God's blessing and which do not. \nOur definition focuses on God and his blessing rather than on terms like good or evil, or right or wrong. Those things that receive God's blessings are good and right, whereas those things that do not receive his blessings are wrong and evil. \n1. Divine Nature- God himself is the ultimate standard of right or wrong, of good and evil. God is not accountable to any standard outside himself. \n2. Divine Action- God's actions demonstrate the standard of morality. God shows His approval by bestowing blessings. He shows his hatred by withholding blessings and pouring out curses.	8	2020-05-25 22:06:06+05:30	2020-06-02 07:32:43+05:30	\N	455	826
1527	Unless a work is done with a right motive, it is not a work that God will reward with blessings.\n1. Faith- Only believers who are indwelled by the Holy Spirit can do works that God rewards with blessings. Only believers have hearts that are purified by faith. Saving faith motivates good works. This is the faith possessed by believers and only by believers.\n2. Love- Our works are useless if they are not motivated by love. Works and even spiritual gifts that produce beneficial results merit no reward if they are not motivated by love. Love is an aspect of every law God requires us to obey. If we do not act in love, no work we do can conform to his standard. Our love must be for both God and neighbor.	8	2020-05-25 22:10:41+05:30	2020-06-02 07:32:43+05:30	\N	455	827
1528	a. Proper Motive- Unless a work is done with right motive, it is not a work that God will reward with blessings.\n Faith- Only believers who are indwelled by the holy spirit can do works that God rewards with blessings and have hearts that are purified by faith, and saving faith motivates good works.\n Love- If not with love, our works are useless and our works and spiritual gifts that produce beneficial results will bring no reward.\nb. Proper Standard- In order for works to be good, they must be according to the standard of the word of God. \n1. Commands- All commands of scripture are designed to guide us. Everyone who sins is guilty of lawlessness so all sin entails lawlessness. All sin violates God's law. \n2. All Scripture- God's word as a whole is a criterion of good works. Paul insisted that all scripture was useful for ethical training, that all scripture places moral demands on us.\n3. General Revelation- God's revelation given through creation is a part of standard for good works. \nc. Proper Goals- Good works may have any number of immediate goals. God approves of works intended to bring him glory and he condemns works that disregard or oppose his glory.	8	2020-05-25 22:22:41+05:30	2020-06-02 07:32:43+05:30	\N	455	828
1529	Ethical judgement involves the application of God's word to a situation by a person. Ethics should be done from three perspectives: -God's word -situation -person\nThe best approach is to do ethics from all three perspectives and to let the insights from each perspective inform and influence the others.\n1. Situational- Problems, Consequences of Actions and Goals. \n2. Normative- God's word is the norm and standard for ethics. We are doing ethics from the normative perspective when we look to the bible to tell us what to do. \n3. Existential- When we approach ethics by asking questions that are specific to the people involved we are doing ethics from an existential perspective.	8	2020-05-25 22:27:39+05:30	2020-06-02 07:32:43+05:30	\N	455	829
1530	God has the ultimate prerogative to determine whether or not particular actions, emotions or thoughts meet or transgress his moral requirements. God will make the ultimate determination of whether of not we have lived morally or immorally and his judgement will be utterly binding. As the absolute judge of morality, God consistently applies absolute moral standard of his character in every judgement he makes. His judgement are perfect, exhibiting flawless insight and wisdom, unfailing fairness and impeccable morality.	8	2020-05-25 22:31:54+05:30	2020-06-02 07:32:43+05:30	\N	455	830
1531	Traditionally, theologians have spoken of God's revelation primarily in two categories: special revelation and general revelation. In the category of special revelation, they have placed direct communication from God such as scripture, prophesy, dreams and visions. \nGeneral revelation has been a catchall category to hold everything that is not considered to be special revelation. It cannot teach us everything but it speaks clearly enough to teach us true things about God. \nand existential revelation, God's revelation in persons, revelation that is often grouped with general revelation but which really deserves to be treated separately.	8	2020-05-25 22:37:23+05:30	2020-06-02 07:32:43+05:30	\N	455	831
1532	Existential Revelation is a different way of categorizing a portion of general revelation. God uses councils, ancient writers, doctrines of men and private spirits to reveal his will to his people. Their determinations must be subject to scripture. \nA. External aspects- The external aspects of existential revelation include such things as Human existence, Human judgement both individual and corporate, human behavior. Individual and corporate human judgement is closely related to the fact that we are created in God's image. God uses human behavior as an external type of existential revelation. \nB. Internal Aspects- Illumination of Holy spirit: a divine gift of understanding that God gives to believers and even to unbelievers. God implants in every human being, a basic knowledge of His law. Inward leading of the Holy spirit more emotive and intuitive.	8	2020-05-25 22:45:19+05:30	2020-06-02 07:32:43+05:30	\N	455	832
1533	In 1 John 1:7, the passage explains that God is perfectly free from sin by defining sin as that which is foreign to God's nature. The metaphor of God as light is primarily a moral evaluation. Darkness is equated with sins and lies, and light is associated with truth and purity from sin. From the moment we confess our sins, till we walk in the light, we are freed from all our sins. If we walk in light, we won't sin, and as he himself is in the light, we have fellowship with one another and the blood of Jesus His son, purifies us from all sins.	8	2020-05-25 22:59:02+05:30	2020-06-02 07:32:43+05:30	\N	455	833
1563	Peter's apostolic confession as mentioned in the book of Mathew 16:15-17, wherein Jesus asked Peter  Who do do you say I am, accordingly Peter confessed that "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God" Jesus replied that - Blessed are you Simon, for this was not revealed to you by man, but by my Father in heaven. \nPeter's confession as mentioned in the gospels, acknowledges and recognizes that Jesus is indeed the Messiah. And then from that point on launches into the role of the suffering Messiah. Peter along with the disciples having witnessed all the miracles that Jesus did. Peter acknowledges that God has been at work through out the life of Jesus, he recognizes Jesus humanity and his divine nature as the Christ or Messiah. \n\nTransfiguration is another event wherein Jesus was revelead in His glory to His disciples, wherein Jesus appearance was transformed to a divine nature. \n\nJesus took his disciples Peter, James & John up to a mountain to pray. And while they were there, Jesus appearance changed. He was transfigured in front of His 3 disciples and also there came Moses and Elijah and they conversed with Jesus. Furthermore God's voice was heard from heaven, confirming that Jesus was the son of God. As Moses and Elijah appeared with Jesus, it showed that both the Mosaic Law and the Prophetic Office was fulfilled in Jesus - messianic identity.	9	2020-06-03 20:48:49+05:30	2020-06-15 18:26:36+05:30	\N	456	766
1564	After Jesus was baptized under John in the river Jordan, was filled by the Holy Spirit in the form of a dove. Therafter the Spirit guided Jesus into the desert where he fasted 40 days before being tempted by Satan. Jesus though he was weakened in His physical state due to the fasting period, yet He was spiritually and mentally strong as He continuously spent time in prayer with heavenly father. Despite his hunger, Jesus refused to use His divine power to satisfy His physical needs. Though Jesus had all the authority He did not demonstrate His power or authority to prove himself when He was challenged by the Devil. Since Jesus goals were well defined, He refused to compromise with the temptations of the devil and have a short cut to reach His goal by negotiating with the enemy through a sinful path. \n\nBiblical scholars point out that Jesus temptation by Satan paralleled by what Adam faced in the garden of Eden. Whereas Adam failed completely, Jesus overcame all the temptation. As the first Adam represented all of mankind, he failed and resulting in all human race becoming a slave to sin. But Jesus the second Adam overcame temptation, bringing salvation to His people.\n\nJesus entire ife was in itself was a test of temptation. Jesus had to overcome all sorts of temptation and be sinless. Also being offered as a sacrifice without sin, as the most perfect substitute for the sins of mankind. \n\nJesus temptation is His impeccability. \nThe word impeccability means inability to Sin. Jesus was the second persion of the trinity. And God can't sin, because he can't act in ways that are contrary to his nature. \n\nJesus overcoming the temptation with proved that He was the most perfect substitue for the sins of mankind, without sin or blemish.	9	2020-06-03 21:03:06+05:30	2020-06-15 18:26:36+05:30	\N	456	767
1565	Crucifixion was a form of death penalty used in the ancient Roman Empire. It's victims were tied to the cross, or nailed to it, as Jesus case and thereafter hung on the cross. Most of the victims died due to suffocation. Jesus crucifixion was unique because it was his responsibility to die on behalf of mankind.\n\nTwo major factors can be considered with regards to Crucifixion\n1. Imputation for our sins to Jesus\n2. Divine judgment against sin\n\nImputation:\nSimple meaning of imputation is assignment or reckoning. When Jesus was imputed to die on the cross, God was assigning the sins of the entire human race on Jesus, in other words Jesus became the offering or sacrifice in our place and God imputed to him all the sins and blame that was reserved for mankind. But in particular Jesus had not sinned, nor was he personaly guilty of any misconduct. But from a legal perspected, God considered Jesus as if He had personally committed every sin imputed to him.\n\nBefore our sins were imputed to him, Jesus was blameless and perfect. But once when our sins were imputed to him or recokened to his account.  God viewed him as a sinner guilty of all the sins that were assigned to him \n\nIn 2 Corinthians 5:21 says, \nGod made him who had no sin to be sin for us.\n\nAnd though we would think is it just or righteous for God to impute our sins to Christ ? but in God's perfect will, God had chosen Jesus to be a sacrifice for the sins of mankind. Only God can prepare such a salvation that is perfect and that fulfills all the requirements of the laws, and that would meet God's standards or requirements. \n\nJesus death was a divine judment against sin. Before God laid our guilt on him, Jesus was perfect and He couldn;t die. But once our sins were imputed to Him, His death was necessary.\n\nJesus bore the full wrath of God that was against our sins, fulfilled all the requirements, and there does not remain any more judgement for human beings, it was all assigned to Jesus.	9	2020-06-03 21:21:23+05:30	2020-06-15 18:26:36+05:30	\N	456	768
1551	Scripture communicates in many different ways. The Bible displays full range of language that we find in human communication. If we do not understand how each type of language communicates, we are likely to misunderstand the bible. \nExtraordinary- Those who believes that Bible speaks in extraordinary ways often oversimplify biblical language, developing a system of interpretation that can be applied fairly equally to all of Scripture. Middle ages mistake: because the bible is inspired by God, it communicates in extraordinary ways that exceed human comprehension. Many Christians have insisted that scripture's extraordinary nature makes its language easy to interpret. \nOrdinary- The bible communicates in ordinary human language, using all the normal conventions of human communication. Clarity of scripture implies in many passages, an overly literal reading would be terribly misleading. The bible uses linguistic conventions of its authors and their original audiences. We have to learn how the authors and original audiences ordinarily used language, and what each author's intent was when he wrote.	8	2020-05-30 22:53:30+05:30	2020-06-02 07:34:13+05:30	\N	457	838
1566	After Jesus crucifixion, on the first day of thew week, Jesus rose from the dead and He appeared to his disciples for a period of 40 days. \n\nDeath is the greatest tragedy and enemy of human beings The worst manisfestation of sin in the world. But Jesus Christ conquered death by resurrecting from the grave through the power of the Holy Spirit. Jesus proved to all of creation that he really is God's Son and the heir to His kingdom. Jesus first fruits from the death, therein He has promised future resurrection to all those who believe in Him and are His faithful followers.\n\nGod's plan to redeem humanity and the rest of creation depended on him fulfilling all the requirements and promises of the law and to establish his kingdom on earth under the kinship of a descendant of David. But God couldn't do this unless Jesus had resurrected from the dead and fulfilled all of God's covenant promises. This is the reasons that the New Testament calls the resurrection an affirmation of Jesus role as Christ.\n\nThe next aspect to be considered is Blessings of Salvation : Jesus having accomplished resurrection, offers a wide variety of blessings. It results in our justification, which is the forgiveness of our sins. It is the regeneration of our spirits and opens the door for our eternal inheritance. The promise of a resurrection for all future believers, paves the way for the blessings of salvaiton that we have access now and also for the future.\n\nThe resurrection of Jesus from the dead is the central point of the New Testament and all the blessings that flow along with it. Firstly the resurrection teaches us who Jesus is. It's a vindication of him as the Messiah and the Lord and the Son of God \n\nFurthermore the most important or the main theme is that Jesus Christ is alive today. He's risen from the dead and is seated at the right hand of the father. Moreover through Jesus, we have access to the risen power and believers are transformed by the Holy Spirit to a new life gaining access to heavenly blessings and power through Jesus Christ.	8	2020-06-03 21:36:17+05:30	2020-06-15 18:26:36+05:30	\N	456	769
1567	Through Jesus Christ, I have access to the spiritual blessings - as a child of God, we are ever transformed by his resurrected power through the Holy Spirit. As a renewed, transformed believer  we are ever at the disposal for the Kingdom of God. Carrying our cross daily and following the Lord, with all our priorities set as God wants us to be, always giving importance to God's will in the areas of our influence in terms of family, neighbors and in our communities etc.\n\nAffirming and confessing that Jesus is the Christ, it comes under our responsibility and beavior to be every impacting the world around us through our life and sharing the benefits and privileges that God has given us as believers. Whether it be within the family among each member of the house hold, or among our neighbors or friends we come into contact with on daily basis. The communities around us wherein we are a part of. It is very important that our doctrine and lifestyles match, otherwise it is very difficult to have any sort of influence in the enviroment wherein God has placed us. \n\nIn these times of uncertainity, it is very important as a representative of God's kingdom to give hope to the communities or families around us. As much as possible to share the burdens together in prayer, and to develop an opportunity for fellowship to support, encourage and motivate. Even if there is a need among any of the family or individual, to find a way to support and sort out the issue to the best of God given ability.\n\nTo bear the marks of the cross and resurrection of Jesus in our life - even in the darkest moment of their life, to give them a resurrecting hope and direct them to Jesus Christ. Also in general ordinary people are confused with all the different religions and doctrines around them, not to entertain further confusion, but to present the gospel in it's simplicity and continuously pray for them, and let God work on their lives.	9	2020-06-03 21:49:51+05:30	2020-06-15 18:26:36+05:30	