1685	Bible contains a full range of language. It contans statements, questions, promises, offers curses, blessings, judgements, commands, advise, request, exclamations descriptions, cries, expression, desires, love and much more everything that can be considered in terms of language. Biblical language can be very emotionally reserved or charged. Using symbolism and other figures of speech. Some are expressed in a direct manner, whereas some are unimaginative. Bible uses both sarcasm and sincere language. \n\nExtraordinary\nThere are those who believe that the Bible speaks in extraordinary ways, they offer solutions that ignore the different types of lanugage. Instead they oversimplify biblical language in order to develop interpretation that gives the same explanation equally to all of Scripture. \nMany theologians believed that because Scriptures is inspired by God, it communicates in extraordinary ways that exceed human comprehension. Their way of thinking as biblical text contained at lease some metamorphic meaning regardless of the human author's intentions.\n\nOrdinary \nBible is not obscure, in other words it does not contain any hidden meanings which needs to be discovered through mysterious means. Neither it  needs special inclidining and can be understood by special offices in the church or a special office. \n\nScripture is clear, it speaks ordinary language and communicates in normal ways\n\nThe Bible uses the linguistic conventions of its authors and their original audiences.\n\nWe have always interpret the Bible responsibly, we have to learn how they ordinarily used language and have to understand what each author's intent was when he wrote.	0	2020-07-08 21:50:46+05:30	2020-07-08 21:50:46+05:30	\N	485	838
1686	The primacy of the Ten Commandments are both historical and theological. Their historical primacy depends on the fact that these laws were the first written legal code that was received by the nation of Israel. \n\nThis law inroduced 430 years later, does not set aside the covenant previously established by God. \n\nBut as per apostle Paul this was the first time Israel as a nation possessed God's laws.  God gave the laws to the nation of Israel through Moses at Mount Sinai. Israel was the first nation on the earth to possess an extensive, supernaturlly revealed code of God's requirements.\n\nGod himself wrote the Ten Commandments on stone tablets, by doing so God demonstrated that these laws were special among his laws and they deserved special attention and notice. \n\nThe Theological primacy is indicated by the special occassion God gave them the Ten Commandments in the midst of thunder, lightning, smoke and clouds on Mount Sinai. God spoke to his servant Moses, and was allowed to be viewed by Joshua and the 70 elders of Israel \n\nMoses identified the Ten Commandments as God's very covenant with his people. \n\nThe Ten commandments were also placed within the Ark of the covenant. \n\nAlso Jesus also mentioned about the Ten Commandments during his discussion with a man who asked him how to inherit eternal life. Jesus indicated by obeying the Ten Commandments, a person can earn eternal life. Jesus also in this discourse gave the importance to the Ten Commandments.	0	2020-07-08 22:07:47+05:30	2020-07-08 22:07:47+05:30	\N	485	839
1687	The three traditional types of law are \n\nMORAL LAW\nMoral laws are typically thought to convey God's ethical standards and are usually identified with Ten Commandments\n\nCEREMONIAL LAW\nThe laws instructing bleivers in worshiping God. Associated with Old Testament sacrificial system, and worship at the Temple.\n\nCIVIL LAWS \nCivil laws provide for the governing of society.\n\nThe traditional threefold category of laws are not taught in the Bible. But they did play an important role in church history.\n\nIn the Westminster Confession of Faith statement states \nAll Ceremonial laws are not valid \n\nThis statement reflects the fact since the death, burial, resurrection and ascension of Christ, God's people are no longer to perform any of the specific ceremonial requirements in the Mosaic sacrificial system. \n\nWith regards to Civil Laws the specific requirements of civil laws do not apply as they have expired.\n\nBelievers of the present age no longer have to behave in many of the ways specified in the Old Testament with regards to ceremony and civil government as these laws have been superseded by the fuller revelation of the New Testament\n\nBut for Civil laws, there are four reasons that Christians should still look to the civil and ceremonial laws of the Old testament as well as to its moral laws, for ethical guidance only. \n\n1)God's Character does not change, - it is our ultimate standard for ethics, Civil and Ceremonial laws still reveal our moral standard\n\n2)Scripture teaches the continuing modern application of every law.\n\n3)Bible consistently teaches that the law is a unified whole and it all stands together\n\n4) All of Scripture is for our moral instruction. It teaches us about modern ethics. \n\nAll of Scripture is useful for teaching, rebuking, correcting and training in righteousness	0	2020-07-08 22:27:11+05:30	2020-07-08 22:27:11+05:30	\N	485	840
1688	Though the believers of the present age need no longer are subject to Old Testament Civil and Ceremonial laws. But it is critical to realize that in another sense the Old Testament civil and ceremonial laws still apply to modern Christians. The civil and ceremonial laws still guide us as God's standard today, just as the moral laws do.\n\nWe need to consider four reasons that Christians should still look to the civil and ceremonial laws\n\n1) God's character requires us to learn from the revelation of these laws. As God's character does not change and it is our ultimate standard for ethics.  Hence Old Testament's civil and ceremonial laws still reveal our moral standard\n\n2) Scripture itself teaches the continuing modern application even of the Old Testament laws. \nJesus himself instructed\n\n3) Bible consistently teaches that the law is a unified whole, that it all stands together without regard to civil, ceremonial or moal\n\n4) All of Scripture is for our moral instructions. It does not exclude any laws, hence it all has to teach us about modern ethics.	0	2020-07-08 22:42:31+05:30	2020-07-08 22:42:31+05:30	\N	485	841
1676	The term "diachronic "derives from two Greek word: first, the preposition "dia" which often means "through"or "throughout": and second, the Greek noun Chronos which means time.\nDiachrony is a concern with the passage of time. In the case of the Biblical theology, the term "diachronics" points to the way Old Testament Theology transformed, changed,or developed throughout time.\nDiachronic approaches to the Bible concentrate on how theological structures progressively unfolded through time. A diachronic approach however, is much more concerned with changes that occurred in theological structures\nfor example it asks questions like"How did God's revelation at the burning bush anticipate his work in Egypt?"These type of issues become of central importance in diachronic approaches to this part of Old Testament.	8	2020-07-05 23:45:33+05:30	2020-07-09 08:55:27+05:30	\N	486	665
1717	There are many cultural changes that set the stage for biblical theology .Biblical theology is in large measure a response to cultural shifts that can be traced back to the Enlightenment of the 17th century.\nSimply put biblical theology is a Christian response to a prominent intellectual movement in the modern period, often called modern historic ism.\nOne of the most well known Enlightenment figures who expressed this cultural shift was the German philosopher George Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel, known for his proposal that every aspect of reality is caught up in logical patterns of historical progress known as the dialectic. \nThe study of geology, biology, and similar shifts toward modern historicism took place in the nearly academic discipline including theology.\nEverything in life was thought to be understand most thoroughly when it was assessed in terms of the flow of history.	7	2020-07-24 23:44:49+05:30	2020-07-25 11:32:14+05:30	\N	496	657
1677	We use term development instead of simply speaking of diachronic changes to suggest two main ideas. First, changes in Old Testament theological structures always move towards fulfilling God's purposes for history. And Second , we speak of developments because theological changes only occur through God's providential involvement in history. \nDivine purposes:\nThe Scripture affirm that everything in history, including shifts in theology, always accomplish God's unfailing purposes for his creation.Isaiah 46:10.God's goals for history exist not only as broad , long -term goals but also as particular , short term goals.God also has an all -encompassing purpose for history.God began history with great destiny in mind and every event in history will, without fail , reach his glorious end.\nDivine Providence:\nThe road of the Old Testament is actually filled with steep inclines , fast descents, and sharp turns to the left and to the right. these abrupt shifts are caused by God's providence, his complex involvement with His creation.God wanted these theological shifts to take place.\nGod offered the first generation of Exodus , the possession of the promised land , but their infidelity led to their rejection . yet none of these human changes were outside of God's Sovereign control.	9	2020-07-06 00:02:14+05:30	2020-07-09 08:55:27+05:30	\N	486	666
1678	There were six times when God established major covenants with distinctive theological emphasis.\nTHE COVENANTS WITH\n1. ADAM\n2. NOAH\n3, ABRAHAM. \n4. MOSES\n5. DAVID\n6. NEW COVENANT\nTHE SIX OLD TESTAMENT COVENANTS FALL INTO THREE CATEGORIES:\nTHE UNIVERSAL COVENANT: The covenants with Adam and Noah is referred as "universal " because they were made between God and all  of humanity.\nTHE NATIONAL COVENANT:\nGod established national covenants with his special people Israel: the covenants with Abraham , Moses and David.\nGod narrowed his covenantal attention primarily to just one ethnic group establishing Israel as the nation that would lead the rest of humanity in service to God. \nTHE NEW COVENANT:\nThe new covenant was designed to govern God's people, when God fulfilled his promise to re-establish his people after exile and to spread his kingdom through them to the ends of the earth. and for this reason we may speak of the new covenant as the covenant of fulfillment.	9	2020-07-06 00:15:17+05:30	2020-07-09 08:55:27+05:30	\N	486	667
