1689	God's plan to redeem human beings from the corruption and consequences of our sin was made before he created the universe. This timing is mentioned in Ephesians 3:11 , which speaks of God's purpose.\n 2 Thessalonians 2;13 says that we were chosen for salvation from the beginning.\n2 Timothy 1:9,10  speaks of grace that was given to us " before the beginning of time".\nEphesians 1:3-4 Paul said that our redemption was determined before the creation of the world. \nIn Ephesians 1:11 we read "In Him we were also chosen , having been predestined according to the plan of him who works out everything in conformity with the purpose of his will. (Ephesians 1:11)\nGod's decree of salvation is referred to by the Greek word Proorizo which is translated as Predestination.\nIn Romans 8:29 and Ephesians 1:5 the word Proorizo is used. It means God's eternal decree of salvation was foreordained or decided before the world began.	7	2020-07-09 23:18:07+05:30	2020-07-16 01:55:13+05:30	\N	492	796
1690	God's eternal plan of redemption involves the work of all the three persons of the Trinity.\n The Father originated the agreement because of his desire to redeem fallen human beings from the curse of the sin. Scripture says that it was the Father's plan to save us ..(Ephesians 3:10-11).It was the Father's eternal purpose to accomplish our redemption through Christ. (Ephesians 1:4, 2 Thessalonians 2:13, 1 Peter 1:20)\nThe Son agreed to add a perfect human nature to his perfect divine nature, so that could die on behalf of sinners. In 2 Timothy 1:9 and in John 17:4,5 Paul said that we received grace in the Son before the beginning of time. \nGod's eternal counsel decreed roles for the Father and the Son, it also determined the Holy Spirit's part. The Holy  Spirit agreed to enable and empower the Son's work and the apply salvation to those\nwhom the Son redeemed .\n2 Thessalonians 2:13 we see that Paul indicated that the Father's choice was made from the beginning and that plan involved the Holy Spirit's agreement to perform the sanctifying work of applying salvation to us .	8	2020-07-09 23:34:39+05:30	2020-07-16 01:55:13+05:30	\N	492	797
1691	Covenant of grace means the Covenant relationship God established with humanity after our fall into sin that provides forgiveness and redemption on the basis of God's mercy in Christ.\nbefore He created the world , God:\nknew humanity would fall into sin ,So HE created a plan to save us involving all three persons of the Trinity. HE also planned to redeem sinners through Christ's death on our behalf.\nThe Covenant of grace fulfills part of God's eternal plan for history. \nThe Father fulfilled his eternal decree in the covenant of grace by sending the Son and the Spirit to do their work. He appointed the Son to the office of Messiah or Christ(Acts 2:36)\nhe grace His powerful Spirit without limit(John 3:34)\nHe prepared the Son's perfect human nature(Hebrews 10:5)\nThe Son fulfilled his eternal agreement to redeem humanity by Veiling his divine glory and adding a full human nature to his full divine nature, living a perfect ,life and dying and atoning death(Philippians 2:5)\nThe Holy spirit fulfilled his part by enabling and empowering the Son's incarnation and subsequent work (Matthew 1:20),by empowering Christ's death on the cross, regenerating our spirits and empowering us to resist sin, giving us spiritual gifts and securing our salvation.(Ephesians :13-14)	8	2020-07-09 23:50:54+05:30	2020-07-16 01:55:13+05:30	\N	492	798
1692	God the Son , joined the covenant of grace as its mediator:\nHe intercedes for God's covenant people.\nHe reconciles us to God by taking the blame and punishment for our sins. \nHe preserves the integrity of the covenant and the lives of his people by suffering the covenant curses and earning the covenant blessings.\nThe Son began to mediate the Covenant of grace when it was first made back in the Garden of Eden, when God first offered redemption to Adam and Eve. And  he has continued to mediate ever since.\nNo one was ever saved or can erase sin  on the basis of his or her own merit or worthiness , since now works of obedience can erase our sin . Jesus , his life perfect faith and obedience , his death on the cross , is resurrection from the dead and his ascension to heaven and as the mediator of the covenant of grace, he fulfilled the covenant of works on our behalf and guaranteed that we would receive his blessings.\nJesus succeeded where Adam failed, and his righteous works benefited us because the covenant of grace does provide the mechanism for forgiveness and grace.\nJesus's role as our fully divine and fully human covenant mediator is what enabled him to atone for our sin by dying in our place.	8	2020-07-10 00:06:28+05:30	2020-07-16 01:55:13+05:30	\N	492	799
1693	The covenant  of grace was administered by various covenant representatives:\nADAM: \nThe covenant of Grace was instituted with Adam immediately after he fell into sin . This administration provided humanity the immediate opportunity to reconcile our relationships with God.\nNOAH:\nThe covenant confirmed with Noah after the flood explicitly incorporated all the terms of Adam's administration . The Noahic administration expanded the covenant blessings by adding God's promises never again to destroy the earth withe flood waters.(Genesis 6:18)\nABRAHAM:\nGod promised Abraham to turn Abraham's descendants into a mighty nation and to bless all nations through them.\nIn this administration, the nation of Israel was charged with expanding God's kingdom throughout the earth (Romans 4:13)\nMOSES:\nwith Mosaic administration God confirmed his promises to Abraham , provided structure for the nation of Israel , gave Israel the first codified version of Law a,d redirected Israel to build his kingdom throughout the world.(Exodus 19-24)\nDAVID:(2 Samuel 7, Psalms 89)\nIN this administration God confirmed the mosaic administration and revealed the greatest covenant blessings would be fulfilled under the kingship of David  and his dynastic heirs.\nJESUS\nThis covenant is referred to as new covenant. in the new covenant , god established a permanent, unbreakable covenant in which his people would receive all his covenant blessings.(Jeremiah 31:31)	8	2020-07-10 00:24:16+05:30	2020-07-16 01:55:13+05:30	\N	492	800
1694	The two fold concept of revelation has characterized biblical theology of the New Testament.The New Testament reports many revelatory acts of God such as Christ's earthly ministry and the ministry of the Holy Spirit in the first century church.It also foretells the acts of God that will take place in this future, such as the glorious return of Christ.But on the other hand , the New Testament also reports word revelations associated with the act of God:God the Father spoke, Christ also spoke  and at times , angels and humans , by the Spirit of God , revealed God's word as well. \nNew Testament narratives report both the actions and words of Jesus, the apostles and other Christians in the New Testament:because God reveals himself through their actions and their word. \nBible theologians  have pointed out that the New Testament contains all three kinds of word revelations as well. The Gospels reports how God spoke through the words  of Jesus to reflect on Jesus earlier actions and Jesus' teachings explained his simultaneous actions and he predicted future actions.\nSame was done by the authors of epistles , Acts and Revelation. Throughout the New Testament God revealed himself through the intersections between his creations and his word.	8	2020-07-10 23:43:29+05:30	2020-07-16 01:54:42+05:30	\N	493	670
1695	One major contrast between old testament and new testament biblical theology is the ways Biblical theologians have handled dichronic developments.\nAS the New Testament moved forward, theological structures under went dichronic changes.\nOld Testament covers a very long period of 1600 years whereas the entire New Testament represents only around 100 years of history.\nOld Testament presents a great variety of circumstances in its  history which includes, Patriarchs, judges, early monarchy, latter kings and leaders , exile and restoration.By comparison , the circumstances of God's people were fairly consistent during the New Testament history like no extreme changes took place, consistently marginalized, no tremendous wealth or poverty, no large group travel and no  wide scale disobedience and obedience.\nThe New Testament deals with only single covenant whereas the old Testament deals with five covenants which were quite different from each other.	8	2020-07-10 23:58:53+05:30	2020-07-16 01:54:42+05:30	\N	493	671
1696	Toe understand how New Testament biblical theologians have understood the last days, we need to become familiar with Old Testament eschatology.Eschatology developed alongside the major covenant administrations of the Old Testament , beginning with Adam through Noah, Abraham, Moses and David.\n1. ADAM:\nGod revealed two crucial elements of eschatology, first being the fact that humanity was created in God' s image. Genesis 3:15 says that seed of women and seed of serpent are the two groups of people competing for control of the world.\n2. NOAH\nIn Genesis 8:22 the covenant secured the stability of nature as humanity pursued God's plan for the world.\n3. ABRAHAM\nIN Genesis 12:1-3. God singled out Abraham to be the one through whim he would carry out his promise given through Adam and Eve \n4. MOSES\nMoses' covenant focused on the law o God as the guide for Israel's special service in spreading\nhis kingdom. One key feature of Moses' eschatology is the way he described this time of Israel's repentance and restoration to the land in Deuteronomy 4:30\n5 DAVID:\nGod's covenant with David focused on specially on the establishment of David's family as Israel's permanent dynasty.	8	2020-07-11 00:16:00+05:30	2020-07-16 01:54:42+05:30	\N	493	672
1697	Jesus' three phase eschatology was so prominent in then hearts of early Christians that we find it either explicitly or implicitly in every page of the New Testament. Eschatology was especially important for the New Testament because Jesus' teaching about the last days represent one of the most decisive way in which Christians had broken with first century Judaism .Jewish religious leaders and the general Jewish population raged against Christianity precisely because of the Christian outlook on the Messianic last days. Christians believed that the Messiah had already come , but in the way that was unexpected . He had suffered and died at the hands of Jews and gentiles. He has been resurrected and had ascended into heaven where he ruled over all and he would return one day to judge all of humanity including unbelievers within Israel.  Such a  Messianic scenario was utterly contrary to what most Jews believed in that days. And for theses reasons, New Testament authors were deeply preoccupied with Jesus' three phase eschatology and this preoccupation reflected in everything hey wrote.	8	2020-07-11 00:29:16+05:30	2020-07-16 01:54:42+05:30	\N	493	673
1698	IN Matthew 13-25, we have studied that Jesus explained that the Kingdom of God would come in three phases.It would began  with his first coming in a small way , grow for an indefinite period of time and reach its fulfillment only when he returned in glory. So having understood that his kingdom is growing now and we all are laborers in His Kingdom, we work hard and sincerely.\n We try to teach, preach, proclaim the message of his Kingdom to all around us. We try to bring about the experience of kingdom in our families and churches. \nWe are to live for Christ in the light of what he has already accomplished in his first coming, we are to live for Christ in the power of his Spirit within us now and we are to live for Christ in the hope of his glorious return.\nWe are getting ready for the visible appearance of His Kingdom.\nwe try to bear fruits and characteristics which appeal to His kingdom.As we read in Romans 14:17\nFor the kingdom of God is not a matter of eating and drinking but of righteousness, peace and joy in the Holy Spirit. so applying the concept of Kingdom of god to all aspects of life helps us to be accountable and responsible throughout our life.	8	2020-07-11 00:45:45+05:30	2020-07-16 01:54:42+05:30	\N	493	674
1707	There are three basic typs of revelation, special revelation such as the Bible, General revealation which comes to us through the creation in general and existential revelation which comes to us through persons. \n\nBut based on these for us to make ethical decisions we have to consider three important aspects they are \nFACTS\nGOALS\nMEANS\n\nFacts :\nThrough revelation God speaks to us facts about himself and what he requires. Undrestand that God is the supreme authority over all creation, his aauthority is absolute and his character is perfect. God's revelation also teaches us about special set of facts that are particularly important for ethics.\n\nGoals :\nGoals in ethics are the targeted outcome that we have in mind. It is similar to the set of goals that we keep in mind, that we set to accomplish. Revelation provides us with goals that we ust adopt in Christian ethics.\n\nScripture teaches us about goals for doing good to our neighbors, raising children, striving for unity of the church. But the most important revelation is it presents God's glory as our primary and most important goal in life.\n\nMeans :\nAny ethical question in biblical manner requires - to find the appropriate means to achieve as revealed by God. Setting goals, assessing facts etc are things that influence our action. But our actions themselves are the means we have chosen to accomplish our goals. Godly means are equally important to achieve our goals and to understand the facts about God.	8	2020-07-20 20:48:07+05:30	2020-07-21 09:39:09+05:30	\N	494	842
1708	Facts are very important for us to know more about God in order to make ethical decisions. \nGod's character should be our ultimate norm or standard, and obligates us to live by the standard of his character. \nThrough revelation, God tells us facts about himself and facts about what he requires. Without the knowledge about the facts, we would still obey God, but without much knowledge.\n\nGod is the supreme authority over all of creation. His authority is absolute, His character perfect. Whenever we receive a revelation about his character, it is a means by which God exercises control over humans, similary to governments exercising control over it's citizens through their laws. \n\nGod not only communicates facts to us, but God's revelation also teaches us about special set of facts that are particulary important for ethics ; the proper goals of Christian behavior and decision making.	8	2020-07-20 20:55:17+05:30	2020-07-21 09:39:09+05:30	\N	494	843
1709	Goals in ethics are the expected outcomes of our endeavors that we have in our mind. Goals are similar to that which we set in our mind to accomplish. Goals can be small or large. They can be immediate goals or that which we plan in the distant future. But in every case our goals give direction to our action.\n\nScripture teaches us about goals of doing good to our neighbors, raising children in Christ, striving for the unity of church. But the most important goals is God's glory.\n\nGod glory in revelation reveals that we must be loyal to God and that we should praise him and glorify God in everything that we do. Scripture teaches us to set God's glory as our highest goal.\n\nExistential revelation helps us to discern good goals from evil goals, especially through our consciences. Holy Spirit is another source of existential revelation, moving within us so that we pursue good goals and shun evil ones.\n\nGod uses all three forms of revelation, special, general and existential in order to teach us the goals that God approves.	8	2020-07-20 21:02:05+05:30	2020-07-21 09:39:09+05:30	\N	494	844
1710	Christians inclined to follow a rigorous strategy towards revelation, resulting in tending to err on the side of overly restricting behavior rather than on the side of permitting it.\n\nRigor often views the situation in Bible as being similar to our own and applies the Scripture directly to our lives without consideration or scope to think about how it differs from the modern world, in terms of facts and goals. \n\nChristians endorsing a rigorous strategy, mistakenly believe that when the Bible's facts, goals and means appear to be vague, the proper response is to apply Scripture in restrictive ways. By doing so we often end up forbidding some things that God permits and other things that God actually commands.\n\nDangers :\nIt destroys Christian Freedom by prohibiting behavior that are wrong under certain conditions. But good under other conditions. As matters of conscience are not clear, a strategy of rigor would tend to prohibit certain actions resulting in missing Godly blessings.\n\nA strategy of rigor also inspires despair in believers by turning God's world into a heavy burden. \n\nAvoid falling into this strategy :\nRealize that modern situations are sufficiently different from biblical situations, we cannot just apply or copy the applications to the present modern era, without giving it further considerations.\n\nWe can avoid a strategy of rigor by remembering that Scripture is sufficiently clear to communicate God's will with regard to ethics.	9	2020-07-20 21:17:11+05:30	2020-07-21 09:39:09+05:30	\N	494	845
1711	Jesus spoke about the Kingdom of God in the Sermon on the Mount found in the Gospel of Mathew Chapter 5\n\nThe Sermon on the mount is well known, Jesus began by encouraging people to live ethically in terms of the Kingdom of God.\n\nLets consider the three different sections wherein Jesus emphasized the importance of the Kingdom of God\n\n1) Mathew 5:3 - the blessing was "theirs is the kingdom of heaven" Though Mathew here used the phrase the "Kingdom of Heaven" biblical scholars have agreed that this term is is the same as Kingdom of God. \n\n2) Mathew 5:5 - the blessing was "they will inherit the earth" this was also a kingdom blessing, and it referred to the new earth that God will create when his kingdom will come in it's fullness.\n\n3) Mathew 5:9 - the blessing was "they shall be called the sons of God" Even this statement of blessedness refers to the kingship and kingdom of God. In the times of old biblical times, human kings were called "father" by their subjects. Same is true about the scriptures. God is our heavenly father. \n\nEach of the blessings Jesus mentioned was connected to the concept of the kingdom of God.	8	2020-07-20 21:26:24+05:30	2020-07-21 09:39:09+05:30	\N	494	846
1712	The Three main components of the Kingdom of God are \n\nKING (role of the King) \nPEOPLE (Citizens of the Kingdom) \nCOVENANTS (that govern the relationship between king and his people)\n\nKing :\nThe concept of King is unfamiliar to the modern people but in oden times Kings were expected to fulfill their responsibilities to the citizens of the country. They were to protect and provide for them and to treat them with kindness. In return King will charge them taxes and raise armies and regulate the many aspects of life.\nGood Kings ruled wisely to benefit the people. \n\nIn the Bible God is presented as the Supreme King or emperor over all creation.\nAll the earthly kings are his vassals or servan kings. \nThough David and thereafter his son Solomon sat on the throne of the Lord in Jerusalem. The throne still belongd to God so that the human kings were just representatives or vassals of God.\n\nPeople :\nThe people here refers to those whom God has called to himself in contrast to the people of the world. \n\nOld testament refers to Abraham and his descendants. In the New Testament, it refers to the Church, since christians of all races have been adopted into the family of Abraham in Christ.\n\nUnder the kingship of Jesus the focus of God's kingdom became ecclesiastical it was centered on the church. God's kingdom includes people from every race and continues to spread to the ends of the earth.\n\nCovenants :\nGod administered his kingdom through covenants. God's expressed good will towards his people through covenants, wherein clearly the parameters were mentioned regarding the consequences for obedience & disobedience and it was binding covenant for both parties.\n\nThere are 6 major covenants between God and his people, beginning with Adam to that of Noah, Abraham, Moses, David and finlly the Final Covenant in Christ Jesus.	9	2020-07-20 21:39:36+05:30	2020-07-21 09:39:09+05:30	\N	494	847
1713	Traditionally Bible has summarized the kingdom of God through three historical phases :\n\nInitial Phase (Creation)\nRebellion (Humanity's fall into sin)\nFinal Peace (Redemption)\n\n1) Initial Phase : \nUnder creation story, humanity lived in perfect harmony with God. Human life was full of joy and gladness, of thanksgiving and singing. in the garden there was great peace.\nThere was no enemy to threaten them, no war, no violence, no sickness, no drought or flood or fire to destroy them etc.\nGod cared tenderly for Adam and Eve, and even met them and walked with them in the cool of the garden.\n\n2) Rebellion \nAs we study Genesis 3, we are aware of humanity's initial rebellion against God through disobedience & the fall of man. Serpent tempted Eve to eat the forbidden tree and shared the fruit with Adam. By sinnning humanity violated one of the covenant obligation.\n\nGod threw Adam & Eve out of the Garden and forced them to live in a world wherein it was the cursed ground followed by pain in childbirth and along with sickness and famine with wild animals and threats of war. They stil lhad to fulfill the covenant obligations but they were experiencing the negative considequences due to sin. \n\nHumanity has continued to rebel against God throughout history. God has punished humans with covenant curses. \n\n3) Final Peace\nGod began to restore peace to his kindom immediately after humanity's fall into sin. God did not wipe out humanity completely when they sinned, but he allowed them to live. God introduced the first steps, promise of salvation through Genesis 3:15. Throughout history God was working toward the goal of full redemption and success of his kingdom.\n\nAt the appearance of Christ, the restoration of final peace took a huge leap and reached the final stages of fulfillment. Jesus came to earth as a vassal king in order to establish a faithful kingd om earth. His work still continues and he will come again in glory and restore the kingdom, bring the whole world under final peace.	9	2020-07-20 21:58:57+05:30	2020-07-21 09:39:09+05:30	\N	494	848
1714	God's laws are a delight and it adds joy to our life. As humans when we obey the laws laid down by God, in return we receive blessings that God has itended for obedient citizens of his kingdom.\n\nLaw's reveals God to us, teaching us how to please him and how to obtain his blessings. Laws were given for our good and for our prosperity and for our joy.\n\nDavid summarized in Psalms 19\n\nThe law of the Lord is perfect, reviving the sould. The precepts of the Lord are right giving joy to the heart\n\nGod has given us rules to follow in order to produce joy in our lives. And those rules are his law. So when we obey God's law, we enjoy him and we glory him at the same time. We enjoy him because he blesses our obedience and because it pleases us to bring pleasure to the God we love. And our godly joy brings glory to God by fulfilling his purpose. In all of these ways, the role of the Law shows us that enjoying God is an important part of God's goal for humanity.\n\nIn our present modern age, our enjoyment of God is often hindered by our suffering. But we also need to understand that God's plan for us is through suffering, it further enhances our faith and improves our understanding of God's grace and knowledge about him. \n\nA close analysis of the Scripture teaches us that God uses suffering as a tool that proves our faith and brings us to spiritual maturit and that ultimately results in our joy.\n\nManytimes we do not understand the full purpose and plan in our sufferings. But if you further study the scriptures and draw closer to God, we will experience more the grace of God in our sufferings and challenges of life. Also our experiences of joy will bring great glory to our divine King. \n\nOur view of the laws and the scriptures need to be focussed on drawing nearer to our creator.  To increase our trust factor in God and to understand that we are the clay in the hands of our master builder.	9	2020-07-20 22:16:58+05:30	2020-07-21 09:39:09+05:30	\N	494	849
1715	Biblical theology is the theological reflection drawn from the historical analysis of acts of God reported in Scripture.Historical analysis is especially concerned with "acts of God".\nThe holy Spirit has led the church to pursue the exegesis of Scripture in three main ways : literary analysis, historical analysis and thematic analysis. Historical analysis looks at Scripture as a window to history, exploring historical events lying behind the Bible.\nBiblical theology approaches the Scriptures primarily with historical analysis. It looks at the bible as a window that gives access to the history. When the focus shifts from theological themes to the historical events described in the Bible, a very different set of priorities and concerns emerge .	8	2020-07-24 23:13:02+05:30	2020-07-25 11:32:14+05:30	\N	496	655
1716	Biblical theological reflection is based on the historical analysis of the acts of God in Scripture. historical analysis take two different forms: factual historical analysis and theological historical analysis. These two tendencies go hand in hand , but their concerns are quite different.\nFactual approach are concerned with  the events are reported in scripture fit within the large environment ancient Near East. A factual approach to historical analysis is concerned with questions like the date of the exodus under Moses etc.\nThe goal of factual historical analysis is straight forward.It is to establish a reliable account of the facts of history along with the data we gather from extra biblical resources.\nChristians agree with Aquinas that theology has two main concerns .A theological matter is anything that refers directly to God.  And on the other hand theological matter is anything that describes other subjects in relation to God. Biblical theology opens the way for enhancing and enlarging our understanding of all of these theological subjects like the human race, sin, salvation etc	9	2020-07-24 23:29:51+05:30	2020-07-25 11:32:14+05:30	\N	496	656
