1730	The word "synchronic" is derived from two Greek words: the preposition "sun" which means "with" or "together" and the noun chronos means "time". In relation to the historical events , it describes occurrences that took place "together in time" or "at the same time".\nThe term synchronic is used to indicate how biblical theologians often explore sets of events in Old Testament history that happened at the same time.\nIllustration:\nIn a movie, the directors tell their stories in a flow from beginning to the end. They depict how one event leads to another and so on. The movies is on , whole unit is broken down into smaller parts called scenes. every scene tells a portion of the larger story. Each story represents a synchronic moment in the movie, period of time in the film. \nSimilarly . The sychronic study of the Old Testament takes the same approach. The Biblical theologians concentrate their attention on particular periods of the time in the Old Testament  as if they were scenes in a movie rather on the flow of its entire story.Sometimes biblical theologians focus on relatively brief historical moments, but other times they concern themselves with relatively long periods	8	2020-07-28 23:17:30+05:30	2020-08-05 04:05:09+05:30	\N	503	660
1731	Synthesis means combining different components of something into a whole. Synchronic synthesis describes the way different components of a particular periods of history fit together in a coherent, logical structure.\nMany critical scholars distinguished biblical theology from systematic theology by pointing out the role of logic in each.Critical theologians believed that logic was a primary feature of the"greek mindset", but was relatively foreign to the "hebrew" mindset. \nAffirmation\nStudies have largely discredited the contrasts between Greek and Hebrew mindsets. Old Testament theology displays substantial concern fro logic and rational thought. Old Testament theology does not employ the standards of formal western philosophical traditions:\nFew technical terms \nvariety of genres\ndifferent emphases\n no all en-compassing system\nIn Romans 5:12-14 Paul treated the time from Adam's fall to the giving of the law as one synchronic unit. Paul's main concern was to prove how the far-reaching effects of Adam's sin far reaching effects of Christ's obedience	8	2020-07-28 23:31:56+05:30	2020-08-05 04:05:09+05:30	\N	503	661
1732	Poets of the old Testament were interested in two different worlds that tell us about history:\n1. THAT WORLD: THE WORLD THEY WROTE ABOUT\nWhen writing about that world, they provided objective facts about God's acts and word.\nin first place, Poetry often opened windows to the past as we see in Exodus 15  :1-21. where we see the song that Miriam and Moses sang.\nIn second place,Old testament often provided windows to contemporary historical information about  their own time as in Psalms 1.\nIn third place Old Testament writers turned their readers attention towards the future as in Isaiah 40:1-11\n2. THEIR WORLD:WORLD OF THEIR READERS\nThe old testament writers design their texts to influence their original readers in particular ways :Exodus 15, Psalms 1 and Isaiah 40\nNARRATIVES:\nTrue stories about historical peoples, place and events that reveal many details about history through:\n words ,speeches , characters , place and connections,\nTWO WORLDS:\nTHAT WORLD: THE SUBJECT OF THEIR TEXT\nNarratives largely focus on the past, and only rarely mention the present or the future.\nMoses wrote about creation etc\n\nTHEIR WORLD: the world in which the readers lived\nThey wanted their readers to think, act and feel in certain ways within their worlds in light of past events.	8	2020-07-28 23:50:16+05:30	2020-08-05 04:05:09+05:30	\N	503	662
1733	It is important while discerning historical information not to expect them to be like modern journalistic historical writing. Historians must seek to be as exacting as their counter parts in science s like chemistry and biology. True historical records will include every significant fact about a situation to give a balance account. They will avoid all subjective evaluations that will prejudice readers.\n\nOld testament narratives wrote in ways that were determined largely by their didactic purposed and not by our modern sensibilities. These narratives were only as comprehensive as suited the didactic purposes of their writers. they dint not include every significant fact. For example in Solomon's life 2 Chronicles 1-9 , they emitted every negative dimension of Solomon's reign.\nThey were only as precise as their didactic purposes required. They did not meet the modern criterion of good history writing	8	2020-07-29 00:03:28+05:30	2020-08-05 04:05:09+05:30	\N	503	663
1734	Old Testament writers were only as precise as their didactic purposes required . There is vital difference between precision and truth. In every aspect of life, precision is always a matter of degree and so long as we respond with as much precision as is needed , no one accuses us of misrepresenting facts. This was true with the Old Testament authors .they were only as precise as they needed to be reach their didactic goals.\n1 Kings 9:5 and 2 Chronicles 7:18, we can read God spoke to David and God covenanted with David respectively. so we can see here that Old Testament narratives were not designed to repeat words and thoughts if god or anyone else with absolute precision. What they wrote was historically true. They did not misrepresent what God said.\nNumbers of people, measurements and geographical references often do not modern significant standards. But this lack of modern precision does not mean the accounts are untrue.\nIn summary we can say with confidence that old testament narratives were not designed to meet modern standards of history writing, but they represent fully reliable historical information that will enables us to construct synchronic synthesis Testament theology .	8	2020-07-29 00:20:53+05:30	2020-08-05 04:05:09+05:30	\N	503	664
