1759	Goodness of humanity will focus on three details of humanity's creation :\n\n1) Humanity was created in the image of God\n2) God's blessing on humanity\n3) Cultural mandate that God assigned to the human race\n\nHumanity :\nIn the image an likeness of God, we need to consider the attributes like reason, spirituality, moral nature, immortality and our original righteousness. Humans share these attributes in common with God.\nGod appointed huan beings to be his living images, so whenever we see a human being it reminds us of God.\nWhen we wrongfully disrespect human beings, we dishonor the Lord whose image we are\n\nBlessing : \nAfter the creation of mankind, God blessed humanity \nGood is not only in terms of God's character, but also in terms of what he blesses and approves. \nWhen God blessed humanity during creation time, he indicated that humanity was morally good\nGod's blessings were just not an affirmation of their behavior but of their being blessed.\n\nCultural Mandate :\nGod's cultural mandate to humanity were - be fruitful and increase in number, fill the earth and subdue it. Have dominion over the fish of the sea, birds of the air and over every livng creature that moves on the ground\nHumans were God's representatives as vassal kings to fill, subdue and rule over.\nGod indicated that humanity was physically and morally capable of accomplishing the task of God in this world.\nGod created us morally pure in our being, possessing the attribute of goodness and being free from the corruption of sin.\nGoodness was rooted in our very being.	0	2020-08-05 22:01:23+05:30	2020-08-05 22:01:23+05:30	\N	506	854
1745	The english word "diachronic "derives from the two Greek Words :first the preposition dia which often means "through" or "throughout" and the second , the Greek Nouns Chronos which means "time". Diachrony  is a concern with the passage of time. \nIn the case of biblical theology, the term "diachronic "points to the ways the Old Testament  theology transformed , changed or developed through the time.\nDiachronic approches to the Bible concentrate on how theological structures progressively unfoldes through the time. It says about God's revelations took place or developed as history moved forward.\nLooking at the Old Testament diachronically is like concentrating on the storyline of a movie as it moves from scene to scene.\nIt's to focus on the ways a film progressively unfolds its drama from beginning to the end.	7	2020-08-01 23:13:36+05:30	2020-08-05 04:03:16+05:30	\N	508	665
1760	Fall has actually damaged our ability to obtain moral knowledge, Unbelievers can read the bible and understand its commands but cannot gain access to the true knowledge and revelation that God has to offer. \n\nFall has indered mankind from the following 3 aspects, namely\n\n1)Access to Revelation :\nFall has hindered humanity's access to revelation by limiting the Holy Spirit's work of illumination and inward leading. Illumination and inward leading is a divine gift \nIn some sense God provides a easure of both illumination and inward leading to all fallen human beings.\nHoly Spirit does not provide the same measure of illumination and inward leading to unbelievers that he provides to believers\n\n2) Understanding of Revelation \nHuman beings have the cognitive ability to understand the basic teachings of God's revelation. But moral understanding depends on more than mere cognition, it involves the whole person\nEthical judgements  are based on many non cognitive factos that influence our ethical evaluations, such as our emotions, consciences, intuitions, desires, fears, weakness, failures etc\nFallen human beings have eys and ears to receive God's revelation but their hearts are hardened, and they are unable to grasp the full understanding or the revelation.\nDue to the fall human hearts were hardened, that hinders them from fully understanding God's revelation\n\n3) Obedience to Revelation\nKnowledge and obedience are essentially the same things, fall hinders our knowledge of God and we are unable to obey\nFirst we receive and understand God's revelation and then we obediently apply it to our lives, whereas due to the fall they lack both understanding and obedience. \nIn the Scripture, obedience is a prerequisite to knowlege	0	2020-08-05 22:20:09+05:30	2020-08-05 22:20:09+05:30	\N	506	855
1761	The Scripture makes it clear that God has authority, the right to rule over all of creation and it derives from the fact that \nGod is the creator, sustainer of all creation\nAll of creation was brought to life by God and depends on him for continued existence\nGod's authority has three basic attributes\n\nABSOLUTE :\nGod has absolute,, complete, total freedom over his creation.\nScripture illustrates God's absolute authority by comparing it with the authority as potter has over the clay\nGod is the creator and has all the freedom and right to do whatever he wants with what he creates\nThe absolute power applies to all of creation\nGod has the freedom and the right to act upon, as he sees fit to require of it. \nWhen God reveals his ethical judgements they are true and never subject to scrutiny\n\nEXCLUSIVE\nGod has exclusive authority over everything he has created his absolute or complete authority belogs only to the creator, and God alone holds this authority. All other authorities are delegated by God, and is subject to God's authority. All smaller or lesser authorities can be overruled by te greater authority of God.\n\nEXHAUSTIVE \nGod has exhaustive authority over the universe. This authority extends over everything he has created in detail.\nEven though most humans rebel against God and refuse to submit to his commands, his moral judgements aply to them as well. All human beings irrespective of our culture or religion, we are all accountable to God\n\nGod's sovereign control is completely compatible with human responsibility. \nGod's attributes are immutable, they do not change, they existed before the universe was formed and is still active & present and also will continue for the future. As God can never change his character or attributes, they are constant. The attributes do not conflict as they have been well defined in the entire personality and character of God. \n\nAs humans we are still learning about God's several attributes and characters by studying his word. God's word is also immutable and stands perfect for us to learn more about God.	0	2020-08-05 22:37:28+05:30	2020-08-05 22:37:28+05:30	\N	506	856
1740	The creed was a collective work that gradually developed over several centuries. Rules of Faith: summaries of beliefs and practices: To preserve the teachings of the church and train its people (especially leaders) Often these rules varied from from congregation to congregation. Generally speaking, they included affirmations of important doctrines as well as teachings and traditions. \nCreeds were the summaries of doctrinal portions of church's rules of faith. These were recited in liturgical settings. One ancient creed that rose to prominence was from the church in Rome, often called the Roman Creed, which was similar to the Apostolic Creed. the Apostolic creed eventually came into widespread use, especially in Western churches. In the early centuries, its precise wording varied from church to church. But in the eighth century the wording was generally standardized into the form that we recognize and use today.	6	2020-08-01 21:07:57+05:30	2020-08-05 04:03:46+05:30	\N	507	866
1741	The bible is our only inspired infallible rule of faith and practice. Creeds, are fallible teaching tools that summarize our understanding of Scripture. And the apostles' creed was created to help Christians learn and remain true to the teachings of the bible. The purpose of the apostles creed is as follows;\n1. Scripture- Scripture in all its parts was word of Christ, and that it was the source of all true doctrine. Basis of doctrine, Sola scriptura- this is the perspective that scripture is the only infallible rule of faith, and final arbiter in any theological controversy.\n2. Traditional teaching- The early church depended on the traditional teachings of the church to summarize and protect the teachings of scripture. Christian leaders created summaries so that all Christians would know and affirm the basic content of faith. \n3. Apostolic Creed- the apostles' creed was largely used to confirm that new Christians believed the fundamental teachings of the Bible.  The goal was to ensure that the churches did not stray from the original meaning of scripture.	8	2020-08-01 21:16:53+05:30	2020-08-05 04:03:46+05:30	\N	507	867
1742	The creed is based on the belief that there is only one God and that this God exists in three persons. The trinity is one of the most important beliefs of Christianity. One essence- only one God. By the term person, we mean a distinct, self aware personality. And by the term essence, we are referring to God's fundamental nature or the substance of which he consists. \nThree persons: The Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit- distinct from one another. \nFather- Almighty, Maker of heaven and earth. The creed'd description of Father doesn't distinguish Christianity from other religions that express belief in a sovereign creator.\nSon- The creed mentions Jesus' incarnation, his coming to earth as human baby, and living a genuinely human life. And it speaks of his suffering, death, burial, resurrection and ascension into heaven.\nHoly Spirit- The creed associates the Holy Spirit with the church, and with our experience of salvation both now and in the future. The creed says enough about God and his persons to distinguish those who affirm the historic Christian faith from those who do not.	7	2020-08-01 21:26:21+05:30	2020-08-05 04:03:46+05:30	\N	507	868
1743	1. Participation- The creed emphasizes the importance of participating in the church. The bible teaches Christians to form an actual physical community and it insists that this community is extremely important to every believer. It is not to be limited to spiritual fellowship, although it is true that Christians have spiritual communion with each other through Christ and his Spirit. Rather, our community is to be like a family or neighborhood. It is to consist of people who interact with each other face to face. \n2. Preservation- The creed affirms the fact that Christ appointed his church in part to protect and proclaim the gospel and other truths. According to Jude, part of church's job is to contend for faith, to protect the truths and beliefs that have been entrusted to it against those who promote false teachings and practices. We have to keep trying to preserve doctrine in the church.	7	2020-08-01 21:35:25+05:30	2020-08-05 04:03:46+05:30	\N	507	869
1746	We use the tem "development "instead of simply speaking of diachronic changes to suggest two main ideas. First the changes in Old Testament theological structures always move toward fulfilling God's purposes for history and second , we speak of development because theological changes only occur through God's providential involvement in history.\n1.Divine purposes-\nIsaiah 46:10 reflects a common outlook throughout God's unfailing purposes for his creation. When we go through the Scriptures , they affirm that everything in history including shifts in theology, always accomplish God's unfailing purposes for his creation.\nGod;s goals for history exist not inky as broad, long term goals , but also short term goals. God has an all-encompassing kingdom purpose for history.From the beginning, his design has been to glorify himself by extending his heavenly kingdom to the entire earth through the work of his image and this unites all of God's purposes\n2. Divine providence:\nWhen we look at Old testament , we see that the road is actually filled with steep inclines, fast descents and sharp turns to the left an the right.These abrupt shifts are caused by God's providence, his complex involvement with his creation. The only explanation that we can hold with certainity is that God simply wanted these theological shifts to take place.	8	2020-08-01 23:32:43+05:30	2020-08-05 04:03:16+05:30	\N	508	666
1747	The six Old Testaments covenants fall into three main categories. First . the universal covenants with Adam and Noah.\nSecond, the national covenants with Abraham, Moses and David.\nand third , the New Covenant predicted by Old Testaments.\n1. UNIVERSAL COVENANT\nWe speak of the covenants with Adam and Noah as universal  because they were made between God and all of humanity.  the relationship between Adam and God emphasized that all human beings have a basic responsibility of loyal service to their creator.\n2. NATIONAL COVENANT:\nGod narrowed his covenantal attention primarily just one ethnic group, establishing Israel as the nation that would lead the rest of humanity in service to God. God's special benevolence toward Abraham and his descendants , God made a second covenant with Israel through Moses when he brought them to  Mount Sinai\n3.NEW COVENANT\nThe prophets of Israel faced the time when Israel would go into exile, they spoke of a covenant , when God would make a final covenant with his people. The New Covenant was designed to govern God's people when God fulfilled his promise to re-establish his people after exile and to spread his kingdom. and the new testament explains the work of Christ , the Holy Spirit and ministry through the apostles	8	2020-08-01 23:51:27+05:30	2020-08-05 04:03:16+05:30	\N	508	667
1748	Biblical Typology is the study of diachronic developments between the theological structures closely associated with significant persons , institutions and events in scripture.\nin simple language , we might say that typology is the study of the types. The word "type" derives from the Greek word tupos.\nBiblical theology applies the term typology to any diachronic development of topics in Old Testament theology. On occasions , biblical theologians will speak of the typology of the doctrine of God, or the typology of worship, and simply mean that these are then ways developed in the Bible.\nFor example Jesus spoke of   a typological connection between Moses' bronze serpent and himself.\n1. Typology functions as a figure of speech For example Romans 5:14 the apostle Paul declared that Adam was a pattern or type\n2. A second feature of biblical theology is that compared elements are quite varied. In Peter 3:21 Peter compared the event of Noah's flood with baptism\n3. In the third place , typologies in the Bible always compare the theological structures that are closely associated with their elements.\n4.A fourth characteristic of typologies in Scripture is that they always reflect diachronic developments	8	2020-08-02 00:09:08+05:30	2020-08-05 04:03:16+05:30	\N	508	668
