1800	Motive can be the purpose for which we take an action. Motives are essentially the same as goals. \nMotive serve as causes, and our actions are the affects they produce. The list of motives include, our character traits, desires, feelings, commitments etc.\n\nBible makes it clear that if we are truly ethical, we must be rooted in God honoring motives. \n\nDavid taught Solomon that obedience to God must flow from the depths of our inner person, and it involes wholehearted devotion and willing mind, as God hates hypocrisy.\n\nGod is not interested only in the outward obedience. He requires every heart and motive behind the thoughts to be truly committed to him, genuine obedience from our deepest thougts and desires. \n\nGod requires wholehearted obedience, that flows from the heart with good motives.\n\nBible condemns lip service, or evil motives calling it hypocrisy.\n\nThe virtue of Christian love should motivate us to act in loving ways. Similarly people who are joyful in the Spirit should be motivated by their joy. Peaceful peple motivated by the peace within them.\n\nThe greatest example we can look at would be the life of Jesus Christ, which provides us a lasting impact with genuine values both inward and outward and motivates us to do our best in glorfying God both externally and internally.	0	2020-08-19 21:20:09+05:30	2020-08-19 21:20:09+05:30	\N	518	860
1802	Yes I do remember when I came first to faith. It was a complete transformation and change in one's life. The submission and surrender as well as the motivation to serve the Lord and this priority, all other factors revolved around this priority pleasing God and serving Him in faith \n\nFaith is the means of initial salvation, it is a tool that God uses to apply salvation to sinful individuals. There is nothing in our faith that deserves or earns salvation. On the contrary Christ freely gives us salvation through faith.\nIt is God's gracious act by which he forgives our sins. And it changes our status forever.\nGod uses saving faith to justify us, and this indeed motivates us to repent of our sin and trust in Christ for our salvation.\nThe good works that follows are the first evidences of our salvation since they can only be motivated by a genuine saving faith.\n\nOngoing commitment, consists of studying the word of God and continuing trust in Christ to save us fro our sin. Active faith plays a very important role in motivating our life to good works. \nSaving faith involves our hearts. creating within us an inward desire to think, speak and act in ways that pleases God\nSpending more time in the Word, meditating on God's promises with continual repentance on daily basis with genuine faith and commitment - helps us to grow spiritually in faith in our salvation journey, motivating us to grow form the point from where we began. This growth is visible both internally and externally.\n\nTrue faith characterizes us to good works, through out our lives and with the guidance of the Holy Spirit - the equation changes completely, because it is God who works in our life to do his good will.	0	2020-08-19 21:35:37+05:30	2020-08-19 21:35:37+05:30	\N	518	861
1795	The visible church is said to be as one worldwide fellowship of all people in covenant with God under headship of Christ. There is no single denomination whose government extends over all Christian congregation. Rather the unity of the visible church is based on the fact that each church is in covenant with same God, and under the covenant headship of the same Christ. The three traditional marks of the church are as follows,\nPreaching of the word. God has given the Bible to the whole visible church and he gave the Holy Spirit to help us understand it. Scripture exhorts the whole church to read, understand, and to teach God's word as we see in Matthew 28.20, 1 Timothy 4.17 etc.\nSecond mark is the right administration of the sacraments of baptism and the Lord's Supper. These sacraments belong to the visible church not just to one denomination.\nthird mark is the formal church discipline such as excommunication. No christian enjoys formal church discipline. And this had led churches to avoid the use of formal discipline.	9	2020-08-16 21:48:42+05:30	2020-08-22 17:28:31+05:30	\N	520	890
2402	Ethics in the Bible refers to the system or theory produced by the study, interpretation, and evaluation of biblical morals, that are found in the bible. It comprises a narrow part of the larger fields of Christian ethics., which are themselves parts of the larger field of philosophical ethics. Bible provides patterns of moral reasoning that focus on conduct and character in what is sometimes referred to as virtue ethics. Bible teaches us about which acts, attitudes, and personal character traits receive God's approval and which ones do not. Christian ethics teaches us how to live.  Christian ethics asks what the whole Bible teaches us about which acts, attitudes, and personal character traits receive God's approval and which ones does not.	7	2021-02-06 08:19:41+05:30	2021-03-10 21:27:33+05:30	\N	641	835
1805	Imagination refers to our ability to form mental images of things beyond our experience. Imaginative abilities are vital to learning and thinking about God the world and ourselves\n\nTo study further we need to consider three factors that affect imagination\n\nCREATIVITY\nImagination is a form of creativity, it involves in everything we make or create. We use imagination on our day to day lives and in many creative ways.\nImagination is involved in everything we make or create. Scientists use thier imaginations to come up with theories and ways of testing them. \n\nFor instance in the old testament we read about Nathan the prophet confronting King David using an imaginary situation, and he asks David to draw a conclusion on that situation.\n\nTIME\nTime is another important factor, since we have to consider that Jesus Christ is no longer on earth teaching his disciples, we have to consider the period in which all these events happened in history. We have to draw out our imaginations accordingly. Bible requires us to pursue goals for the future in glorification of God through the triumph of his kingdom. But since these events are yet to happen, we have to figure out the best means to use to reach this goal and this cannot happen unless we imagine the future. \n\nDISTANCE\nMost of the historical places mentioned in the Bible are places which we have never seen for ourselves, but we need to imagine those places and study the scriptures with regards to the context and place. When people and things are so far rom beyond our range of senses, we cannot have it as part of experience, but instead we need to use our imaginations to think about the place they lived in and the situation around them. We have to strongly rely on the Holy Spirit to evaluation our imagination according to God's word and then to harmonize it with our abilities to understand and study the Scripture further.	0	2020-08-19 21:54:43+05:30	2020-08-19 21:54:43+05:30	\N	518	862
1806	Assessing knowledge is an important step in the process of making ethical decisions, it can be divided into three categories\nReason\nConscience\nEmotions \n\nREASON :\nReason is defined as the capcity to make logical inferences. \nAs a Christian, right reasoning is the ability to think in orderly ways and to make judgements in accordance with biblical pattern\nReasoning enables us to make sense of the situation, both by helping us understand the facts and to compare these facts with the word of God. All the knowledge that we acquire through sensory experience requires a measure of reasoning. \nJesus taught us that since God feeds the birds, which have little value, how much more will he feed his people who have more value.\nScripture uses reasons all the time and it calls us to do the same. It constantly present logical, moral arguments.\n\nCONSCIENCE \nIt is God given ability to discern both good and evil. It is also referred to as the sense of conviction that our thoughts, words and deeds are either pleasing or offensive to God.\nConscience enables us to assess the knowledge we have acquired and to judge it against the standard of God's word. It approves us when we believe we are acting in accord with God's word and it condemns us when we believe we are violating God's word.\n\nEMOTIONS\nEmotions are inner feelings, they are affective in our ethincal sensitivity.\nEmotions are God given human abilities that enable us to assess our knowledge in many diferent ways. Our emotions are the initial interpretation of the facts.\nOur emotions can be very useful tools for determining how God's word applies to our lives\nFeeling of compassion can result in helping those in need\nAnger can persuade us to follow pursuing justice\nExperiences of Joy can enable us to affirm God's goodnees even in difficult times\nFeeling of guilt can alert us to times we have fallen into sin\n\nE	0	2020-08-19 22:14:46+05:30	2020-08-19 22:14:46+05:30	\N	518	863
1807	Will is the capacity for making decisions, it is our ability to make choices. \nWhen we consider our will, it is to view our entire decision making process from the perspective of the choices that we make, especially from the perspective of the final outcome.\n\nFor Christians this eans that while Holy Spirit enables us to make decisions that please God, there is always the possiblity that indwelling sin will persuade us to make sinful decisions. \n\nOur will may be neither active nor passive. We make decisions in a passive unconscious manner wuch as by force or habit. But at other times the ethical questions we face require reflection and conscious decisions.\n\nThere are many other ethical issues that we handle in a passive manner such as those that we deal with habitually or we respond to in a reflective manner.\n\nIf we really need to please our Lord, we must submit our will to him at every turn. We must will that God's word commands and we must allow the Holy Spirit working within us to influence our will.\n\nApostle Paul says in the book of Philippians\nIt is God who works in you to will and to according to his good purpose\n\nGod has given faculties that play important role in choosing good. If we overlook any of them we run the risk of not being able to make truly moral decisions.	0	2020-08-19 22:23:45+05:30	2020-08-19 22:23:45+05:30	\N	518	864
1808	Jesus here refers to the fact that motives of the heart does matter. God requires every heart and every motive behind the thoughts to be truly committed to him. He requires genuine obedience that flows from our deepest thoughts and desires. \n\nHerein Jesus refers as to who is a good man, who has his heart with the right motives, it is referred to as good treasure stored in his heart. And that treasure when it is traded or brought out makes the person itself good in the sight of God, his entire personality is changed due to the fact that the treasure stored in his heart is good. The radiance of God that flows out through his life, reflects his lifestyle and behavior and that does play a major role in impactive the lives of all those who come in contact with him and he is referred to as "Good Man" useful for God and man.\n\nBut whereas exactly the opposte - the Evil man is definied by his source of motive in the heart - wherein is stored evil motives, same fact here also but the treasure stored is evil.  His life and personality has a negative impact on the society. Life itself becomes a tragedy as the motives of the heart does matter in the sight of God and Man. His entire personality revolves around the evil that emanates from his heart. \n\nJesus made it very clear that a persons actions or words are known by what is stored in his heart.  Out of the abundance of his heart, his mouth speaks, and that defines his lifestyle in general.\n\nFrom this perspective Jesus wants all of us to surrender our lives and hearts to the will of God, and to draw closer to him in genuine good works with godly motives. \n\nMy heart and works are in sync with the Word of God and under the guidance of the Holy Spirit. \nAs long as we are in the flesh, there is bound to be corruption, but God is gracious and helps in correcting our desires and motives under the guidance of the Word and Holy Spirit. \n\nNo man is perfect, but everyone needs to strive and work out your own salvation and grow spiritually as Christ the Head.	0	2020-08-19 22:41:52+05:30	2020-08-19 22:41:52+05:30	\N	518	865
