1791	The concept of the church in the New Testament finds it roots in the Old Testament. The NT speaks many times of the church using the word ekklesia. But this term comes from the Greek translation of Old Testament, Septuagint. Ekklesia and its Hebrew counterpart is frequently used to identify the gathered nation of Israel. In the New Testament, ekklesia became a technical term referring to the church while being used to the assembly of Old Testament Israel. For example, in Acts 7.38- the Greek word translated assembly is ekklesia, which is commonly translated to ''church.'' This means that the assembly of Israel was Old Testament equivalent to our New Testament Church.\nWe can see that the Old Testament church has been improved and built into the New Testament church. They are different in some important ways and they represent different stages of growth, but are still the same church.	9	2020-08-16 21:09:34+05:30	2020-08-22 17:28:31+05:30	\N	520	886
1792	In the original context of Jesus ministry, the only church that existed was Jewish synagogues scattered throughout the region and the temple of Jerusalem. They were the Old Testament forms of Israel's assemblies, but Jesus still called them, ''the church.'' Firstly, the New Testament presents Jesus as the fulfillment of God's promises to Israel. As we see in Romans 8.1-4 and Galatians 3.16-29, Jesus is shown as the faithful Israelite who keeps God's covenant and inherits all the blessing, God promised to Abraham and Moses. \nSecondly, Jesus is the founder of  the New Testament church, who brought the restoration and renewal that changed the failing Old Testament into the New Testament church. He is called the head of the church in Ephesians 5.23 and Colossians 1.18. He is also called the husband of the church in Ephesians 5.22-23. \nThus Jesus built his church in these ways that relied and advanced the Old Testament church.	8	2020-08-16 21:18:38+05:30	2020-08-22 17:28:31+05:30	\N	520	887
1793	Jesus ordained the New Testament church as the restoration and development of Old Testament Israel, one important implication is that there is fundamental continuity between the Israel in the Old Testament and the present Christian church in the New Testament. Practically, we should expect the community of God's people in the New Testament to reflect its Old Testament. \nPurpose- to turn the world into God's earthly kingdom. Redemption of humanity and creation has always been the purpose of God's church in both the testaments. \nBelievers and Unbelievers- both assemblies include believers and unbelievers. The continuity between Old Testament church and New Testament in the present time is that both assemblies of God's people include believers and unbelievers.\nObligations- Both the testaments, people of God were given the responsibility to love God, spread His Kingdom throughout the world,and bring him glory. Greatest commandment is Heartfelt love for God as in 1 John 5.3.	8	2020-08-16 21:27:29+05:30	2020-08-22 17:28:31+05:30	\N	520	888
1794	The word ''catholic'' means universal; or including all Christians in all congregations. word Catholic coming from the Latin word Catholicus derives from Greek preposition  .Kata and the adjective holos means whole or ''complete.'' It is a description of the unity that exists between all churches that faithfully follow Christ. The Apostolic Creed speaks of the catholicity of the church, it does not have in mind an organization of all Christian congregations. Rather, it tells about the unity of the Holy Spirit that exists all legitimate Christian Churches. In history, it meant to extend the name ''church'' to every Christian congregation. At this time, these churches began to use the word ''catholic'' in new exclusive sense. By insisting that their own churches were catholic or universal, each meant to identify itself as the only valid church, and to condemn rival churches.	8	2020-08-16 21:40:32+05:30	2020-08-22 17:28:31+05:30	\N	520	889
1799	God was not willing to let the entire human race remain under the curse of sin. So He sent us the redeemer, Jesus Christ to solve the problem of sin. \n\n1. Father- The Father sent the Son into the world and appointed him as the redeemer. He is the great architect of redemption. \n2. Son- Son was sent to the world becoming incarnate as Jesus, the long awaited Messiah, in order to atone for human sin. Jesus atoned for sin by dying on the cross in place of sinners. He received the divine curse that had been warranted by our sin. \n3. Holy Spirit- It reconciles us to God for forgiving all the sins we have committed. He gives us new spiritual life by regenerating our spirits, implications of the saving grace, continuity to forgiveness to us and maintains our faith, and leads us to daily repentance. \n\nWe have been given the right to make petition to all three divine persons, give thanks to all three persons of God and take great confidence n our salvation, knowing that all three persons of the Trinity love us and work to ensure our redemption.	8	2020-08-19 21:18:48+05:30	2020-08-22 17:29:12+05:30	\N	521	892
1801	The process of forgiveness involves an element of individual responsibility.\n1. Conditions- Faith in God- God's divine sovereignty, loyal submission to him, and trust that he will show us mercy for sake of our redeemer Jesus Christ should be acknowledged. Those who fear the Lord receive his forgiveness. Genuine sorrow over sin and true regret over violating God's law shows the brokenness of heart. \n2. Means- Sometimes Christians fail to distinguish between means of grace and basis of grace. The basis of grace is the merit of Christ. The tool is prayer and means of grace is faith. Prayer is an ordinary means for appealing to God for grace and forgiveness. We can receive forgiveness by asking for it with a broken heart. Prayer of intercession acts as extra ordinary means of forgiveness. Sacraments, which refer to Lord's supper and baptism, become means to ask for forgiveness.	8	2020-08-19 21:33:14+05:30	2020-08-22 17:29:12+05:30	\N	521	893
1803	God created human beings to consist of physical bodies and non physical souls, as according to Hebrews 4.12 and 1 Thessalonians 5.23. Before our fall into the sin, our bodies and our souls were unaffected by sin and its corrupting powers. But when Adam and Eve fell into sin, sin corrupted not only their souls, but also their bodies. And this corruption of their bodies ultimately resulted in their physical death. \nWhen Adam and Eve sinned against God, he cursed them both with that they would be mortal. They would die and return to dust. Since all human beings descend from Adam and Eve, we all are born with similar corruption. \nSin affected Adam and Eve in both spiritual and physical ways. Our souls come into the world in a state that Bible describes as spiritual death. We are all under God's judgement and have lost all ability to please him. This corruption results in physical hardship, sickness and eventually death, as said in Romans 6.12-19 and 7.4-25. Sin therefore, corrupts all that we are- our entire being, body and soul.	9	2020-08-19 21:46:05+05:30	2020-08-22 17:29:12+05:30	\N	521	894
1804	The three stages of redemption is as follows,\n1. Present Life;- Salvation of our bodies begins with the indwelling of the Holy Spirit. Bible teaches that salvation of our bodies begins when we come to faith, with the indwelling of Holy Spirit. and our bodies continue to benefit through it, by the process of sanctification. Sanctification of our bodies is similar to the sanctification of our souls.\n2. Intermediate State- During this state, our souls dwell with Christ in heaven while our bodies remain on earth. When we die, our bodies are temporarily separated from our souls. Our bodies are still corrupted by sin, as proven by the fact that they can decay. But the sin that corrupts them can no longer influence us to commit sin.\n3. New Life- Our bodies will receive new, perfect life when they are restored to life in the general resurrection. In the resurrection, the consequences of sin will finally and forever be completely put away from us.	8	2020-08-19 21:54:26+05:30	2020-08-22 17:29:12+05:30	\N	521	895
