1819	The word derives from Greek words related to the verb hermeneuo, which means ''interpret'' or ''explain.'' Hermeneutics refers to the interpretation or explanation of some kind of message or communication. \nFreiedrich Schleiermarcher [1768-1834] is the father of modern hermeneutics. In 1819 he spoke of the need for ''general hermeneutics'',a unified theory for understanding all literature. \nBiblical hermeneutics is the study of interpreting the meaning and significance of Scripture. Informal approaches to the Bible are of great value, and these lessons will build on what most of us already understand. But we'll also move beyond informal hermeneutics and explore the kinds of issues that move to the foreground in academic, scholarly interpretation of the Bible.	8	2020-08-27 21:07:36+05:30	2020-09-08 06:02:53+05:30	\N	525	675
1820	The biblical roots of scientific hermeneutics make it a legitimate exercise. People living in biblical times weren't modern scientist. But this doesn't mean that they were unintelligent or irrational. On the contrary, their sophisticated architectural accomplishments, extensive maritime travel, innovative agricultural programs, and countless other cultural achievements demonstrate that people in biblical days dealt with facts and thought rationally about the world, much like modern scientists do.\nFor this reason, Biblical authors interpreted other scriptures with an orientation toward factual and logical analysis [Romans 4.3-5]. As this one example illustrates, time and again biblical authors presented this kind of careful interpretation of scripture. And their approach to Scripture indicates that scientific biblical hermeneutics is firmly rooted in the Scriptures themselves.	8	2020-08-27 21:15:57+05:30	2020-09-08 06:02:53+05:30	\N	525	676
1821	Devotional hermeneutics is the Christian tradition of emphasizing our need to draw near to God as we interpret the scriptures. Devotional hermeneutics focuses primarily on the divine authorship of scripture. Christians have acknowledged that the human words of scripture are also word of God. As 2 Timothy 3.16 tells us, the scriptures were inspired by God, or more literally were ''God-breathed.'' This fact makes biblical hermeneutics distinct from other facets of general hermeneutics because we must interpret the scriptures devotionally, as the living God himself. The biblical authors approached the scriptures devotionally. Followers of Christ are to read the scriptures as the word of God, in the presence of God, in ways that bring about extraordinary even supernatural experiences of God.	8	2020-08-27 21:22:58+05:30	2020-09-08 06:02:53+05:30	\N	525	677
1822	There are two important aspects of organic inspiration that helps us orient ourselves to task of interpretation ; first, the fact that the Holy Spirit was the divine source of scripture, and second the fact that he worked through human means to produce scripture. \n1] Divine Source- The Holy Spirit has intimate knowledge of the Bible's meaning and of the way it communicates that meaning. One of the most important implications of the divine origin of scripture is the Bible's unquestionable veracity. \n     a] Critical Interpreter - Reject the divine divine inspiration of scripture and elevate their understanding over the spirit's authority\n     b] Submissive interpreter- affirm the divine inspiration of scripture, expecting and assuming it to be true and harmonious even when they can't demonstrate or prove its truthfulness.\n2] Human Means- God had the scripture composed by means if human authors. The Scriptures were written by different people, and they reflect the diversity of that human authorship.	8	2020-08-27 21:31:50+05:30	2020-09-08 06:02:53+05:30	\N	525	678
1823	Illumination\nThe Holy Spirit grants us knowledge of His word. And this knowledge isn't purely cognitive.  It also imparts our imagination, intuition, emotion, will, motivation, desire, moral conscience- any part of us that contributes to our understanding of scripture can be illumined by the our understanding of scripture can be illumined by the Spirit. Human beings are so deeply affected by sin that we can't understand the things of God on our own. We desperately need God himself- the Holy Spirit- to illumine us. Without the work of the Spirit, we have no hope of grasping the thoughts of God as we should. This is why the Spirit's personal illumination is so important to our interpretation of the Scriptures.	8	2020-08-27 21:37:48+05:30	2020-09-08 06:02:53+05:30	\N	525	679
