1825	Critical interpreters have suggested at least three alternatives. some interpreters have looked for an unknown James in the early church. They say that the person who letter was indeed James , but he wasn't the son of Jesus. The simplicity of the author's identification at the beginning of the letter indicates that he was well known.\nAnother theory that critical interpreters states is that of pseudonymity. it refers to the practice of assigning written books to someone other than the actual author. first they say  that there is no mention of the author's relation to Jesus. they say that it's unthinkable that a brother of Jesus would write to the churches and not reveal this familial bond when he identified himself. \nSecond , some critical scholars assume pseudonymity because the book gives evidence that the author was hellenistic  or Greek, James was from Palestine. \nA third argument for pseudonymity points to inconsistencies with the theological portrait of James in the books of Acts and Galatians.\n we affirm the traditional outlook that Jesus' brother James wrote this epistle ,as the writer didn't give any credentials beyond saying that he was a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ. And based on this authority, he gave command after command. This open greeting makes a strong sense for Jesus' brother James because of his status in the early church.\nIn the second place , the testimony of the early church confirms this outlook on te authorship of the book. The first epistle of Clement, written aorund A.D 96 and the shepherd of Hermas, written around A. D 140, both refer to or quote from James commentary on the epistle to the Romans.	9	2020-08-27 23:23:11+05:30	2020-09-08 06:03:21+05:30	\N	526	771
1826	In James 1:1 James identified his readers as " the twelve tribes scattered among the nations ".\nThis seems to be  a reference to Jews who lived outside of Israel and in 2:1. James addresses his audience as Believers in our glorious Lord Jesus Christ (James 2:1).\n Taken together, these verses indicate that James' original audience was made up primarily of Jewish Christians who lived outside of Palestine.\nOn several occasions in his book, James addressed his audience affectionately as "brothers".\nJames even being in Jerusalem, know his audience well enough to speak to them in  such a way as we read in Acts 8:1-4, in the wave of persecution following Stephen's martyrdom , members of the Jerusalem church were scattered throughout Judea and Samaria. So may be James being the leader of the Jerusalem church was writing to the scattered members of the twelve tribes.\nThe vocabulary James used also supports the idea that his original leaders were Jewish followers of Jesus. As in James 2:2 James chose the word Synagogue in Hebrew language original script.	8	2020-08-27 23:35:58+05:30	2020-09-08 06:03:21+05:30	\N	526	772
1827	Location\nBoth the New Testament and early church fathers suggest that James lived his life of ministry in Jerusalem. And he remained in Jerusalem until he was martyred in A. D 62, so there is no reason to think that he wrote the epistle from any other location.\nDate\nActs 12:17 indicates that James became a significant leader of the Jerusalem church by the time of Peter;s release from prison. According to Acts 12:19-23, Peter was released in the year Herod Agrippa I died in A.D 44. So this letter was not written much before this date. \nSecond possible date for the epistle is A. D 62, the year of James martyrdom.\nPurpose:\nJames 1:2-4 says that  "considers it pure joy whenever you face trials and James called them to have pure joy in their trials. A we have read that, James audience was scattered abroad , far from their homes , so it wasn't easy for them to find joy in their trials far from their homes. This appears to have led some of them to abandon their loyalty to Christ. Instead they were pursuing what James called friendship with the world.  (James 4:4). So he commanded and exerted his authority as a leader of the church.	8	2020-08-27 23:53:19+05:30	2020-09-08 06:03:21+05:30	\N	526	773
