1862	The famous theologian Thomas Aquinas, who lived from around 1225 to 1275, championed a much more responsible approach to Quadriga. Unlike many of his predecessors and contemporaries, he insisted that the literal sense is the singular meaning of Scripture. He also taught that nothing necessary to faith was communicated as spiritual meaning without being taught elsewhere in scripture in the literal sense. Not all scholars would agree that Aquinas always followed these principles as he interpreted the scriptures, but nevertheless he insisted the principle that every sense of a passage of scripture must be tied to its literal meanings. The meaning of scripture is multifaceted. Each passage's meanings has many smaller parts of aspects that contribute to the singular unified meaning we've called its literal sense. But the most responsible approach to the richness of scriptures is to ensure that everything we say about a biblical passage is tied to its grammar set within the historical context of the ancient world.	8	2020-09-04 21:09:25+05:30	2020-09-08 06:05:46+05:30	\N	531	687
1863	Biblical Elaborations are places where one part of scripture directly or indirectly comments on an aspect of the meaning of another passage in scripture, these elaborations always accord with and confirm original meaning.\n1] The first biblical elaboration of this passage is primarily a repetition of these exact words in Deuteronomy 5, where Moses reminded the nation of Israel of the content of Ten Commandments. In Deuteronomy 5.17, scripture again says, You shall not murder. This repetition confirmed the commandment and reminded God's people of the term of his covenant. \n2] The second type of elaboration is clarification, and we find clarification of commandment against murder in Numbers 35. In that chapter, Moses distinguished between murder and accidental manslaughter.\n3] The third type of biblical elaboration was expansion, in which scripture provides additional information about the passage or topic it references. We find an expansion of commandment against murder in Matthew 5, where Jesus criticized the rabbis of his day for wrongly limiting the commandment's scope	8	2020-09-04 21:20:29+05:30	2020-09-08 06:05:46+05:30	\N	531	688
