1865	The writer, the document, and the audience are interdependent guides to meaning. If we fail to account for their interdependence, it's very easy to fall into error. We need guidance from all of these sources so that our readings of Scripture aren't skewed by an imbalanced emphasis on just one or two. The three interpretive fallacies are \nIntentional fallacy- If we overemphasize the author, we often fall into an intentional fallacy. An intentional fallacy relies too heavily on what we think we know about a writer and his intentions, and de-emphasizes the things we learn about document and audience. \nGraphic fallacy- over emphasizes the document itself to the relative exclusion of contextual considerations like writer and audience. \nAffective fallacy- Overemphasizes the audience and focuses too heavily on how scripture affects the audience.	8	2020-09-04 21:44:41+05:30	2020-09-08 06:05:46+05:30	\N	531	690
1866	Normally a summary will come from a particular vantage point or emphasize a particular concept that appears in the passage. Since every passage has a complex meaning, summaries help us by narrowing our study, allowing us to focus on just a portion of what the passage has to say. Multiple legitimate summaries bring us closer and closer to the passage's full meaning. One student summarizes how characters developed in the story. One summarizes cultural values of the day. Another summarizes the events in chronological order. All of these responses are legitimate summaries of the presentation's meaning. But not the full meaning of the entire thing. So multiple summaries help us focus on individual aspects of meaning and allows us learn the whole picture. And taken together, it brings us closer and closer to the passage's full meaning.	8	2020-09-04 21:52:03+05:30	2020-09-08 06:05:46+05:30	\N	531	691
