1875	God is immutable, meaning that he doesn't change. Since God is immutable. The biblical concept of divine immutability doesn't imply that God is inactive. But the God of Scripture is constantly interacting with his creation is real and meaningful ways. \n\n1. Eternal Counsel- Christian traditions understand God's eternal plan differently, everything God has done, is doing, and will do is part of a unified plan. God knows everything, and he is using that knowledge to direct history towards the ends for which he created it God's eternal counsel, or ultimate plan for history, is immutable and directs history toward the ends for which he created it.\n\n2. Character - God reveals different aspects of his character more prominently at some times than others. Sometimes he displays his mercy, sometimes his wrath. Sometimes he reveals his omniscience and other times he hides it. But the full range of his attributes- his eternal nature- always stays the same. God's character, his essence, persons and attributes, never change.\n\n3. Covenant Promises- Every revelation of God in scripture assumes that God will keep his covenant and his covenant promises. The original audiences were supposed to understand every passage of scripture in this light, and modern audiences should do the same. Where God hasn't made a promise, his words are best understood as threats of curse and offers of blessing.	9	2020-09-07 21:10:45+05:30	2020-09-08 06:06:13+05:30	\N	534	692
1876	There are at least three ways in which modern people are very similar to the people who first received the scriptures. First, all human beings, no matter when or where they live, are sinful images of God. Second, we suffer religious divisions. and third, humanity still includes the same classes of people. \n1. Sinful Images- God created humanity in his image, but we have fallen into sin. All recipients of scripture, whether ancient or modern, share the same sinful nature.\n2. Religious Divisions- Both the original and modern audiences of scripture suffer religious divisions. Readers of scripture fall into one of three religious groups; Unbelievers- who refuse to submit to God. False believers- people who make superficial commitments to God. Believers- who make faithful commitments to God. \n3. Classes- the same classes of people exist for both the original and modern audiences. Many portions of scripture are directed toward particular classes. The connections we share with the classes of people that exist in every age can help guide our application of Scripture.	8	2020-09-07 21:17:51+05:30	2020-09-08 06:06:13+05:30	\N	534	693
1877	The developments between original and modern audiences should influence our application. We need to account for the epochal developments of redemptive history when we apply the Bible to modern life. \nThe Bible's perspective on world history in three stages; \nCreation- the progressive nature of God's rule over creation has resulted in periodic developments that create discontinuity between the various epochs mentioned in scripture. When God first made the world. But theologians also commonly identify epochs according to God's various covenants throughout the Bible, especially those associated with Adam, Noah, Abraham, Moses and David in the Old Testament, and with Jesus in the New Testament. \nFall- when humanity first committed sin and was cursed by God. \nRedemption- the period following the fall, in which God redeems us from our sin. Soon after Adam and Eve fell into sin, God began a long slow process of redemption. And throughout the millennia, he had mercifully built his kingdom of redemption within and alongside the cursed creation.	8	2020-09-07 21:26:01+05:30	2020-09-08 06:06:13+05:30	\N	534	694
2029	Terms used for "hell" in Scripture: \nSheol- home of unregenerate souls (Psalm 9:17)\nAbaddon- "destruction" (Job 26:6, Proverbs 15:11)\nBowr- "pit"; a place of punishment (Isaiah 14:15-19)\nHades- place of punishment (Luke 10:15)\nabussos- "abyss" prison for demons (Luke 8:31)\nplace for human souls, including Jesus' righteous soul (Romans 10:7)\nGehenna- universally identifies a place of fiery punishment, torment and destruction. It's mentioned in many passages, including Matthew 5:22. Gehenna is often described as bodily suffering, which associates it with the final state, rather than with the intermediate state. Suggesting that it's where unregenerate souls go during the intermediate state. Jesus described a rich man who died and was immediately tormented in Hades.	8	2020-10-16 21:32:52+05:30	2020-10-17 03:03:57+05:30	\N	567	641
1878	Significant theological changes are the basis for identifying epochal divisions. Each epoch is period of time characterized by substantial and long lasting transitions in Old Testament theology. Time doesn't pass in sharply segmented periods. So, transitions between epochs generally took place gradually. And the epochs themselves often overlapped. The Old Testament reports many theological changes that took place throughout history. Theological changes occurred every time God altered his expectations regarding the concepts, behaviors and emotions of his people. And when the changes were significant enough, they became the basis for identifying epochal divisions. In similar way, theologians obviously tend to use theological criteria to delineate the epochs. But they still don't always agree on the divisions because theology within the Old Testament developed in different ways at different times. In a similar way, some parts of Old Testament theology advanced slowly, others proceeded at a medium pace, and others changed rapidly. And many of these parts did their growing at different times.	9	2020-09-07 21:34:53+05:30	2020-09-08 06:06:13+05:30	\N	534	695
1879	The divisions of the Old Testament make it clear that God wanted his people to apply theological themes in different ways at different times. And just as Old Testament believers weren't supposed to serve God as if they lived in earlier periods of history, New Testament believers should never apply the scriptures as if they live in earlier periods of history. Once God commanded a new way for sacrifice in worship, he expected his people never to return to the old ways. Christ's sacrifice has superseded every earlier form of sacrifice [Hebrews 8.13]\nAs people living under the new covenant, we follow Jesus the great son of David, as he leads his people into war against the forces of evil. Another striking example is the theme of leadership in battle. Imagine that you live in the period of David's dynastic covenant. You know that God has ordained the kings of Israel to lead his people into war against evil. The kings receive direction from God, and in turn direct the ways that you participate in war. \nGod has always expected his people to serve him in ways that were appropriate to the covenant age in which they lived. So, understanding how Old Testament history divided into major covenant epoch is essential for applying the scriptures in our day.	9	2020-09-07 21:44:58+05:30	2020-09-08 06:06:13+05:30	\N	534	696
