1966	Flood of Deliverance- These chapters form a symmetrical five-step drama. \n1. Initial Covenant- The first step of this narrative also tells us that God spoke to Noah and revealed why he planned to destroy the human race. God planned to start over again by delivering Noah and his family. \n2. Enduring Covenant- God entered into a second covenant with Noah at the end of the flood narrative. God decided to give humanity the opportunity of a new order in the world. Noah was the mediator and God promised a covenant that flood won't destroy the earth and setting his bow was a sure sign.\n3. Escape from Water- It appears in 7:1-16. Noah prepared the ark and brought animals of every kind into it. Noah, his family and the animals he had gathered were safely sealed in the ark. \n4. Exit to dry land- A dramatic counterpoint to the second step. After a waiting period, dry lands appeared God commanded Noah to leave the ark, just as he had previously ordered him to enter it. \n5. Divine Remembrance- The center or turning point of the narrative. God did not forget the covenant he made with Noah. he remembered the passengers of the ark, and moved on their behalf against raging flood.\n Moses' chief concern was to show that through Noah God brought humanity into a world of tremendous blessings.	9	2020-10-06 21:11:21+05:30	2020-10-11 06:52:21+05:30	\N	555	821
