2051	Critical scholars have offered three arguments with regards to the authorship of the Epistle of James\n\n1) They say that there is no mention of the author's relation to Jesus in the entire Epistle. They suggest that it's not possible that a brother of Jesus would write to the churches and not reveal his identity or relationship details when he identified himself\n\n2) Some other critical scholars assume psedonymity, as the book gives insights into the Greek culture, whereas James was from Palestine. Some of the Greek phrases or terminology used in the book of James is more advanced.\n\n3) The thrird view is that some of the views expressed in the epistle of James does not tally or match with  theological outlooks in the New Testament books. \n\nBut when we study or analyze the epistle traditionally, these arguments against the book's authoriship are weak and substantial to prove otherwise.\n\nThe arguments in fact are more compelling to prove that James has written this epistle. Also because of this, most evangelical scholars rightly affirm that James the brother of Jesus was the author of the letter that bears his name.	8	2020-10-21 20:46:31+05:30	2020-10-21 22:51:34+05:30	\N	562	771
2052	As the Epistle of James 1: verse 1 begins by addressing the epistle to \n1) The Twelve tribes scattered among the nations\n2) James 2:1 - addresses to Believers in our glorious Lord Jesus Christ\n\nWhen we consider the above 2 verses, it indicates that James original audience was primarily made up of Jewish Christians who lived outside of Jerusalem or Palestine.\n\nAfter persecution of the church began after Stephen's death the church were scattered throughtout Judea and Samaria. James was the leader of Jerusalem Church and was writing this epistle to the scattered members of the twelve tribes.\n\nThought the language and vocabulory supports the idea that it was addressed to the Jewish followers of Jesus. \n\nBut also there were many Gentile God fearing converts to Judais who attended synagogues, Gentile believers were regarded as Abraham's offspiring. Hence the epistle also covered gentile believers.	8	2020-10-21 20:52:47+05:30	2020-10-21 22:51:34+05:30	\N	562	772
2053	James the brother of Jesus Christ wrote this epistle addressed to the Jewish believers :\n\nPLACE :\nJames lived all his life in Jerusalem, until he was martyred, hence he wrote this epistle from Jerusalem\n\nWHEN : \nThis Epistle was written in AD 44, as leader of the early church in Jerusalem, \n\nFurthermore there is another probability that the latest possible date of composition is AD 62, the year of James martyrdom.\n\nBut since the epistle talks about the word synagogue, it seems to indicate an early stage in the development of Christian movement. \n\nWHY ? PURPOSE ?\nJames 1:2-4 addresses his readers\nThe early Jewish converts were facing trials of many kinds, James was encouraging them and motivating them, saying that the testing of your faith produces perseverance. And let perseverance finish its work that you may be mature and complete, not lacking anything\n\nJames audience were facing trials of many kinds, James was calling them to have pur joy in their trials. Trials produce persevrance. Those who persevere will become mature and complete not lacking anything. Facing trials, ask God for wisdom and it will be given to you\n\nJames called his audience to pursue wisdom from God so that they would have joy in their trials. Wisdom from God was the key to receiving joy as they endured their many trials.	8	2020-10-21 21:09:28+05:30	2020-10-21 22:51:34+05:30	\N	562	773
2056	The Epistle of James focuses a great deal of attention on wisdom as the way to find joy in trimes of trial. Many interpreters have considered it to be a book of wisdom.\n\nPreceding the Epistle of James, there were alread wisdom writings in the Old Testament, including the books of Job, Ecclesiastes and Proverbs. James indebtedness to the Old Testament literature is evident when he takes the example of Job to proote perseverance. \n\nJames touched on many topics, such as speech, the treatment of widows, orphans, poverty and favoritism. These topics reflect numerous parallels with the content of the book of Proverbs.\n\nMuch of Jesus instruction recorded in the gospel is characteristic of wisdom teaching in Israel, biblical scholars have noticed a number of similarities between the writing of James and instructions from Jesus\n\nJames book resembles wisdom literature in form and content. It focuses on important themes, by spending few verses on a particular topic and thereafter moving on to another. \n\nThe most important point which is unique to James in terms of wisdom is that he links his wisdom very closely with the teachings of Jesus. \n\nJames uses a wide range of practical illustrations to explain his point to the readers, referring to ships being guided by little rudders and farers that are patiently waiting, about merchants that are travelling. \n\nBut the content of James is really carrying forward the way in which Jesus presents the kindom and the way the presence of the kingdom changes your life.\n\nAs part of the New Testament, James is an epistle written to the Church to gain practical wisdom.	9	2020-10-21 21:21:25+05:30	2020-10-21 22:51:34+05:30	\N	562	774
2059	James audience were the Jewish believers, Christians who had been driven out of Jerusalems and were scattered around, away from their homes. They were facing different kinds of trials that discouraged them. And for this reason James first words about the importance of wisdom began with a call to joy.\n\nJames 1:2 says\nConsider it pure joy, whenever you face trials of many kinds\n\nJames is encouraging his readers and appealing to them to consider trials pure joy, complete and unmitigated joy.\n\nJames taught that perseverance through trials makes it possible for believers to be mature and complete. In other words, when God's people endure hardships, they grow into the fullness of all that God intends for them. \n\nJames 1:5 \nIf any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask God who gives generously\n\nJames suggested that those who want to have pure joy as they suffer trials must ask God for  insight and wisdom to help them understand how their trials will lead them to a better understanding that God intends us to have. We need to have wisdom to understand how God works through the trials and we can be hence joyful. Wisdom strengthens our confidence and our trust in god increases preparing us for eternal salvation.	8	2020-10-21 21:39:45+05:30	2020-10-21 22:51:34+05:30	\N	562	775
2005	The word eschatology comes from two Greek words: eschatos meaning "last" or "final"; and logos in this case meaning "study". So, "eschatology" is "the study of last things" or "the doctrine of last things". Broadly understood, eschatology covers the entire period of the last days that began with Jesus' life and ministry, and that will be completed when he returns.\nOld Testament Expectation: God's kingdom was expected to unfold in three phases: the initial creation of the universe and its creatures; a long period of redemption necessitated by humanity's fall into sin; and finally, the everlasting eschaton- the ultimate state of the universe, after redemption is complete, when God's heavenly kingdom fills the earth.	7	2020-10-14 21:06:17+05:30	2020-10-17 03:02:06+05:30	\N	563	633
