2006	Creation\nGod's work of creation is detailed in Genesis chapters 1 & 2. God intended humanity to be his "vassals" or servant kings to rule over creation on behalf of God, the great "suzerain" or emperor. \nCultural Mandate: God's command to reproduce and create human culture throughout the world (Genesis 1:27-28). That means not only reproducing to create enough people to fill the world, but also creating human culture throughout the world. Humanity's directive to "work" and "take care" of the garden (Genesis 2:15) uses language that also describes the priests' work in the tabernacle (Numbers 3:8).\nHuman beings are God's royal and priestly images that serve and honor him by ruling over creation on his behalf.  Our job is to extend his rule until it fills the world, and to cultivate the whole earth until everything resembles the Garden of Eden.	8	2020-10-14 21:13:52+05:30	2020-10-17 03:02:06+05:30	\N	563	634
2008	Redemption \nAll Christian should know the story of humanity's fall into sin recorded in Genesis 3. God put Adam and Eve in the garden to work it and to take care of it. But Satan spoke through the serpent in the garden. He tricked Eve into eating the fruit from the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil, which God had forbidden. Then Eve gave the fruit to Adam and he ate it, too. Because of this sin, God cursed all humanity, and the great hope of his eschatological kingdom appeared to be lost. But God mercifully introduced a plan of redemption that would rescue individuals through personal salvation, and would restore the hope of his great messianic kingdom on earth. Proto-Euangelion (''first gospel")- the plan of redemption first introduced after the Fall (Genesis 3:15). God promised to redeem humanity from the curse of sin. He continued to affirm this expectation through redemptive acts associated with his covenants.	8	2020-10-14 21:20:54+05:30	2020-10-17 03:02:06+05:30	\N	563	635
2010	1. Adam- After Adam sinned, God offered him salvation through the proto-euangelion (Genesis 3:15). Expectation- God's worldwide kingdom would come to pass, but its growth would be characterized by strife between the children of the serpent and the children of Eve. From the beginning, human race has been divided between those who serve God's purposes and those who rebel against God.\n2. Noah- God made a covenant through Noah never to flood the earth again and to establish the stability of nature (Gen 8:21-9:17).\nExpectation- The growth of God's kingdom would proceed without further global catastrophes. \n3. Abraham- God called Abraham and his descendants to serve him in a special way. They were the special nation through whom God would extend redemption to rest of humanity.\nExpectation- God's earthly kingdom would include members from all nations on earth. \n4. Moses- God confirmed that the Mosaic covenant incorporated and continued the early covenants. God promised if they repented of their sin and sought him with all their heart and soul, he would restore them to his in "later days"\nExpectations- In future age, God would fulfill all of his covenant promises of blessings for his people and judgement against his enemies.\n5. David- God promised to establish the house of David as permanent dynasty ruling God's earthly kingdom. \nExpectation- God's kingdom is a real place, populated by real people who will live in perfect harmony with God forever.	9	2020-10-14 21:32:16+05:30	2020-10-17 03:02:06+05:30	\N	563	636
2011	Theological Developments, two great ages of Jewish eschatology: "this age" (olam hazeh)- characterized by sin, exile, suffering, and death; began at the Fall and corresponds to the age of redemption. And "the age to come" (olam haba)- future eschaton when God's kingdom would fill the earth; characterized by forgiveness, righteousness, peace and eternal life. Most first century Jewish sects agreed that the Messiah would lead an army of angels and faithful men to victory over Israel's enemies. Many of God's people that the prophecies in Daniel chapters 2&7 indicated that their oppression was about to end. Jesus and New Testament writers also taught the Messiah would end "this age" of sin and death and usher in "the age to come" with all its blessing (Mark 10:29-30). Jesus indicated that he was the Messiah that was expected to bring transition from this age to the age to come (Mark 10:29; Matthew 12:32). Paul associated the triumph of the age to come with Christ, the Messiah (Eph 1:20-21, 1 Cor 2:6-8, 1 Tim 6:17-19). Jesus and his apostles and prophets agreed with most other Jews in their day about the basic structure of the eschaton.	9	2020-10-14 21:44:00+05:30	2020-10-17 03:02:06+05:30	\N	563	637
