2085	The scriptures are clear that Jesus Christ, the second person of the Trinity, is uncreated and eternal, just like God the Father. He is equal to the Father in power and glory, sharing equally in all the attributes of the Godhead. And the same thing is true of the Holy spirit as well. In fact, every Trinitarian formula found in Scripture mentions the Holy Spirit as an  equal member of the Trinity, together with the Father and the Son. The Trinitarian formulas of the New Testament help us to understand that the Holy Spirit is fully divine and fully personal. For example, In the Great commission Jesus commands us to baptize people in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit. And in Paul's benediction at the end of 2 Corinthians 13: "The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, the love of God, and the fellowship of the Holy Spirit.." again there's a blessing in the name of the triune God, grace extended through the sacrifice of the Son, motivated by the love of God and applied to us by the Holy Spirit.  So, we see the Spirit is fully divine, equal with the Father and the Son, and fully personal, no less than the Father and the Son.	8	2020-10-26 21:52:53+05:30	2020-10-27 23:56:29+05:30	\N	579	931
2100	From biblical theology, we can benefit from reading the early parts of the Bible without knowing how it ends. But if we do not also study the ending of the Bible, the New Testament. The Old Testament raises questions, and hopes, but the answers, resolutions, and fulfillment appear at the end of the scriptures, in the New Testament.  \nChristians have tended to follow three main strategies toward exegesis, or interpretation, of Scripture: literature analysis, looking at the Bible as a literary portrait designed to emphasize certain theological perspective;  thematic analysis, looking at the Bible as a minor that reflects our traditional and contemporary interests and questions;  One of the best ways to get at the heart of New Testament biblical theology is to compare and contrast it with what we have learned about biblical theology of the Old Testament.	7	2020-10-28 07:43:39+05:30	2020-11-05 23:44:14+05:30	\N	580	670
