2126	The three members of the Trinity are each eternal and uncreated. There was never a time when any member of the Trinity didn't exist. But the Father's relationship in the Trinity is unique. The Father is not begotten and doesn't proceed from any of the other members of the Trinity. He has never become incarnate, and the other two members of the Trinity have never sent him to do divine work. There's one God, yet they exist in some type of mysterious unity as one God, three persons. It's clear in New Testament that son gladly and joyfully submits to the Father and Spirit gladly and joyfully submits to Father and to the Son. They are always of one mind. They are always of one will and they work in an absolute perfect unity and harmony. So any idea that there's a disagreement among the Triune God is just a creation of man's imagination and nothing such can be found in God's revealed Word.	8	2020-11-01 21:37:24+05:30	2020-11-05 23:39:46+05:30	\N	584	937
2127	Just as the Father's relationship in the Trinity is unique, the Son's relationship is also unique. The Bible uses language that tells us that the Father "begat" the Son. Scriptures like Revelation 5:13,14 are clear that Son is eternal. He has always existed. Because of this theologians say that the Son is "eternally begotten" of the Father. New Testament uses the word "God", as first person and so when there's a father, there has to be a Son, and this relationship goes back to eternity. He's not a creature out of nothing but He's a Son begotten of his Father from eternity before all worlds. Son is of one substance with the Father, and through the Son all things were made, so that the Father, through the Son, made all things.	7	2020-11-01 21:43:26+05:30	2020-11-05 23:39:46+05:30	\N	584	938
2128	When we say that the eternal and uncreated Holy Spirit "proceeds" from His Father, we do not mean that the Spirit had a beginning. Just like the Father and Son, the Spirit is eternal, without beginning or end. So, Holy Spirit eternally proceeds from the Father. This is the doctrine of Holy Spirit's "eternal procession". The Bishop of Constantinople said that the Holy spirit was created being like an angel and that he was subordinate to the Son. Constantinople reaffirmed Nicea, and added that Holy Spirit is Lord. He is life-giver. He is to be worshipped as the Father and the Son. And this is known as the Nicea Creed. By calling the Holy Spirit both "Spirit of God" and "Spirit of Christ," Paul implied that the Spirit not only proceeds from God, the Father, but also proceeds from Christ, the Son. Other passages that teach this same doctrine include John 16:13-15; 20:22 and Galatians 4:6.	8	2020-11-01 21:58:23+05:30	2020-11-05 23:39:46+05:30	\N	584	939
2129	In 589 A.D, a church council met in the western city of Toledo, Spain and among other things, attempted to solidify the doctrine of eternal procession. Here western theologians added the Latin term "filioque", meaning "and the Son," to the Nicene Creed of 381 AD. With this addition, creed clearly articulates that the Holy Spirit "proceeds" or "spirates" from the Father and the Son. They argued in the filioque clause that means "and from the Son"- they affirmed the double procession of the Holy Spirit and the Spirit proceeded from the Father and the Son. Many would say that it was a political matter but the truth was  that a big division came over the very issue of the trinity. And it is very clear that the issue that really divides the East and the West on this doctrine of the Trinity is this issue of the filioque or the double procession of the Spirit.	8	2020-11-01 22:15:10+05:30	2020-11-05 23:39:46+05:30	\N	584	940
2130	the word "Ontological" refers to being and existence. So, the theological term "ontological Trinity"- has to do with the existence and personal interaction of the Father, Son and Holy Spirit apart from their acts of creation and salvation. We refer to the fact that the three persons of the Trinity- Father, Son and Holy Spirit- are equal in substance, power and glory. And none of them are superior in this regard. The Father is God. The Son is God. The Holy Spirit is God. It means God in himself, God as present only to himself and what God in himself beyond all time and space, before creation, God within himself. The ontological Trinity is what from Scripture, we derive as to the relations within God himself beyond all creation. It is the same God at work in creation, but God in an inner life of the Father, Son and Holy Spirit relating to each other eternally.	8	2020-11-01 22:23:48+05:30	2020-11-05 23:39:46+05:30	\N	584	941
2136	Missions originated in the heart of God. It is not something we decide to do for God, but God reveals his purpose to us so that we may have a creative part in his mission. God's mission, however, is to redeem man from the clutches of evil and save him. Three basic purposes are;\n1. To bring glory to God- In Ephesians, Paul stated three times that God's eternal plan is for his people to be to the praise of his glory (Eph 1:6,12,14). God stands as both the originator and the goal of the redemptive process. The glory of God is the ultimate goal of God's mission. God receives glory when man fully realizes the purpose of his existence, Eph 3:19)\n2. To share the good news with the alienated\nGod's mission includes recreating man spiritually.  "We are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus unto good works , which God prepared before ordained that we should walk in them" (Eph 2:10) God restores man's identity and his purpose for being. The mystery of God's mission is clear: "That the Gentiles should be fellow heirs, and of the same body and partakers of his promise" (Eph 3:6)\n3. To display the wisdom of God to evil powers- Man becomes God's display of his wisdom to Satan and his evil beings: "In order that the manifold wisdom of God might now be made known through the church to the rulers and authorities in the heavenly places" Redeemed man is the primary exhibit of God's grace. God seems to be depending on us to demonstrate his goodness, wisdom, and power. We do not understand what is at sake for God, but we are told that it is supremely important to him and us.	8	2020-11-04 21:12:58+05:30	2020-11-05 23:38:51+05:30	\N	585	717
2137	God's new strategy was to select one individual as his friend and partner. Christian, are elected in Christ and inherit the promises (Gal 3:13-16). God has called us and selected us to be one of his children. He expects us to walk in obedience to his commands and to serve him through serving the world. God intends for his people to be a disciplined people, holy priests and suffering servants to the world. Both Old and New Testament emphasize that God is forming a holy nation of servant priests. \nA disciplined people- The OT sketches God's efforts to form a holy people. Holy means to be separated unto God. God called Abraham to leave home and family to become a pilgrim people, and represent God's holiness to other nations of the world. They learned that righteousness of God demanded disciplined, obedient people. Israel was to be a parable and proclaimer of God's holiness.\nA Nation of Priests- At the beginning of the covenant with Israel, God promised that they would be a kingdom of priests. God has decreed that they shall be called "priests of the Lord" and "ministers of our God". Priest were the models to the whole nation who represented God to man and Also represented man to God, They were a holy priest nation to whom God would reveal that they might transmit the revelation to other nations\nA Servant People- the Lord of heaven and earth serves us and expects us to be servant people. We are to love like God, serve like God, and minister to all people like God. The servant passages of Israel clearly expresses the servant role of Israel and ultimately of Christ.	8	2020-11-04 21:24:05+05:30	2020-11-05 23:38:51+05:30	\N	585	718
2138	The incarnation- When God spoke at the beginning of time, the world was created, when God spoke by the Word in the incarnation, new humanity was created. The incarnation is the good news that the kingdom of heaven has come in Christ. Almost as startling as the incarnation was Jesus' becoming a servant. He served  man in the most menial ways. He fulfilled the intentions of the Father as stated in Isaiah. The graphic picture of his servant role is when Jesus took the towel and washed his disciples' feet. God, who became man, now stooped to serve man that man might catch the vision of serving. \n\nThe Crucifixion- The self emptying of Christ climaxed with Gethsemane and Golgotha. Philippians 2:8 moves directly from Christ's incarnation to his crucifixion: "Being found in fashion as a man, he humbled himself, and became obedient unto death, even death to the cross." Jesus completely surrendered to the Father that the struggle centered on knowing the Father's will. He fulfilled the priestly role by his prayers and by death, positioned himself between sinful man and the Holy God as intercessor and represented man to God as well as God to man. By His death, satisfied the Father, subdued Satan and reconciled man to God.\n\nThe Resurrection- Jesus' resurrection proved that the evil forces of satan had been conquered throughout the cosmos. In his death and resurrection, Jesus "spoiled principalities and powers, he made a shew of them openly" (Col 2:15). During these times the gospel is to be preached to all nations. Jesus gave the Great commission on basis of resurrection. The resurrection was the condition for world missions. The gospel all our lives may not realize what good news it is. \n\nThe incarnation tells the masses that God cares; the crucifixion proclaims forgiveness of sin; the resurrection shouts that the victory has been won over sin, death, and Satan.	8	2020-11-04 21:40:57+05:30	2020-11-05 23:38:51+05:30	\N	585	719
2139	The mission of Holy Spirit who came to take Jesus' place, to inspire, to empower, and to guide them. \nThe Inspirer of Missions- The mission of Christ could not be accomplished without the mission of the Holy Spirit. The Holy Spirit came to make God's people into the body of Christ. He regenerates, sanctifies, teaches, guides, comforts, illuminates, and intercedes. But the most important ministry in relation to his mission is the filling of Christians for service. The Great Commission the Holy Spirit who inspires men to carry it out spontaneously. When God's people are revived, they witness. Result is that sinners coming to Christ has not been an outpouring of the Holy Spirit. \n\nThe Empowerer of the Church- The power they received with the filling of the Holy Spirit was life changing and world changing because it was living, having authority spiritual power. Jesus used the word dunamis, from which we get the word dynamo and dynamite. Holy Spirit gives us the spiritual ability to make an impact on those hearing the testimony. The spirit filled believer never witnesses alone. The Holy Spirit works within the lost person to verify and to personalize the witness of the believer. He makes real the authority and the presence of Christ.\n\nThe Guide in Missions- As executor of Christ's will the Holy Spirit determines the course of missions. Holy Spirit thrust them farther into the fields. Acts 1:8 outlines the Spirit's movement: Jerusalem (Acts 2:! to 8:4); Judea and Samaria (Acts 8:5 to 12:25) and the remainder of the world. Holy Spirit redistributes the harvesters from an area where there are many laborers to other places to thrust them out into the world as flaming witnesses.	8	2020-11-04 21:55:15+05:30	2020-11-05 23:38:51+05:30	\N	585	720
2140	The church lives with two identities. First, it is the remnant that God has chosen, sanctified and commissioned to be blessing to the world. Second, it is the body of Christ that must fulfill his ministry in the world. \n\nPriest of God- The Calvary Principle\nAs God's chosen generation, we live in the election stream of God's great purpose. This part of the chapter explores what it means to be chosen to be priests. Formerly we were Christ less, homeless, promise less, hopeless and Godless (Eph. 2:12). Just as the veil in the Temple barred man from the holy of holies, so our sins had created a barrier that kept us separated from God and each other. Old Testament priests, as representatives of God, pronounced blessings on individual people; we bless all nations by who we are and what we do. The Old Testament priesthood was limited to the tribe of Levi; the New Testament priesthood includes all believers. We should be different in our thoughts, talks and walk. \n\nServants of God- The incarnation Principle\nThe church as the body of Christ is incarnate in the world to become the suffering servant. It is not called out of the world but called to go into the world and love like Christ, forgive like Christ, and give itself to the world like Christ. But a more common application of the incarnation principle is to empty ourselves of earthly power and self importance. Proclamation grows most naturally out of life of service. His incarnation best illustrates the servant attitude. \n\nSons of the King- The resurrection principle\nFrom the time God created the world, his intention was to rule over obedient persons. The first church faced almost insurmountable odds. Obstacles like persecution, deception, discord and prejudice that cause the church not to fulfill its co-mission with God. Satan unleashed his attacks in ever increasing force and cunning, but the early Christian would not be silent. As children of King, we announce that kingdom has come. With joy we tell the good news that our Father owns everything.	8	2020-11-04 22:13:06+05:30	2020-11-05 23:38:51+05:30	\N	585	721
