2223	First , His temptation taught him obedience:\nobedience:\nJesu was entirely sinless, he never disobeyed God. but he was also fully and truly human. so he had to learn God's righteous requirements and to overcome challenges and temptations throughout his life. Jesus obeyed God both by keeping the requirements of his law, and by submitting to the father's plan for his life. and this obedience prepared him for his work as Christ\nHebrews 5:8,9\nSympathy:\nJesus recognized that the things Satan offered him were desirable and his weakened condition from fasting must have amplified his longing for them. And this experience caused him to grow in compassion and understanding for us as we suffer and struggle with temptation in our own lives\nHebrews 4:15\nImpeccability:\nthe word impeccability means inability to sin.\nbut because of his human nature, Jesus experienced temptations from a human perspective. He recognized the value of the things offered to him, and keenly understood the benefits they could provide him. Jesus is impeccable, his obedience and sympathy are actually increased , since he learned perfectly from the experience and now responds to us in away that is perfectly suited to our needs.	8	2020-11-26 23:27:22+05:30	2020-11-29 11:24:58+05:30	\N	595	767
2224	Jesus Crucifixion was unique because it served as a atoning offering for sin. \nAs the Christ it was his responsibility to die on behalf if his people, Hebrews 9:11-21\nimputation: it simply means assignment or reckoning.  we are referring to the act in which God assigned the guilt of sinners to the person of Jesus.  but from a legal perspective God counted Jesus as if he had personally committed every sin imputed to him. Jesus offered himself on the cross as a substitute for his people. Bible often refers to him as a sacrifice Romans 3:25. He is also called our ransom in places like Matthew 20:28\nJudgement:\nJesus death was also a divine judgment against sin\nBefore God laid our guilt on him, Jesus couldn't die, but our sin was imputed to him on the cross, his death became not only punishable but necessary. It was the only just response God could make to such tremendous guilt.	8	2020-11-26 23:38:46+05:30	2020-11-29 11:24:58+05:30	\N	595	768
2225	God's anointed Christ conquered death for all of us, when he rose from the grave, through the power of the Spirit, he proved to all creation that he really is  God's favored son and the heir to his kingdom.\nhe ensured the future resurrection and blessing of all his faithful followers.\nPlan of redemption:\nGod's plan to redeem humanity and the rest of creation depended on him fulfilling his covenant promises to establish his kingdom on earth under the kingship of a descendant of David, otherwise known as the Christ. Jesus resurrection was a critical step that enabled God to fulfill his covenant promise . this is one of the reason that New Testament calls the resurrection  as an affirmation of Jesus' role as Christ. Luke 24:45,46\nBlessings of salvation:\nThe new testament associates Jesus resurrection as a wide variety of blessings, we receive as part of our salvation, it results in our justification, regeneration, opens door to our eternal inheritance, produces good works, and is a true witness to Christ in our bodies and lives. Its the source of the future bodily resurrection of believers.	9	2020-11-26 23:51:25+05:30	2020-11-29 11:24:58+05:30	\N	595	769
