2185	Paul tailored his letter to address the specific problems of a relatively localized group of people whom he knew directly or personally. But in Ephesians he did something different: he responded to the problems of several churches in different locations, many of which he had never met.  The purpose is divided into two:\n1. Kingdom of God- Christ's kingdom exists in the overlap of the ages: The present age- sin and death and the Age to come- God will pour out ultimate blessings and curses. Our divine King is using his power and authority: Citizenship, the most valuable and well-known citizenship in Paul's day was citizenship in the Roman Empire. Inheritance, Paul associated it with inheritance in Christ's kingdom; Military Service, Christians engage in spiritual warfare also implied citizenship in God's kingdom. Rule over Creation and Sources of our names were because we are a part of His Kingdom and An Ambassador. \n2. Challenges- A. Sinful Nature- Sin must not characterize the citizens of God's kingdom.\nB. Racial Tension- Paul used the imagery of the God's kingdom to address tension between Jews and Gentiles in the church. \nC. Demonic Forces- The church- God's kingdom- is in a cosmic battle with the kingdom of darkness, ruled by Satan and his demons.	8	2020-11-13 21:16:55+05:30	2020-11-16 21:18:17+05:30	\N	597	911
2186	Paul's job as an apostle was to provide authoritative teaching and leadership to the church, and he did this partly by writing letters. But Paul didn't just want to spread sound doctrine or record it for posterity. First and Foremost, he wanted to minister to the church in his own day by applying sound doctrine. His letter were pastoral and caring, and they directly to the problems that the church faced in the first century. \nThis means that as we study Paul's letter to the Ephesians, it helps to begin by asking questions like: To whom was this letter written? And what significant issues were they facing in life? Knowing the answers to the questions like these will help us make greater sense of Paul's teachings.	7	2020-11-13 21:20:58+05:30	2020-11-16 21:18:17+05:30	\N	597	912
2187	A section of praise to God appears next in 1:3-14. This is Paul's only canonical letter in which the salutation is followed by such a section of praise to God. Typically Paul followed his salutation with a personal reference or greeting. But as we have seen, there are no personal references of any type in the epistle to the Ephesians. We don't know for certain why Paul decided not to include any personal greetings. Maybe he thought that a section of praise would work better in a circular letter. or maybe he wanted to lay the groundwork for the doctrinal sections that followed. Some have seen this section as the beginning of a dialog of prayer that spans the first three chapters. Paul praised God for our inheritance in Christ. \nPrayer- The kingdom of God provides the context for Paul's prayer. He mentioned God's sovereignty when he spoke of: The Father's  "incomparably great power" and "mighty strength". Christ being enthroned above all other rulers and shares his inheritance with us, so that his inheritance is our inheritance too.	8	2020-11-13 21:29:32+05:30	2020-11-16 21:18:17+05:30	\N	597	913
2188	1. Citizenship- The body focuses on the contrast between the righteous kingdom of God and the sinful kingdom of demons and fallen humanity. \na. In Kingdom of Darkness, the human race is sinful and fallen. \nb. In Kingdom of Light, God sovereignly determined to deliver people so they would inherit salvation.\nc. In the nature of citizenship, God has fulfilled the Old Testament ideal of combining both Jews and Gentiles into one kingdom under God's sovereign rule. \n2. Administration- Just before his arrest in Jerusalem, Paul had warned the Ephesians elders that false teachers would arise from their own ranks. The apostles possessed: special grace from God that empowered them in their ministry, special revelation from God that taught them infallible truth. \n3. Code for Living- \nA. Ecclesiastical Order in the Kingdom- Paul portrayed the Lord as a victorious king returning from the battle.\nB. Purifying the Kingdom- Believers within the kingdom of light have a new nature that they can rely on to overcome their sin. \nC. Domestic Order in the Kingdom- Proper relationships of authority must be maintained within all levels of Christ's kingdom.\nD. Warfare of the Kingdom (6:10-20)\nE. Final Greetings- Paul offered a closing blessings and indicated that Tychicus would deliver this letter	8	2020-11-13 21:41:24+05:30	2020-11-16 21:18:17+05:30	\N	597	914
2189	God requires that we help him expand and grow his earthly kingdom. The kingdom of God is a building, with each Christian being a stone in the structure. The goal of the building is to become God's dwelling, so that God will live in the midst of his people. When Paul taught that both Jews and Gentiles would live in God's presence as his Temple, he meant that God's kingdom was moving toward its final goal. The big picture: Honoring God, Living in his presence, Striving to increase Christ's glory rather than our own. We must be humble- no believer is more deserving of blessing than any other. We must embrace all believers as equals in the Kingdom of God. All believers collectively composing Christ's body. Like the metaphor of the Temple, the image of the body described the kingdom of God: Christ was seated as king in heaven. He ruled for the benefit of his people, the church. Paul's fullest use of the body imagery appears in Ephesians 4:1-6, where he argued for ecclesiastical order in the kingdom. These leaders are to guide the church toward two goals: Unity in the faith, Attaining to the whole measure of the fullness of Christ- to bring all of creation under the rule of Christ.	8	2020-11-13 21:50:45+05:30	2020-11-16 21:18:17+05:30	\N	597	915
2190	In the Bible, the gospel is cosmic in scope. It is the message that our divine King is using His power and authority to subject his enemies and to conquer sin, to redeem his people from their bondage and to establish them as rulers over the new earth. John The Baptist, Jesus and his disciples have all preached about the nearing Kingdom of God. "The time is fulfilled, and the Kingdom of God is at hand (Mark 1.15)." The same gospel is being preached today also. In our understanding, people most often associate the "gospel" or  "good news" with things like the forgiveness of an individual's sins and the promise of an individual's everlasting life. And these are the wonderful aspects of our hope.	8	2020-11-13 22:04:15+05:30	2020-11-16 21:18:17+05:30	\N	597	916
2191	1. Philemon- Paul's partner in gospel ministry and the host of the local church. He seems to have had a significant history with Paul that formed a strong bond between the men. Paul meant that he had brought Philemon to faith, and owed Paul a great debt. But they knew each other very well. \n\n2. Onesimus- Onesimus was Philemon's slave, he was a member of Philemon's household. But unlike his master, Onesimus was not a believer, at least not initially. But after Onesimus left Philemon's household the apostle led him to faith in Christ and grew to love him greatly. Paul expressed his love for Onesimus, in Philemon 10-16 and because he had developed a fatherly love for him. \n\n3. Witness- Paul mentioned a number of other Colossians in his letter to Philemon: Apphia- as Paul's "sister" might simply indicate that she was a believer. It is more likely that she was a member of Philemon's household- probably his wife. Archippus, may have been the host of the local church met in Philemon's house. He was mentioned as person of some influence over Philemon, whether as a local pastor or a part of Philemon's household. Epaphras, Paul expected them to serve as familiar witnesses in his appeal to Philemon on Onesimus' behalf. He had a keen interest in making sure that Philemon would respond properly to Paul's letter.	8	2020-11-15 21:11:14+05:30	2020-11-16 21:18:42+05:30	\N	598	917
2192	"Onesimus" is derived from a Greek word meaning "useful" or "profitable." But Onesimus had been a useless or unprofitable slave. The initial problem in Philemon's household was that Onesimus had caused some loss to Philemon, whether intentionally or unintentionally, through negligence, sloth or malice. And this problem was compounded by the resulting tension between Onesimus and Philemon, including probably Philemon's anger and intent to discipline Onesimus, and Onesimus's fear. And finally, it culminated in Onesimus' flight from Philemon. Philemon may have assumed that Onesimus was a fugitive. But Onesimus' true motives remained to be seen.\nUnder Roman law, a master had the right to punish slaves severely, even with heavy beatings. Onesimus fled in fear. God had a positive reason for allowing the situation. The law permitted slaves to leave their masters temporarily to find an advocate or mediator. If Onesimus fled in order to ask Paul to be his advocate and mediator with Philemon, he was not a fugitive.	8	2020-11-15 21:18:32+05:30	2020-11-16 21:18:42+05:30	\N	598	918
2193	1. Onesimus' Petition- Scripture provides some details suggesting that Onesimus sought out Paul as his advocate. He knew where Paul was- the church at Colosse had sponsored Epaphras' mission to care for Paul in prison. He pursued  a meeting with Paul and pursued Paul's advocacy. Paul wrote to Philemon only after Onesimus had endeared himself to Paul: Paul converted Onesimus to Christianity. He ministered to Paul in prison. Some scholars conclude that Onesimus was seeking to start a new life far from Philemon, and encountered Paul only accidentally. \n2. Paul's Agreement- Philemon had the right to punish Onesimus. So, Paul defended Onesimus on the basis of mercy. Godly authority figures carry out appropriate punishments because it is the right thing to do. Onesimus initially stayed with Paul to convince the apostle of his good intent. During this time: Paul preached the gospel to Onesimus. The Holy Spirit brought Onesimus to faith in Christ. He might have remained with Paul without becoming a fugitive. Morally, this would not have been the best solution. The Christian values of charity and reconciliation demanded his return to Philemon.	8	2020-11-15 21:26:48+05:30	2020-11-16 21:18:42+05:30	\N	598	919
2194	Paul presented his petition to Philemon in verses 8-21. The petition represents the main purpose of the letter, namely advocating on behalf of Onesimus before Philemon. Paul had the authority to order Philemon to do the right thing. Instead, he wrote to Philemon in ways that elicited Philemon's sympathy and concern. Paul spoke as a weak, elderly man in need of help. \nPaul wanted Philemon to respond to this situation with genuine Christian love. Compassion for an elderly man in prison. Compassion for the newly converted brother in Christ who ministered to him, We will explore the petition in some depth, breaking it down into the following six elements:\nAn explanation of Paul's role as advocate in verses 8-10\nAn explanation of Onesimus's role as petitioner in verses 11-12\nAn explanation of Philemon's role as master in verse 14\nAn explanation of God's role as providential ruler of the universe in verses 15-16\nPaul's petition itself in verses 17-20\n And Paul's statement of confidence that the petitions would be granted, in verse 21.	9	2020-11-15 21:33:15+05:30	2020-11-16 21:18:42+05:30	\N	598	920
2195	Paul said in Philemon 11-13, that Onesimus who was unprofitable in the past, but is now profitable to you and me, whom Paul has retained and in Philemon's absence, ministered him in the bonds of gospel. Paul appealed on the basis of mercy, because Onesimus had sinned and was guilty, hence Paul with mercy appealed for him. It is easier and better to appeal for mercy, as we all have sinned and have no right to make demands. God is extremely merciful and gracious to us. And in verse 12, "He is very dear to me but even dearer to you, both as a man and as a brother in the Lord." Paul's high praise of Philemon as a deeply loving Christian, as well as Paul's strong advocacy for Onesimus, it appears that Paul expected this to be the outcome.	8	2020-11-15 21:50:07+05:30	2020-11-16 21:18:42+05:30	\N	598	921
2196	Paul had planted the church in Philippi during his second missionary journey, around the year A.D 48 or 50. Many of Paul's activities in Philippi are recorded in Acts 16:12-40, it was in Philippi that Paul gained his first convert in Europe, the merchant woman, Lydia. And it was in Philippi that he was jailed for an exorcism he performed on a slave girl. He led the Philippian jailer to a profession of faith in Christ. The church was poor and not always able to help Paul financially. But, when they had opportunity, they gave to him generously. Paul referred to the Philippians as his "dear friends" (2:12, 4:1).	9	2020-11-17 21:04:40+05:30	2020-11-29 11:21:48+05:30	\N	599	922
2197	We approach Paul's letters to the Philippians, we have questions like: Who were the Philippians? What was happening in their lives and in Paul's life? And why did Paul write to them? The answers to questions like these will help us to understand Paul's authoritative teaching in this letter, and to apply it to our own lives. \nAs we investigate the background to Paul's epistle to the Philippians, we will focus our attention on three matters: First, we will consider Paul's relationship with the Philippians. Second, we will mention some details pf Paul's suffering in prison and Third, we will explore the conditions in Philippi at the time Paul wrote to them.\nRelationship- Paul had planted the church in Philippi during his second missionary journey, around the year AD 49 or 50. The church was poor and not always able to help Paul financially. But when they had opportunity, they gave to him generously. \nSuffering in Prison- Paul often suffered greatly. He did not always bear up well under these hardships. At times, he was depressed, even despairing. He spoke frequently of death as welcome relief from his suffering. \nConcern for Paul- As soon as the Philippians were able, they demonstrated their concern by sending: A gift to meet Paul's earthly needs. Epaphroditus to deliver the gift and minister to Paul.	9	2020-11-17 21:13:50+05:30	2020-11-29 11:21:48+05:30	\N	599	923
2198	Paul instructed the Philippians to remain faithful to Christ and to live exemplary lives even in midst of distressing circumstances. \na. Importance of Perseverance:- God had planned the Philippians' sufferings as a means of blessing them. Paul wanted the Philippians to rejoice in the midst of suffering because of the blessings it produces.\nb. Help for Perseverance:- It is much easier to endure suffering when we have real people helping us on a daily basis, and suffering right alongside us. Paul sent Epaphroditus back to them in order to ease their minds as well as to minister to them, planned to send Timothy to Philippi. Paul hoped that he would be released from prison, and would come to minister to the Philippians. \nc. Examples of Perseverance:- Paul was a positive example of perseverance in the faith: Mindset, Behavior Paul refused to rely on his earthly merits. He depended only on Christ's merit, which God credited to him by means of faith.\nd. Challenges to Perseverance- Paul encouraged the Philippians not to falter in their faithfulness to God because of: False teachers, Conflict within the church and Personal hardship.	9	2020-11-17 21:23:22+05:30	2020-11-29 11:21:48+05:30	\N	599	924
2924	Universal Covenants = between God and all humanity. Adam - the covenant of foundations, Noah - the covenant of stability. \n\nNational Covenants = between God and Israel. Abraham - covenant of promise, Moses - covenant of law and David - covenant of kingship. \n\nNew Covenants = the last major covenant mentioned in the Old Testament. The covenant of fulfillment: first coming of Christ, spread of faith, and Jesus' return. \n\nThe six major divine covenants provide us with ways of understanding the different emphases of major historical epochs in the Old Testament.	0	2023-05-04 06:08:07+05:30	2023-05-04 06:08:07+05:30	\N	800	667
2199	A. Salutation- Paul is the primary author of the letter. The letter also comes from Timothy. Philippians is Paul's only letter that never calls explicit attention to the apostolic authority. \nB. Thanksgiving- Paul presents a fairly standard statement of thanks, speaking of; the joy the Philippians have brought to him. His expectations for their ultimate salvation. \nC. Prayer- Paul's prayer is packed with statements that reflect the emphasis of the entire letter: Make proper judgements, perform good works, persevere in faith and practice, bring glory and praise to God. \nD. Body- Paul let the Philippians know how much he loved them and how thankful he was for their friendship and ministry included Paul's perseverance: a. Present Ministry, 1:12-18- Paul persevered by finding reasons to be joyful despite his suffering. \nb. Future Deliverance, 1:18-21- He focused on the possibility that he might eventually be released from prison.\nc. Future Ministry, 1:22-26- He looked to the possibility of a future ministry to the Philippians as source of joy. \nE. Final Greetings- The mention of believers within Caesar's household demonstrates that Paul's imprisonment had not hindered his gospel ministry.	9	2020-11-17 21:31:43+05:30	2020-11-29 11:21:48+05:30	\N	599	925
