2394	The writer , the document and the audience are interdependent guide s to meaning, if we over emphasize the author, we often fall in to a intentional fallacy. it relies too heavily on what we think we know about a writer and his intentions and de-emphasizes the thinks we learn about the documents and audience. we might make unwanted assumptions about the author's intentions by speculating about what he was thinking when he wrote.\nA second type of mistake  we make is over emphasizing the document. this kind of error , is often called a graphic fallacy. the term graphic , comes from the Greek word, graphe which means writing. graphic fallacy overemphasizes the document itself.  we might commit this fallacy by thinking that we can sufficiently grasp the meaning of a passage  merely by analyzing its vocabulary, grammar and stricture without regard to its author or original audience\na third type of interpretive mistake we make is over emphasizing the audience. its called an affective fallacy, because its focus too heavily on how scripture effects its audience. the fallacy is often committed when an interpreter speculates too much about the mind set of the original audience and fails to pay enough attention to the writer and the document.\n we should access to resource that help us understand all its features. our information should not be limited, for our interpretations to be more generalized.	9	2021-02-02 23:58:02+05:30	2021-02-03 03:17:08+05:30	\N	635	690
