2409	The word 'Synchronic' derived from two Greek words 'sun' and 'chronos' which means ' together with' and 'time' respectively. So the meaning can be derived as 'together in time or ' at the same time'. The term ' synchronic' is used to indicate how biblical theologians often explore sets of events in Old Testament history that happened in time. \nAs a movie is divided in to different scenes which tell a portion of the large story; the old Testament history is approached with similar approach. \nin Synchronic synthesis, biblical theologians concentrate their attention on particular periods of time in the OT as if they were scenes in a movie rather than on the flow of its entire history. Biblical theologians when they apply synchronic approach some times, they focus on relatively short time frames and other time on long term periods of history. For example some times, the sacrifice of Issac is dealt seperately from Abraham's story; other time his life as a whole story. Some times biblical theologians treat the whole OT as a synchronic unit, to know what God did and said in the OT period.	8	2021-02-08 15:11:17+05:30	2021-03-10 21:28:04+05:30	\N	640	660
2410	'Synthesis' simply means 'combining different components of some thing in to a whole'. In this approach, we describe the ways different components of theology revealed in a particular period of history fit together in a coherent, logical structure. \nBy the middle of the 20th century critical theologians began to deny the logical character of the OT. Based on a number of linguistic and cultural assessments they argued that the Greeks focused on abstraction and logical order, much like systematic theology.  And by contrast they suggested that the Hebrew mind set looked everything in terms of historic dynamics. From this point of view, the OT did not focus on logical systems or on theological relationships between beliefs.\nBut in contrast to the denial, the logical character of OT is affirmed because of two reasons. Primarily, recent studies have largely discredited the kinds of contrast between Greek and Hebrew minds; but concluded they were very similar to each other. Secondly, the OT theology displays substantial concern for logic and rational thought. \nThough the OT theology does not employ the standards of formal western philosophical traditions; God's revelation in scripture were not random, disconnected or contradictory.	9	2021-02-08 15:23:57+05:30	2021-03-10 21:28:04+05:30	\N	640	661
2411	The poets of the OT were interested in two different worlds that tell us about history. First they gave attention to 'the world' they wrote about. In the first place poetry often opened windows to the past as they provided objective facts about God's acts and words. Eg: Song of Moses and Miriam in Exodus 15. \nSecondly OT poetry provided windows to contemporary historical information from the writer's own time. Eg: Psalms 1:1- Meditation of the law. \nThirdly OT poets turned their readers attention towards the future. Isaiah 40:1-11, return of exiles. In one way or other the OT poetry often conveyed information about the revelatory acts and words of God in the past, present and future. \nLike poets, the authors of narratives also stood between two worlds. On the one side, they wrote about world that was the subject of their 'text' or 'the world'. Narrative, largely focus on the past, only rarely mention the present or the future. \nOn the other side the authors of the narratives also dealt with their ' world', the world in which their readers lived.  They wanted their readers to think, act and feel in certain ways within their own worlds in the light of past events. \nThe OT narratives were doxological, theological, poetical, polemical, moral and motivational. They were didactic, designed to teach early readers about their lives.	8	2021-02-08 15:37:10+05:30	2021-03-10 21:28:04+05:30	\N	640	662
2412	Since the enlightenment in the 17th century, historians expect OT narratives to be like modern journalistic historic writings. Three modern standards that are often mistakenly applied to OT narratives are : primarily, historical accounts should be comprehensive. But the OT authors felt no compulsion to include every significant fact because it was written with a didactic purpose. Though they did not meet the criterion of comprehensiveness in good history writing; their narratives are true and authoritative records of the past. \nSecondly, the OT authors were only as precise as their didactic purpose required. They were only precise as they needed to reach their didactic goals. The lack of modern precision does not mean that the accounts are untrue. \nThirdly the OT narratives are not objective by modern standards. God inspired OT authors to shape the opinions of their readers. This goal influenced what they omitted, what they included and how they described them. \nThe OT narratives were not designed to meet modern standards of history writing. They only present fully reliable historical information that will enable us to construct synchronic synthesis of OT theology.	8	2021-02-08 15:49:07+05:30	2021-03-10 21:28:04+05:30	\N	640	663
