2472	The Term ' Diachronic' derives from two Greek words; 'dia' means ' through' or ' through out' and 'Chronos' which means ' time'. Diachrony is a concern with the passage of time. In biblical theology, the term 'diachronic' points to the ways Old Testament theology transformed, changed or developed through time.\nDiachronic approaches to the Bible concentrate on how theological structures progressively unfolded through time. This is in contrast with the synchronic synthesis. Though these approaches differs, they are also interdependent. So biblical theologians move back and forth between synchronic and diachronic work in variety of ways. Both these approaches are necessary to understand the theology of the Old Testament properly.	8	2021-03-10 15:08:25+05:30	2021-03-10 21:32:37+05:30	\N	652	665
2473	We use the term development to suggest two main ideas. First, changes in Old Testament theological structure always move toward fulfilling God's purposes of history. Secondly, we speak of development because theological changes only occur through God's providential involvement in history.\nDivine purposes; The scripture affirms that everything in history including shifts in theology, always accomplish God's unfailing purposes for his creation Isaiah 46:10. It fulfills the long term and short term goals of God in history. And ultimately it leads to God's glory and establishment of his Kingdom. \nDivine providence: Though the Old Testament theological development often appears like a road seen from distance, actually on a close look, the road of the Old Testament is filled with steep inclines, fast descents, and sharp turns to the left and right. These abrupt shifts are caused by God's providence, his complex involvement with his creatures.	8	2021-03-10 15:17:19+05:30	2021-03-10 21:32:37+05:30	\N	652	666
2474	The covenants with Adam and Noah known as universal because they were made between God and all humanity. The theological structure of the covenants tell us much about the relationship between God and all people. \nFollowing the universal covenants, God established national covenants with special people Israel: the covenants with Abraham, Moses and David. God narrowed down his covenantal attention to just one ethnic group establishing Israel to lead the rest of humanity in service to God.\nThe people of Israel were promised by the prophet about a New and final covenant God would make with his people Jeremiah 31:31. This covenant is also known as the covenant of fulfilment. The New covenant charectarises every particular theological structure from Christ's first coming to his glorious return. \n We see the six major divine covenants so permeated the theology of the times in which they were revealed that they provide us with ways of understanding the different emphases of major historic epoch in the Old Testament. Adam's covenant introduces the age of foundations; Noah's covenant began an age of natural stability; Abraham's covenant established promises for Israel; Moses' covenant introduced God's law; David's covenant emphasized Kingship and the New covenant brought all of these earlier covenants to their ultimate fulfillment.	8	2021-03-10 15:32:28+05:30	2021-03-10 21:32:37+05:30	\N	652	667
2475	In a broad sense biblical theology applies the term' Typology' to any diachronic development of topics in OT theology. In a narrow sense, we can summarize the meaning of typology as follows: ' Biblical typology is the study of diachronic developments between theological structures closely associated with significant persons, institutions and events in scripture'.\nIn simple language typology is the study of types in three important passages NT writers spoke of particular theological structures in the OT as Types of the theological structures of the NT faith. \nI Romans 5:14, Paul declared Adam as ' a pattern of the one to come'. Paul observed that Adam was a type of Christ. \nIn 1 Peter 3:20-21, Christian baptism is presented as a NT counterpart to Noah's flood. Colossians 2:17 speaks of OT ceremonial law as a shadow of the things to come, which is fulfilled in Christ.	8	2021-03-10 15:41:40+05:30	2021-03-10 21:32:37+05:30	\N	652	668
