2478	In the development of OT theology, each time God reveals himself acting or speaking in history, to one degree or another, his new revelations reconfigured existing theological structure. \nAs NT history moved forward, theological structures underwent diachronic changes. But because diachronic changes in NT history were not as dramatic as changes in the OT. NT biblical theologians have shifted their attention in a new direction. Instead of focusing on different historical periods, they have tended to treat the entire period of NT history as a whole. The theological unity created by the NT brief history, uniform circumstances and single covenant makes it difficult to do extensive diachronic study. So instead of dividing the history of God's NT act and word revelations in to small segments, they have focused on the ways, different NT authors provided different perspectives on the entire period.	7	2021-03-11 15:18:33+05:30	2021-05-11 04:27:50+05:30	\N	654	671
2479	The OT eschatology also developed in significant ways over time. These diachronic developments in the OT set the stage for what the NT biblical theologians have discovered about eschatology in the New Testament as well. \nOld Testament eschatology began in seminal form in the days of Adam and was refined further in the days of Noah. In Abraham's time, Israel's blessings to the world became the means by which God would bring history to its ultimate end.  Moses connected this hope with Israel's glorious return from  exile. The covenant with David set his dynasty and Jerusalem at the center of these glorious last days after exile. And also there was a brief period of hope, Israel's continuing rebellion caused the OT to end with the hope of the eschaton postponed to the distant future. Upon these background developed the New Testament eschatology.	7	2021-03-11 15:28:11+05:30	2021-05-11 04:27:50+05:30	\N	654	672
2480	Biblical theologians have demonstrated that in one way or another every NT teaching was shaped by Jesus' three phase view of last days. \nFirst, the NT authors spoke of the entire period as the last days. Hebrews 1:1-2. Second, NT writers designated the extended period of Church history as the last days. 2 Timothy 3:1-5. Third, the NT authors described the consummation of the Kingdom at Christ's return as the ' last days'. John 6:39.\nThe NT writers believed that all revelations from God from time to time of Jesus' earthly ministry until his return in glory took place in the last days. Jewish religious leaders and general Jewish population raged against Christianity precisely because of the Christian outlook on the messianic last days. Christians believed that Messiah had already come, but in a way that unexpected. Such a messianic scenario was utterly contrary to what Jews believed in that day. This brought a break between first century Judaism and Christianity. And for these reasons NT authors were deeply pre occupied with Jesus' three phase eschatology.	8	2021-03-11 15:38:54+05:30	2021-05-11 04:27:50+05:30	\N	654	673
