2487	It was once believed that the Apostles' Creed was written by the twelve Apostles in the first Century. But there is no evidence. In the writings of the early Christians there are many lists of essential doctrines that resembles the Apostles' creed. The writings of the Church fathers like Origen and Irenius indicated the existence of written rules of faith; to preserve the faith. They included the affirmation of important doctrines, as well as moral teachings and traditions. \nSome ancient doctrinal lists are in the form of creed which were often recited in liturgical settings. In early centuries each congregation had its creed, summarising essential biblical truth. By the third or fourth century, some creeds rise to prominence and being used in multiple churches. \nThe Roman creed was one among the prominent. This creed is so similar to the Apostles' creed. Many Scholars believe Apostles' creed as a later version of Roman creed. \nRegardless of its origin, the churches especially wester churches began widely using it. In the early centuries its wordings varied from Church to church. But in the eighth century the wordings was generally standardized in to the form that we recognize and use today.	8	2021-03-15 15:13:53+05:30	2021-05-11 04:27:07+05:30	\N	655	866
