2673	the pillars of Jewish faith- complete submission to the Scriptures and the hope in the Messiah were essential dimensions of Paul's Christian outlooks. \nPaul lived not only in Jewish Palestine, but at different times in his life he lived in the gentile world as well. according to acts 21:39, Paul came from the gentile city of Tarsus in Cilicia. in Acts 21:39Acts 9:30. 11:25 indicate that Paul again lived in Tarsus as an adult. Paul's contact with the gentile world was enhanced by the fact that he enjoyed full Roman citizenship. Acts 22:28 we see that Paul hadn't purchased his citizenship but had been born into it. Paul's awareness of Gentile culture did not lead Paul to alter Christianity to make it acceptable to Gentiles. He remained distinctly Jewish in his basic orientation. Paul's contact with the Gentile world affected him in at least two ways. on the one hand, it equipped him to minister to Gentiles outside the church. Better than many , he knew the values and beliefs of Gentiles and was well prepared to bring the gospel to them in effective ways. Romans 11:13, Paul called himself "the apostle to the Gentiles. Paul was also prepared to minister to Gentiles within the church and even to fight for them .Paul played an important role in convincing the apostles and elders that Gentile converts did not need to be circumcised. Paul insisted that Christ had destroyed the wall between the Jews and Gentiles. A central theme in many of Paul's epistles was that Jesus had flung open the door of salvation to the gentiles nations so that any gentiles who was in Christ was counted as a full blooded Jew and perfect law keeper n God's eyes.	9	2021-09-13 23:33:23+05:30	2021-09-20 18:20:48+05:30	\N	705	698
