2932	Biblical theology is theological reflection drawn from the historical analysis of God reported in Scripture. To understand what is meant by 'historical analysis' a few broad perspectives should be examined. First of which is Literary analysis. Literary analysis is a literary portrait designed to influence readers in particular ways. Next is historical analysis. Historical analysis is a window to history, exploring historical events lying behind the Bible. Then there is thematic analysis, which is a mirror that reflects our interests and questions. Biblical theology approaches the Scriptures primarily with historical analysis. \n\nHistorical analysis looks at the Bible as a window that gives access to history. When the focus of exegesis shifts from traditional theological themes to the historical events described in the Bible, a very different set of priorities and concerns emerge. While sound biblical theology does not contradict sound systematic theology, it nevertheless leads to significantly different theological perspectives. Historical analysis is primarily concerned with the acts of God. The Bible reports many different kinds of historical events, but biblical theology primarily asks, "what do the Scriptures say that God has done?".	7	2023-05-10 05:58:33+05:30	2023-05-10 07:49:10+05:30	\N	802	655
2933	Theological tendencies of factual historical analysis and theological historical analysis go hand in hand as well as have some differentiators. First there is factual historical analysis, which is how the events in Scripture fit within the larger environment of the ancient Near East. Then there is the theological historical analysis which Biblical theologians are interested in the theological significance of the acts of God reported in the Scripture. A theological matter is anything that; refers directly to God (theology proper) and describes other subjects in relation to God. \n\nThe goal of factual historical analysis is rather straightforward. It is to establish a reliable account of the facts of history by combining what we learn from Scripture with the data we gather from extra-biblical sources. As important as such factual concerns may be, biblical theology is more concerned with theological historical analysis. Biblical theologians are more interested in the theological significance of the acts of God reported in Scripture.	7	2023-05-10 06:05:54+05:30	2023-05-10 07:49:10+05:30	\N	802	656
2934	The cultural changes that took place in the 17th century that led to the formal discipline of biblical theology can be traced back to the Enlightenment of the 17th century. Biblical theology is a Christian response to modern historicism: the belief that history hold the key for understanding ourselves and the world around us. Christians have always explored the acts of God reported in Scripture. But in the modern period, significant cultural shifts took place that led theologians to emphasize these historical interests as never before. Simply put, biblical theology is a Christian response to a prominent intellectual movement in the modern period, often called modern historicism. \n\nOne of the most well-known Enlightenment figures who expressed this cultural shift was the German philosopher Greg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel, who lived from 1770 to 1831. Hegel is best known for his proposal that every aspect of reality is caught up in logical patters of historical progress known as the dialectic. The entire universe, he thought was so ordered by God that it followed a divinely ordained historical logic. From his point of view, we understand every item in the world best when we see it in the light o f this rational patter of history. \n\nAs a result of the cultural changes, historicism rose to prominence for many reasons; including archeology, geology, biology, and nearly every academic discipline. Historicism has had countless effect on modern Christian theology, particularly biblical theology. Theologians have either embraced historicism in ways that compromised essential Christian beliefs or incorporated historicism in ways that upheld and enhanced the Christian faith.	8	2023-05-10 06:15:15+05:30	2023-05-10 07:49:10+05:30	\N	802	657
2935	Modern Christians have come to approach the Scriptures through biblical theology due to the effects of historicism. Theologians have either: embraced historicism in ways that compromised essential Christian beliefs; or incorporated historicism in ways that upheld and enhanced the Christian faith. There have been many Christians in many branches of the church throughout the world who have not followed the critical rejection of biblical authority. These evangelicals continue to hold that the Scriptures are true in all that they claim, including what they claim about history. Despite these unwavering commitments to biblical authority, modern historicism has had significant effects even on the ways that evangelicals approach the Scriptures. \n\nAccording to Charles Hodge, he believed in the authority of the Scripture and his commitment to biblical authority led him to teach that Christians are obligated to base systematic theology on the findings of biblical theology. Hodge insisted that systematic theology must submit to all the discoveries biblical theology made in Scripture by putting them into logical order.	7	2023-05-10 06:24:31+05:30	2023-05-10 07:49:10+05:30	\N	802	658
2941	The understanding that the biblical writers were only as precise as required by the didactic purposes help us overcome challenges to the truthfulness of Scripture by understanding that the no biblical passages were written in a theological vacuum. The authors would write their texts within a context of beliefs and theological structures. We must be able to grasp more fully the coherent, synthetic structurers of the historical period knowing that God may have given other special revelations that we do not have a record of. There are a variety of levels in spectrum as to simple structures versus very elaborate structures of theological structures within the Bible. \n\nSince  there are a variety of levels: basic level structures, middle level structures, and complex level structures there will always be a range of simple to elaborate understanding in how we overcome challenges to the truthfulness of the Scripture. As a reader continues their journey within the Scripture they are able to reflect on theological structures on many different levels. It is crucial to note that every moment of the Old Testament history was governed by logical covenant structures. These covenants help us make sense of God's particular act and word revelations.	8	2023-05-13 06:38:53+05:30	2023-05-24 18:35:35+05:30	\N	803	664
2936	We speak of biblical history as the process by which God will be ultimately glorified by extending his kingdom to the ends of the earth because at each stage of biblical history, God had specific purposes or goals that guided his act and word revelation. Biblical theologians spend much of their time delineating these diverse goals. But at the same time, in Romans 11:36, the apostle Paul pointed to the ultimate goal of history: For from God and through him and to him are all things. To him be the glory forever, Amen. As Paul put it here, all things are from God in the beginning. All things continue their existence now through the sustaining power of God. And all things are 'to him' that is, they are for God's glory and praise. In a word, God so orders the history of his creation that it will ultimately bring him immeasurable glory. \n\nGod will be ultimately glorified before every creature by extending his kingdom to the ends of the earth. As Jesus taught us to pray in Mathew 6:10, 'Your kingdom come, your will be done, on earth as it is in heaven'. The divine goal of all world history is the extension of God's perfectly heavenly reign to every corner of the earth. When God's will is done as perfectly on earth as it is in heaven, every creature will bow before God and honor him as the divine king, the supreme creator of all. At that time, the ultimate goal of history will be fulfilled. The Scriptures trace how certain historical events are crucial to reaching the goal of spreading God's kingdom throughout the world.	7	2023-05-10 06:34:35+05:30	2023-05-10 07:49:10+05:30	\N	802	659
2937	The term 'synchronic' is defined as the occurrences that had taken place together in time or around the same time. The actual origin of the word synchronic is derived from two Greek words, 'sun' which means 'with' and the noun 'chronos' that means 'time'. When the term synchronic is referred to within biblical theology it refers to the occurrences that took place either 'together in time' or 'around the same time'. The author of the text refers to the example of how the Old Testament has a similar approach when referring to a synchronic study to that of how a movie may have one overall story or theme, but in essence each scene will display or portray a specific story or moment of the overall movie. \n\nWith the synchronic approach illustrated within the Old Testament and within biblical theology, specific approaches are focused on within historical moments. They may vary in length of timeframe however time passes through each of these moments and allows theologians to have a relative perspective on the moments portrayed. Theologians at times actually refer to the Old Testament as a synchronic unit, and they concentrate on the theological perspectives that are established by the end of time.	8	2023-05-13 05:58:40+05:30	2023-05-24 18:35:35+05:30	\N	803	660
2938	The term 'synthesis' is defined as combining different components of something into a whole. The actual concept is rather difficult to comprehend, although we typically use it daily. A common example is going to work one day, and being in several meetings, and coming across your colleagues and listening to several different dialogues in the background. When coming home and sharing your day with your spouse, it will be nearly impossible to reiterate each conversation and theme, however in this instance you would synthesize and provide a recap of the day holistically. \n\nOne of the common denials of the Old Testament's logical character was that of applying logic within each discipline. For example, theologians would believe that logic was a primary feature of what they would refer to as the 'Greek' mindset, but it was reasonably foreign to the 'Hebrew' mindset. The argument would then focus on basis that Greeks would focus on abstraction and logical order which is much like systematic theology. However, we affirm it's logical coherence by looking at a few relevant points. The first being that studies have shown that the contrasts between Greek and Hebrew mindsets were discredited. Although their mindsets were different in many ways, they were also very much similar to one another. Second, while many things in the Old Testament will remain mysterious to humans as God's thoughts are much beyond ours, the logical thinking is only a question of how it did versus if Old Testament theology involved logic.	8	2023-05-13 06:08:48+05:30	2023-05-24 18:35:35+05:30	\N	803	661
2939	The two worlds of interpretation for both poetic and narrative literature includes 'that world' and 'their world'. That world poetry commonly is referred to poetry that gave attention to the world that they wrote about. In writing that world poetry, objective facts about God's acts and words were provided. Another way that Old Testament poets focused on was the actual world of their readers, which is referred to as their world. The poets would work on designing their poetry to influence the lives of their beloved readers. \n\nIn a narrative sense, that world poetry would open windows to the readers in reference to the past, present, and future. When referring to their world In a narrative sense, their world poetry can be seen in Exodus 15 when readers where encouraged to move forward with confidence to the Promised Land. The text was designed to place influence on the reader to particular ways. When reading Old Testament poetry, it should not be read the same way you read pose, instead you should recognize the unusual vocabulary and figures of speech along with its emotional impact.	7	2023-05-13 06:19:37+05:30	2023-05-24 18:35:35+05:30	\N	803	662
2940	When discerning historical information from the Old Testament narratives, it is important not to expect them to be like modern journalistic historical writing as it contains unusual vocabulary and syntax, it's figures of speech, imaginative concerns, and it's emotional impacts. It is important to note that within historical accounts the historical records will include every significant fact about a situation to give a balanced account. Unlike modern journalistic historical writing, all subjective evaluation that may prejudice readers will be avoided. Another important fact to point in differentiating how they will be different from modern journalistic historical writing is that they will either report details with exact precision or at least acknowledge that they haven't. \n\nIn modern journalistic historical writing, it is very common practice to omit the actual facts and report that all facts have been included. Or, it is also notable that the writer will be bias and when reporting out the summary they will persuade the reader to be influenced in one direction of another. This will create a completely false narrative and drive a pre-meditated approach towards the reader. This will then create propogandas and religious fantasies.	8	2023-05-13 06:27:24+05:30	2023-05-24 18:35:35+05:30	\N	803	663
2961	God's authority over creation as absolute, exclusive and exhaustive is described by his unlimited power. God has unlimited, omnipotence, and matchless power when it comes to doing anything he will do at anytime. He can create the universe while choosing to destroy everything that he has created. He can control the weather, he is able to rule and control human governments, defeat his enemies. He has and continues to save his people while performing mighty miracles. He is the Father and has the ultimate and unlimited authority and absolute power to do as he wishes. When we refer to God's authority being absolute, we can see within Scripture how God chose to create the heavens and the earth, how he chose to create man and woman, and how he also chose to wipe out the creation that he had made. This reveals his absolute reign as being the King and freedom to whatever he wishes to do with his creation. \n\nWhen we refer to the exclusive authority belonging to God, we acknowledge that the final word and authority  belongs only to the one that created everything, God. If God chose to undo or wipe out the world, he is the only one who can do so, because he is the creator of the  universe and without his authority nothing can come to be. While man and mankind do possess and hold some level of rule, no one holds the authority that belongs only to the creator. Humans have a limited measure on authority and the Father's authority is always superior to the authority of any creature or mankind. \n\nLastly, God's authority as being exhaustive is described as God having authority over everything and everything is under God's authority. That means everything over the universe is subject to the authority of God. His authority extends over everything that he has created and it clearly outlines that nothing is free from the obligation to obey God. Everything that he has created was created for a concise purpose, and no matter what the subject is, there is no moral neutrality.	9	2023-06-16 18:26:41+05:30	2023-07-04 01:03:01+05:30	\N	807	875
2942	The word diachronic is derived from Greek origin and refers to two Greek words, Dia, meaning through or throughout and Chronos which refers to time. Diachronic is commonly pointed to the way that Old Testament theology was transformed, changed or developed through time. While synchronic synthesis commonly focuses on the theological structures which emerged within chosen periods of biblical history, diachronic refers more in line with the storyline of history moving from one scene to the next. A common analogy which is made in reference to diachronic views in the Old Testament is that of watching a movie unfold from one scene to the next. \n\nWith a diachronic approach, biblical theology is more concerned with the changes that occurred in theological structures. A diachronic approach warrants questions probing what changes occurred during a given time when God spoke or acted? Or another example would be what theological developments occurred from the time of Moses' early childhood to his call at the burning bush. It is essential to note that while diachronic and synchronic approaches may differ, they are also highly interdependent of one another as well. Meaning, you are not able to purse one approach quite extensively without the other.	8	2023-05-15 06:03:29+05:30	2023-05-24 18:36:09+05:30	\N	804	665
2943	The term development is used instead of simply speaking of diachronic changes to suggest two idea. The first idea is that changes in the Old Testament theological structures tend to always move towards fulfilling the purpose of God throughout history. The second reason is because theological changes only occur through God's providential involvement within history. \n\nDevelopment involves divine purposes and divine providence. Within divine purposes, God's unfailing purposes for his creation is always accomplished, including everything in history and shifts within theology. This essentially translates into the fact that no matter what changes take place, history remains on course and reaches the goals that God had always ordained for it. When referring to divine providence, the Old Testament is filled with abrupt shifts which are ultimately caused by God's providence. With God's complex involvement within creation, divine revelations are connected to the historical circumstances. One example of this would many of the ceremonial practices that God had required of the Israelites within the Old Testament. God had commanded many practices in order to make his people into a holy place, and to our surprise some of these practices were marks of holiness.	9	2023-05-15 06:12:07+05:30	2023-05-24 18:36:09+05:30	\N	804	666
2944	Universal covenants are defined as covenants between God and all of humanity. First we see the universal covenants with Adam and Noah and it is referred to as universal because the covenant itself was made between God and all of humankind. So rather than thinking of it as a covenant just between God and Adam or God and Noah, it was more about a covenant to tell us much more about the relationship between God and all people. The universal covenant refers to the governance of divine-human relations established between God and man. \n\nNational covenants are defined as covenants between God and Israel. The national covenants that we can see are with Abraham, Moses, and David, or his special people. God chose Israel to be the nation that would lead the rest of humanity in service to God. That is why this covenant is referred to as the national covenant, the attention is primarily focused on one group. When we look at the covenant of Abraham, the covenant emphasized promises for the increase of Israel and the promised land, and Israel was to then spread God's blessings to the entire world. That is why the covenant between Abraham and God was known as the 'covenant of promise'. Moses' covenant was respectively known as the covenant of law and David's covenant is referred to the covenant of kingship. \n\nNew covenants are defined as the last major covenant that was mentioned within the Old Testament. The new covenants were predicted by Old Testament prophets and emphasized the covenant being established after the exile. The time when Israel would repent of their sin and God would bring history to it's final stages. The new covenant had a primary goal or design which was to govern God's people when God fulfilled his promises to re-establish his people after exile and to then spread his kingdom to the ends of the earth.	9	2023-05-15 06:24:33+05:30	2023-05-24 18:36:09+05:30	\N	804	667
2945	Biblical typology is essentially the study of diachronic developments between theological structures closely associated with significant persons, institutions and events in Scripture. In laymen terms, biblical typology is the study of types. For example, when the apostle Paul declares Adam was a 'pattern of the one to come.' We know from text that the the one to come is Christ, and therefore from a broader view we can see that Paul is observing that Adam was a type of Christ. \n\nBiblical typology is important to study as it functions as a figure of speech. When we refer to typology serving as a figure of speech, it is important to note that specifically it can be also a figure of comparison. A figure of comparison can be an indirect way of describing something by comparing them with something else. It is crucial to identify that biblical typology should be viewed as being designed to point towards futures antitypes. For example when we look at Adam being a type of Christ, it is important to affirm that in his providence, God has arranged history so that earlier persons, institutions, and events anticipated or foreshadowed later persons institutions and events. The reason that it becomes so important is that once we begin to grasp the process of reflecting, we can that identifying typologies are an important part of studying the Old Testament. In this way, you can begin to see how the theology of the Old Testament developed over time.	9	2023-05-15 06:37:54+05:30	2023-05-24 18:36:09+05:30	\N	804	668
2946	We should apply the Scriptures concerning aspects of Old Testament worship (sacrifice, temple, Passover meal) in church services today under the new covenant by first realizing what is the new covenant. As mentioned earlier, the new covenant was established after the exile and at the time when Israel would repent of their sin and God would bring history to it's final stage. Knowing that the church is currently living in it's final stages today at a time where the second coming of our Lord is imminent we should indeed take heed to the Scripture and apply the aforementioned teachings of Old Testament worship. \n\nBy knowing that God has allowed for what is to come to be explained and outline within scripture, it is important to note the covenant of fulfillment regarding the coming of Christ, to spread the word, and Jesus' return. The application of Old Testament worship to be applied also coincides the the fact that God's people have to remember that they can not and should not live as they were in an earlier covenant age. And they should also never forget the wisdom of what God had taught in earlier ages, hence the Old Testament teachings. In the Old Testament, worship by humans on earth always imitated the reflection of angelic worship of God in heaven. The worship of Israel was centralized at the tabernacle around the ark of the covenant, and while we as humans reside on earth, the footstool of God is here on earth. Therefore it is essential to remember that while he died for us, we have an ultimate responsibility to worship our creator.	8	2023-05-15 06:46:50+05:30	2023-05-24 18:36:09+05:30	\N	804	669
2947	The twofold concept of act and word revelation has characterized biblical theology of the New Testament similar to how God had revealed himself in the Old Testament, through act revelations and word revelations. Within the New Testament, there are several reports of revelatory acts of God referencing the earthly ministry of Christ. The ministry of the Holy Spirit is also referred to in the first century church relating to revelatory acts of God. The New Testament also provides foresight into the acts of God, especially things that are to take place in the future. For example, the return of Christ is depicted several times within the New Testament, and reports on word revelations with the acts of God. \n\nThe New Testament provides both the actions and words of Jesus , the apostles, and other Christians. God reveals his ultimate self through the actions and words of others in the New Testament, depicting the concept of act and word revelation. This can be seen throughout the New Testament, specifically in Acts and Revelations as well as the epistles. God clearly revealed himself through these passages between his actions actions and his words. These words would reference to God's actions on behalf of his people and report God's word to his people as well.	8	2023-05-16 23:55:16+05:30	2023-05-24 18:36:42+05:30	\N	805	670
2948	New Testament biblical theologians differ from Old Testament biblical theologians in regards to the way that they handle diachronic developments. For example, as New Testament history moved forward, theological structures also underwent diachronic changes. The earthly ministry of Christ brought forth hope of forgiveness of sins through his crucifixion. Also, as the Old Testament hoped for an entirely new creation to come in Christ, the New Testament refers to this as the clear prediction of Christ's glorious return. The primary difference though is outlined in three main obstacles. \n\nThe first obstacle is that the New Testament only refers to around 100 years of history conceptually. This is much different from the Old Testament. It simply does not cover enough of time for major diachronic developments to occur. The second is that in the New Testament many of the instances are very similar to one another. In contrast to the Old Testament, the New Testament God spoke to the people appropriate to the situations and God's people were fairly consistent during this time as opposed to the Old Testament. Lastly, the New Testament only represents one covenant, the New Covenant in Christ, where this diminished the significance of diachronic developments within the New Testament.	8	2023-05-17 00:06:55+05:30	2023-05-24 18:36:42+05:30	\N	805	671
2949	Becoming familiar with the background of Old Testament eschatology helps us understand diachronic developments in New Testament eschatology. Eschatology is a theological term, which essentially means the 'the doctrine of the last days, final things, or the end times.' The Bible is very clear about certain aspects when it comes to referencing the end times. The New Testament holds great promise and biblical theologians have approached eschatology in ways that cut across the grain of debate in a traditional sense. \n\nIn the Old Testament, the development regarding the last days developed significantly over time. It began with the covenants with Adam and goes up to the time of Noah, Abraham, Moses, and David. Within the beginning of the Old Testament and in biblical history, God revealed two crucial elements of eschatology, the first beginning with the story of mankind and how humans were created to to fill the earth and subdue it. The goal for history and creation was for the earth to be a place where glory would dwell with his people. The second element was the separation brought on as a result of sin. Two groups were formed as a result of the woman eating from the tree, and the first group is in reference to the seed of the woman who remain faithful to God. The seed of the serpent would be the second group who follow the ways of Satan. \n\nElements such as the one dating to the time of Adam, crossed over to the time of Noah, Abraham, Moses, and David. With these diachronic developments of eschatology within the Old Testament, ewe are able to see how the last days developed even further within the New Testament. First century Judaism with a twofold view on history, along with the relationship of John and Jesus which drove advancements in the development of eschatology are noted in the New Testament. Prophecies from older times were being fulfilled within the New Testament referring to the last days.	9	2023-05-17 00:18:26+05:30	2023-05-24 18:36:42+05:30	\N	805	6