2950	New Testament authors were preoccupied with Jesus' three-phase eschatology because biblical theologians have demonstrated that every New Testament teaching of Jesus' was shaped by his three-phase view of the last days. Every New Testament writer was deeply engaged with Jewish theology. New Testament authors were deeply focused on Jesus' three phase eschatology and their deep interest in this was displayed in everything that they wrote. For example, when the authors would write, they would commonly refer to the New Testament as the 'last days.' Christology, referred to as the doctrine of Christ displayed Jesus as the fulfillment of Israel's hope regarding the last days. Lastly, soteriology, the doctrine of salvation was shaped as well by eschatology. The New Testament authors had a similar theme in all of their writings and had displayed these three common themes within their writings as there was such a high importance around the end times and what was to come during the last days. The three-phase eschatology focused on components such as systemic theology, biblical theology and would concentrate on the application of salvation in recurrent themes, ordo salutis. \n\nThis three-phase eschatology represents a decisive break between first-century Judaism and Christianity because Jesus' teaching about the last days represented one of the most decisive ways in which Christians would break away from first century Judaism. An important factor in this was that Jewish leaders and the general population would be extremely upset and raged against Christians in regards to their views on the last days. They were furious that Christians believed that their Messiah had already come.	9	2023-05-17 00:35:28+05:30	2023-05-24 18:36:42+05:30	\N	805	673
2959	Both ontological Trinity and economic Trinity speak of the same Trinity, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. However the two emphasize different aspects of the relationships between the three persons. Ontological Trinity is a perspective of the Trinity and relates to being, and essence. If you are referring to or focusing on God's being, the ontological Trinity perspective focuses on the three persons of the Trinity and how the persons are integrated into one another and share a single essence. From this perspective, all three of God's persons are eternal, infinite, and unchangeable. With each of these three persons, they also have the same essential attributes as well. They include divine attributes such as wisdom, power, holiness, justice, truth and goodness. \n\nEconomic Trinity is also a perspective of the Trinity and relates to household management, and how the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit relate to one another as distinct personalities. When we refer to the economic Trinity, we are taking into consideration how God's persons interact with one another. As well as studying how they are able to relate to one another as individual persons. Although the concept is three in one, each of the three persons hold a different set of responsibilities. They each have a different level of authority, and a different assigned role and task to perform. The three persons engage in communication with one another and act upon each other. \n\nFrom both ontological and economic Trinity point of view, the Father is said to be the first person. The reason being the Son was created by the Father and the Holy Spirit was proceeded from the Father. We can see of the Father's authority in the Scripture as the Son refers to doing the Father's will. Therefore with both economic and ontological Trinity, they both share the similarity that they accept the belief of three persons in one essence, however they differ from one another as economic Trinity is focused on the relation of Father to son and Holy Spirit and the level of authority and household versus the ontological Trinity which is the essence of one God but three persons.	9	2023-06-16 18:05:17+05:30	2023-07-04 01:03:01+05:30	\N	807	873
2951	My understanding of the kingdom of God shapes the way that I do my ministry in several different ways. To begin, having a strong foundational knowledge of the Scripture is vital to ministry. Unless you have a clear understanding about a subject, you are not capable to speak to it, defend it, or be able to advance it. That can be taken into consideration in any field of work, but most importantly with the Word of God, as that is something you can not merely substitute. The kingdom of God is displayed throughout the Old Testament and New Testament and the Scriptures teach us that without salvation, there is no other path to heaven. There were many themes we read in the Old and New Testament about how the perception of human kind and nature perceived the teachings of Jesus and his prophets and disciples. Many of times, it was not received. In ministry, there is no guarantee that they ears that hear or the eyes that see have to believe as we can clearly see in the Bible, however the kingdom of God is at hand and it is our dire responsibility to ensure that we clearly apply the principles that God has set forth for us to be faithful stewards even when we are not accepted. \n\nMy ministry will definitely change because I was born into this life, I did not choose this life, rather this life chose me. I need to apply the concept of God's kingdom to all aspects of life and ministry knowing that even when God was denied by his own disciples and his people, he did not stop doing what his mission was. I will change my mindset that because I grew up in the church, everyone in the church will accept what we speak of, which is simply not true. I have to be willing to expect and accept that even members of a church and gathering may deny Christ, his teachings and what the Scripture points out. This is a cultural and mindshift for me as I move forward and advance my studies in theology.	9	2023-05-17 00:42:20+05:30	2023-05-24 18:36:42+05:30	\N	805	674
2952	The Apostles' Creed was developed over several centuries. The creed was composed of a collective work which would change and develop over time. It is a well known and commonly used summary of what a Christian believes in. Around A.D. 700, the creed was standardized in Latin. Although there is no evidence of such, it is believed that the Apostles' Creed was written during the first century by the twelve apostles themselves. There are many speculations on who was the original author(s) however, regardless of the author, the Apostles' Creed was widely used within the Western Churches. \n\nThe creed that we know today was at one point referred to as the latter version of the Roman Creed as there were many similarities between the two. During the initial days of referring to the creed, the wording would vary from church to church, however in the eighth century the wording was standardized into the commonly recognized form that we use today. While Creeds are well purposed and significant articles, it is important to note that they should never be given the same importance of the Bible. The Bible is the only inspired, and infallible rule of faith and practice, breathed by God. The Creed was created and formed to assist Christians to learn and remain true to the teachings of the Bible.	8	2023-06-14 17:41:14+05:30	2023-07-04 01:02:29+05:30	\N	806	866
2953	The church believed it was important to create and use a creed because the Apostles' Creed was created to help Christians learn and remain true to the teachings of the Bible. During the early days, many Christians were suspicious of creeds, and questioned whether a creed held the same authority as Scripture. No creed should ever be elevated to this level whether in theory or practice. The importance of creating a creed was to have a tool that summarized the understanding of the Scripture and help Christians learn and remain true to the teachings of the Bible. \n\nThe use of a creed was to originally look at the Scripture in it's original form, holding true to doctrine. Next the churches' traditional teachings as affirmations of Scripture were viewed and then the Apostles' Creed was used to summarize the churches understanding of Scripture. Therefore the main reason why the creed was important and created was to provide an understanding of the core beliefs of a Christian and provide a summary. The early church would rely heavily on Scripture for the basis of all its doctrine, but they would still depend on and require traditional teachings of the church to be able to summarize and understand the teachings found in the Scripture.	7	2023-06-14 17:47:36+05:30	2023-07-04 01:02:29+05:30	\N	806	867
2954	The Trinity in terms of persons and nature are described in the Creed on the belief that there is only one God and this God exists in three persons, yet one essence. The Trinity is one of the most important yet complex beliefs of Christianity, the belief of one essence being only one God, yet three persons, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. The first person, Father is referred to in nature as Almighty and the maker of Heaven and Earth. The Son is resembled in nature as one who went through incarnation, suffering, death, burial, resurrection, and ultimately ascension. Lastly, the Holy Spirit which allowed for the virgin Mary to become pregnant with Jesus and is associated with the experience of salvation. \n\nThe different work attributed to each by the Apostles' Creed is clearly outlined as the creed has a lot to say about the Son, Jesus Christ. For example, several mentions of details of his earthly life and ministry are revealed in the creed. His incarnation, coming to earth as a human baby boy, living a genuinely human lift, and suffering, death, burial, resurrection, and ascension into heaven. The creed also mentions that Jesus will judge all of humanity on the last day, condemning the wicked while granting the believers eternal life. The creed may not distinctly call out each statement extensively, however the creed says enough about God and his persons to affirm the belief of Trinity and the historic Christian faith.	9	2023-06-14 17:56:45+05:30	2023-07-04 01:02:29+05:30	\N	806	868
2960	Fatherhood as it relates to his role as Creator refers to God being the creator of all things. He is the father of everything he has created. Paul affirmed this idea in the Scripture by stating that to create something is to become its Father. While the creator has formed and brought all things to being, He continues to show great patience time and time again while the world that He created continues to fall into sin and grow further and further away from Him. \n\nFatherhood as it relates to his role as King is revealed in the Scripture similar to how a king  at times is referred to as the father of the people he serves and the people he serves are referred to as his children. David was referred to as a King and father to his people and when the people referred to David as their father, they were referring to him as their king. Similar to this concept, God was called Father because he was the great king. The great king ruled over all of the world and because he ruled over his chosen nation, Israel, they were his children and he was their father. God is referred to as Father since he sits on his heavenly throne and rules over all of creation and leads armies into battle. This shows an authoritative father and divine king caring for us, giving us comfort and confidence. Similar to the Lord's Prayer, we refer to God as our Father in Heaven, having dominion over all the Earth.  \n\nFatherhood as it relates to his role as family head over his people is seen in the Old Testament in many different areas. For example, as Adam was the family head over his wife and sons, God was the head of Adam. God is also portrayed as the head of the nation of Israel, he takes care of his people and portrays his love for Israel since it's childhood. Jesus also taught in the Scripture that our Father answers our prayers in the same way that a human father provides for their children. And he also disciplines us for our own good in the same manner that a human father would discipline his children. The church is commonly referred to as God's household or family and the love he has for his children is displayed throughout the Scripture.	8	2023-06-16 18:16:37+05:30	2023-07-04 01:03:01+05:30	\N	807	874
2955	It is important to participate in the church and to preserve its central doctrines as the Bible clearly teaches Christians to form an actual physical community and the Creed also emphasizes the importance of participating in the church. The church is identified in prominence as Christ has appointed the church to protect and proclaim the gospel of Jesus Christ. The main and primary job of the church is to protect the truth. The Apostles' Creed refers to the church in two different phrases, one being the holy catholic church and the other being the communion of Saints. It is important to note that the holy catholic church does not refer to the Roman Catholic Church, but as a church in all its parts throughout the world. The phrase communion of saints refers to the fellowship of believers, re-affirming the need and importance of the church. \n\nEven in the first century, believers wanted to avoid the gathering of saints and communion of fellowship, but the Scripture teaches that the church is not only important, but necessary for Christians. The Bible teaches Christians to form an actual physical community. The community is to be like a family where people are able to interact with one another face to face. The Apostles' Creed does not teach or encourage a believer or Christian to believe everything the local church teaches, rather the Creed affirms the fact that Christ appointed the church to protect and proclaim the gospel and its truth. \n\nThere are many false teachings around us, whether in the church or outside the church. There are many sinful practices as well. During all of this, God has never drawn back the assignment of the church, which was and is to protect the doctrine of the truth. The church has a job to do, and sometimes it is done better than other, however we have to keep trying to preserve doctrine in the church. If deny or abandon this responsibility, we are denying a major article of Christian faith.	9	2023-06-14 18:06:24+05:30	2023-07-04 01:02:29+05:30	\N	806	869
2956	The Apostle's Creed is foundational to Christian theology as it introduces the teachings of the apostles recorded in Scripture. Just a building requires a strong foundation, the church is symbolized as being founded on the apostles and prophets. Without a strong foundation that is true the church could not be built in a way that would honor God. Therefore, in a similar manner, Christian theology required a strong foundation on true doctrines and principles. This allows us to honor God and be useful to his people. \n\nThe Apostles'  Creed is foundation to theology since it introduces the teachings of the apostles in Scripture. The Apostles' Creed provides a summary of the central beliefs within Scripture, and the Bible is so clear on these points that we can not compromise them. If a false teacher or prophet presents us with bad or incorrect theology, the creed is a standard that helps us remain faithful to Scripture when standing against these false teachers and prophets. The Apostles' Creed serves as an anchor even when you are up against some of the most convincing false teachers that come against you. An example of this took place during the early centuries and the teaching of Gnosticism. The Apostles Creed would confidently reject this idea on the basis of the Scripture and be able to stand against such false teachings. \n\nLogical Basis is the other method in how the Creed is foundational to Christian theology. We need to initially learn the big doctrines first in order to get the shape of the tree in place, and be firmly rooted in Scripture. When we are able to picture it this way, we can see the value of the beginning with the Apostles' Creed. We are able to see the relationship between different beliefs within our theological systems.	9	2023-06-14 18:17:17+05:30	2023-07-04 01:02:29+05:30	\N	806	870
2957	Christians say that the God of the Bible is the one and only true God because of two concepts that they strongly believe in: the singularity of God along with the simplicity of God. To begin with the singularity of God, many people believed in false gods of the Greeks and Romans in the early days. This belief was so common that polytheism was at times required by law. Polytheism is the belief in the existence of multiple gods. However as the Scripture teaches us, people of all kinds know that deep within their hearts that the universe could not have been formed without the hands and work of a divine creator. People accept and understand the existence of God in the Scripture, however through humans sin and wrong doings we refuse to glorify him and give God the thanks He deserves. Within the belief of polytheism there is a belief in multiple gods, however no one god is able to claim being the only supernatural being, which is the role of the one true God.  \n\nThe second point refers to the singularity of God. Although God, being a single being exists in three persons. Christians believe in the Trinity and worship all three persons of the Trinity. By neglecting any one person of the Trinity is to reject God himself. Father Almighty known at the creator of all things refers to the entire Trinity hence the singularity of God. \n\nThe creed denies polytheism by using the singular form of 'god' and by dismissing any plural forms of 'god'. The Scripture also refers to the presence of God in different names, El Shaddai (almighty God), El Elyon (God most high), Adonai (Lord, Master, Ruler), and Yahweh (Jehovah, Lord). The creed proclaims that their is only one God that deserves to be called 'God' and that is the God of Christianity.	9	2023-06-16 17:43:53+05:30	2023-07-04 01:03:01+05:30	\N	807	871
2958	Simplicity in terms of describing God's essence refers to the fundamental nature/substance that makes up his being. God is anything but simple in nature, He is not easy to understand. However when using the term simplicity, His essence is not a combination of different substances. He is a unified whole that purely consists of a single substance. Central to the core belief of the Trinity, God has three persons but only one essence, which again illuminates His simplicity. The Scripture commonly refers to God in three persons, the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. However, the Scripture also states that there is only one God. So when teaching about God, we don't separate three separate Gods, but rather join them together in the Trinity allowing for three to become one. \n\nThese three persons had always existed together as only one God. The church made it absolutely clear that three persons in one God is one divine being. The Apostle's Creed also refers to the belief of one God who exists in three persons. Even in worship we fully acknowledge and offer worship to all three persons of the Trinity. To neglect any one person of the Trinity would be to reject God himself. The essence of God is fundamental in nature, and is not a composite of different substances, rather substance that makes up his being. He is unified and simple consisting of only one substance.	8	2023-06-16 17:52:31+05:30	2023-07-04 01:03:01+05:30	\N	807	872
3021	As the Creator, God has authority over man and all of creation. As in Isaiah 45:18 - For thus says the Lord, who created the heavens, who is God, who formed the earth and made it, Who has established it, Who did not create in vain, Who formed it to be inhabited. I am the Lord, and there is no other.\n\nGod created man in His image, and He also gave man authority over the fish of the sea, over the birds of the air, and over the cattle , over all the earth and over every creeping thing that creeps on the earth.\n\nGod is the ultimate authority simply because He is "the Lord, and there is no other" It is He who created everything, including mankind. God is the Father of mankind, and as such, He has authority over us.  Because God is our authority in every area, we need to make sure that we know what His word says, and strive to make it our authority in every area of life.	7	2023-10-21 07:30:36+05:30	2023-11-08 15:27:02+05:30	\N	837	875
2962	When the Scripture refers to the "Son of God" when applied to Jesus, it is  referring to Jesus being God's son in a unique way. The Trinity depicts this example of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit, and Jesus is the divine and eternal Son of God. When we refer to the deity of Christ, we must realize that Jesus is fully God and also fully man. We can not have one without the other. Jesus came in the form of man to this world and he is also fully God. This is one of the most fundamental pieces of fact that we must understand to comprehend the divinity of Jesus. \n\nThe Scripture refers the "Son of God" commonly to talk about things in the Scripture that are not divine, for example angels, the nation of Israel, the Kings of Israel, Adam and believers were all referred to as the Son of God throughout the Scripture, and  none of these beings were divine. Jesus, being the son and part of the Trinity comes to us with authority of power, and glory to the Father and the Holy Spirit. There is nothing that can be changed, added, revised about the Trinity and Son of God, He holds the same divine attributes of wisdom, power, holiness, justice, goodness, and truth. Although there are three different persons within one essence, each person holds a different level of responsibility. A father can not replace a son, a son can not replace a father, and a son can not replace the Holy Spirit. The Son of God is subordinate to the Father's authority and the Son has authority over the Holy Spirit. Therefore, with each role, comes a level of authority and responsibility. \n\nThe divine essence of the Trinity and Jesus being the Son of God is what provides the clear distinction of who Jesus is. He has always been the Son of God and is the eternal Son of God. We are told in the Scripture that Jesus was God's divine Son and is the exact representation of the Father's being. While God has three persons, there is only one essence. While the highest rank always belongs to the Father, 