2967	Although the Apostles' Creed does not state that the Holy Spirit is divine, the Apostles' Creed does affirm that the Holy Spirit is divine in two way. First, as the Trinity hold true to the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit being equals and as well as the creed's description of the conception of Jesus as being divine. The Trinitarian formula emphasizes the belief of having only one God which exists in three persons, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. During the early church, Hippolytus explained the baptismal creed used in his day made the structure of the Trinity very explicit. For example, a person was immersed three times during water baptism. First confessing in the articles of faith relating to the Father, then the Son, and lastly the Holy Spirit. \n\nThe second way the creed affirms the divinity of the Holy Spirit is through the conception of Jesus. When the angel of the Lord came and spoke to Mary, the angel state the Holy Spirit will come upon you, in reference to the Most High. Only God the Father can have the power of the Most High, all pointing to the work of the Holy Spirit. The Apostles' Creed affirmed the full divinity of the Holy Spirit through this conception affirming that creating the body of Jesus was specifically the work of God. With the belief and confirmation of the Trinitarian belief, we can see the expressed form of divinity as the angel states to Mary that the Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you. This references the magnificence of the Father as the authoritarian that will be divine and fulfill the will of the Father.	8	2023-06-21 17:39:56+05:30	2023-07-04 01:04:16+05:30	\N	809	881
2968	The biblical basis for the creed's teaching about the Holy Spirit's divinity can be revealed in several different ways. The creed has always been recognized as a summary of the Scripture and never to be taken in as a replacement for the word of God. The Bible however points to the teachings of the divinity of the Holy Spirit in several different ways. The areas of focus are around the names that he is called, the attributes that he possesses, the work he performs, and the Trinitarian formulas that refer to him.  In the Bible, there are several names that are connected to the Holy Spirit and and some of these names suggest the divinity of the Holy Spirit in a very clear and concise manner. Though the word holy does not in itself indicate the Holy Spirit is divine, God is commonly referred to as the Holy One which is part of the Trinity. Names that were implicit on the continuum were: Spirit of the Lord, Spirit of God, Spirit of the living God, Spirit of Jesus, Spirit of Christ, Spirit of your Father, Spirit of his Son, Spirit of him who raised Jesus from the dead. \n\nAttributes that were possessed by the Holy Spirit demonstrates the continued divinity through communication, and forms of non-communication that can be expressed. God possesses the attribute of communication with human beings. He demonstrates that we were created by a rational God that we are able to communicate with him and he to us with a form of communication. This is also relevant in a non-communication form through the omnipotence of God. While God is perfect, his power is all throughout which exemplifies the divinity of the Holy Spirit. This can also be revealed through the works that he has done, which are appropriate for God alone and none other. He is creates new life, and regenerates our spirit and is the access to the Father. There is also power behind his works and his miracles which are purely divine. \n\nThe Trinitarian formula affirms the Holy Spirit being a member of the Trinity while being of one essence. The qualities possessed are unique to persons and includes the will of the father, intelligence, emotions and agency. There may not always be a clear distinction between the Spirit and the other persons as we have learned from previous chapters since the Father controls the Son and the Son the Holy Spirit but they are always going to be three persons with one essence. The father will always hold the authoritative power over the son and the holy spirit while being unchangeable but with divine attributes.	9	2023-06-21 17:52:41+05:30	2023-07-04 01:04:16+05:30	\N	809	882
2969	The Bible ascribes personal attributes to the Holy Spirit as being too many to list, however we will list a few for demonstration. To being the holy spirit does indeed have a will. This is the vehicle used to plan, to desire and to choose. If this were possible, it can not be of a simple force of power, rather of a divine nature that is able to have the ability to have a will and act upon it. If the holy spirit has a will and wishes to impart on specific people, specific gifts than the spirit must indeed be a person. Forces that are impersonal can not have plans or desires and fulfill  such actions. The holy spirit also has the personal attribute of intelligence. This intelligence is revealed in more than one ways, for example the searching and knowing the mind of God is known through this attribute. He also gives wisdom and knowledge to others. Forces that are impersonal are not able to do this and can not think, know or teach. \n\nThese attributes demonstrate his personhood by acknowledging first and foremost that the Holy Spirit is the third person of the trinity. Not the third god or third spirit, but the third person of the Trinity. The Trinity is composed of three persons, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. The responsibility that each person holds is different from one another and each has their own authority and toles. The spirit is subject to the higher authority of the Father and the Son. Which in return means that the Father has dominion and power over the other two, although they are one essence. The mission of the spirit is to carry out the will of the father, and as we can see in the Scripture, to carry out the instruction of the Father and to glorify the Father and the Son.	9	2023-06-21 18:01:42+05:30	2023-07-04 01:04:16+05:30	\N	809	883
2970	The Holy Spirit is distinct from the Father and the Son as a separate person within the Trinity as there are three persons part of the Trinity. While the Trinity is composed of one essence, there is indeed three persons part of the Trinity with clear responsibilities, authority and roles for each person. The holy spirit is a subject to a higher authority to the Father and to the Son. The primary role of the spirit is to carry out instructions and glorify the Father and the Son. This is why the holy spirit is distinct from the Father and the Son as a separate person within the Trinity. The holy spirit is called the 'third person' of the Trinity. \n\nThe scripture clearly highlights the distinction of the Spirit and the other persons of the Trinity that shows their interaction with one another in ways that emphasize their difference. The Scripture points to how the Father would send the Son first and the Holy Spirit, with each person they had an appointed role. The spirit would not begin the work appointed until the son had completed his work on earth and ascended into heaven. This makes it clear that the spirit is  separate from the son and a member of the trinity. The spirit is also our defense attorney, pleading our cases before God when we intercede with the Father. When Paul taught us about the holy spirit, he also mentioned the trinity and how  there is one person that is interceding while the others are hearing the intercession.	8	2023-06-21 18:07:26+05:30	2023-07-04 01:04:16+05:30	\N	809	884
2983	God's eternal counsel regarding Christ is that humankind has a purpose and redemption is available only through Jesus Christ. The Bible teaches us clearly the purpose that man was created in the Old Testament in the beginning of time. When God designed and Adam and then Eve to have authority over the Earth and gave them free will, they chose to dishonor and disobey God which then allowed for the infamous curse to fall upon them and all of mankind. The ultimate consequence around their sin has been passed down since then leading to death. However, God the Father allowed for his Son to die for our sins, to allow for redemption to take place. \n\nThis redemption has created a hope for humanity. God the Father promised he would send a Redeemer, who we know as his Son. The Redeemer would bring salvation to the sinner and be able to restore the world as well as bring judgement. Jesus will return only at a time that is known to his Father and will return in the same manner that he ascended into Heaven and it will be public and visible for all to see. He will return as a powerful conqueror and all those who have ever died will be resurrected and the last judgement will take place. God the Father gives us this counsel that Jesus will destroy all his enemies and allow the blessing of all of his faithful people. No human being will be exempt from judgement, and sinner will be purged and creation will be renewed. This will then allow for all of creation to be restored to the original plan of God.	9	2023-07-05 18:57:26+05:30	2023-08-07 21:34:29+05:30	\N	812	762
2971	The Holy Spirit's relationship to the other two persons of the Trinity have been described from two different perspectives. The ontological and the economic trinity perspective have both mentioned the perspectives on the same trinity - the father, the son, and the holy spirit. When referring to the ontological trinity, the reader is focused on the existence of God, the Father. The holy spirit is equal in power and glory to the father and the son in this perspective. All of the three persons in this belief are infinite, eternal and not changeable. The have the same and divine attributes as wisdom, power and holiness. When referring to the economic trinity, you consider how the three persons interact with one another. The trinity here is described as having three different roles and assignments. The highest authority is granted to the Father and then the Son and then the holy spirit is subject to the higher two. The role of the spirit is to carry out the will of the Father and the instructions that are set forth in order to bring glory. \n\nThe reason the holy spirit is the third person of the trinity is because he is breathed out by the father, who is the first person, and by the son who is the second person. There have been claims in other churches during the early days that it was only the father that breathed out the holy spirit, however since the creed was created before this controversy, it did not address these claims. The holy spirit does hold the lowest rank from the perspective of the economic trinity, however it is crucial to note that the he is still fully God and has complete authority over every aspect of creation. There is also a mutual deference, what one person does, they all do. They are alike and agree on everything in nature, the lower rank of the holy spirit or subordination to the Father and Son does not mean he is inferior to them, he is not, the spirit is equal to them both.	8	2023-06-21 18:15:02+05:30	2023-07-04 01:04:16+05:30	\N	809	885
2972	The church in the New Testament finds its roots in the Old Testament several different times. In the New Testament, the church is spoken of by using the Greek word ekklesia. However, this term was derived from the Greek translation of the Old Testament. It was commonly used to describe the gathering of the nation of Israel. Even while the the term ekklesia may be used to refer to the church, the word is originally found to refer to the gathering and assembly of the people of Israel. While there may be many differences in translation, the church does not represent a physical building or structure, rather a congregation of people here on earth. \n\nThe church is established for a purpose and has been granted authority to establish and maintain our relationship with God. The church is equivalent to God's kingdom on Earth through which he dispenses his grace. We can see that within the New Testament, Peter refers to the church by names that were applied to Israel in the Old Testament hence finding and connecting its roots back to the Old Testament. The church was identified as a chosen people, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, and a people belonging to God, 1 Peter 2:9. One of the key differences to note between the Old Testament church and gathering against the New Testament church was that the Old Testament church was commonly depicted as the root of the tree. The strong foundation and level of the church was the Old Testament, and the New Testament was considered to the be branches of the same tree. This illustration was was identified in several portions of the Scripture and the Old Testament was the bulk of the tree consisting of primarily Jews, while the Gentile Christians were the branches of the tree.	8	2023-06-26 18:40:25+05:30	2023-07-04 01:04:49+05:30	\N	810	886
