2974	Some of the implications of the relationship between the Old Testament and the New Testament church is that Jesus ordained the New Testament church as the restoration and development of the Old Testament church. We can see the continuum of the gathering of the people of Israel and the church described in the New Testament. There are several fundamental connections between the Old Testament and New Testament churches and they all are driven with the same purpose, group of  people and obligations.\n\n To begin with the purpose, the purpose has never changed between the church of the Old Testament and the New Testament. The history of the world can be summed up in three stages, between the creation, the fall and redemption. Beginning with the creation of the world, man and animals by God. He formed man and the beautiful garden of Eden, and following the creation, there was a mandate or ordinance to follow, and through the failure to abide by this, God has has now created the opportunity for man to redeem itself through redemption and restoration. \n\n The goal of the gathering of Israel and the New Testament church remains the same and continues to be that their purpose is to turn the world into God's earthly kingdom. Simply put, the church has a moral purpose to convert the world and the people of this world to be a replica of the kingdom of God. The group of people gathering can also be identified within the implications of the Old Testament and New Testament church, the gathering of the assemblies should included both believers and unbelievers. \n\nWe can clearly see this portrayed within the New Testament throughout several parables and teachings of Jesus as to how no one should be separated or go astray, and if one were to, then the it is our responsibility to bring them to Christ. Lastly, the obligations of the Old Testament and New Testament church are similar in nature. To love God, to spread and advance his kingdom, and to bring him glory.	8	2023-06-26 18