2975	The word catholic means, universal or including all Christians in all congregations. The reference is not to the Roman Catholic church, rather it is a description of unity that exists between all of the churches that follow Christ faithfully. When we speak of the Apostles' Creed and how it was understood within the creed, it is important to note that there is no organization of all Christian gatherings mentioned. Rather, the creed is referring to the unity of the Holy Spirit and how it should exist between all Christian churches, despite any organizational difference. The term catholic is meant to be inclusive and include  all Christians in all congregations. \n\nThe term catholic is derived from the Latin word, catholicus which means whole or complete. Meanwhile the latter churches were fragmented by divisions and strife, reversing the original intention and causing them to be exclusive. However the creed has the original intention of the unity of the Holy spirit among the churches and continue the covenant with God and under the headship of Christ. In 1 Corinthians 1:2, Paul wrote in his teachings, "To the church of God in Corinth, to those sanctified in Christ Jesus and called to be holy, together with all those everywhere who call on the name of our Lord Jesus Christ - their Lord and ours." As it is mentioned, called to be holy and together in the name of our Lord and theirs can be a clear portrayal of how the church is to be viewed as universal. The creed refers to this as the catholicity of the church, where it does not have in mind a organization of ALL Christian gatherings, rather the unification of the Holy Spirit acknowledging the purpose to be holy and just advancing the kingdom of God among all churches.	8	2023-06-26 19:11:01+05:30	2023-07-04 01:04:49+05:30	\N	810	889
2976	The visible catholic church is defined as one worldwide fellowship of all people in covenant with God under the headship of Christ. The catholic church has distinct markers including the word, sacraments, and discipline. The means of grace considered as the tools or mechanisms that God would ordinarily use to apply grace to his people are measured through these three areas, word, sacraments and prayers. The visible catholic church also believes in spiritual gifts for the purpose of building up the church through the Holy Spirit. These spiritual gifts are shared by the entirety of the church through public worship services, sign for the unbelievers, building up the entire church and believing that unbelievers are condemned. The visible catholic church also believes in the sharing of material goods with one another who were in need during the early church and within the Bible. \n\nTo decide which churches should be embraced as part of the catholic church or the universal church should be grounded within the word of God. There is no church, claim, interpretation or proclamation that has the exclusive right to interpret and teach the Scripture. God has given his word to the whole visible church and he has given the Holy Spirit to help us understand the Bible. \n\nThis is a common problem faced today, to know which church should a believer embrace, the catholic church or the universal church of Christ. Either one churches opens their arms too wide and include any church that claims to be a Christian or the other is too exclusive and only accept narrowly defined congregations of denominations. The sacraments to administer and conduct baptism is given to the entire church, not just one denomination or another. Lastly, discipline is granted to all churches and not exclusive to a single denomination or congregation.	8	2023-06-26 19:23:02+05:30	2023-07-04 01:04:49+05:30	\N	810	890
