2979	Individual responsibility plays a role in the forgiveness of sins by two factors. The first is having the fear of the Lord which can receive his forgiveness. The Bible teaches us that God does not punish us according to our wrong doings and mistakes,  however we must acknowledge that the Lord is slow to anger, compassionate and gracious. He has the ability and authority to not treat us according to our sins. However, with that concept comes the fear of the Lord. If you set your hearts towards seeking him and seeing the true meaning of repentance and forgiveness, God is able to forgive. In a spirit of true brokenness, and repentance, we must be able to express the depth of our sorrow and regret to the king who is the only one able to forgive. \n\nWe must strive to have faith in God, knowing that he is divine and acknowledging his grace. He is able to show mercy and forgiveness to those who fear him. When we do ask for forgiveness of sins, individual responsibility also means to show our brokenness. Our genuine remorse and guilt over the sin we have committed breaking God's law. Forgiveness can be granted through prayer, asking God for forgiveness. Meditate, seek God's face, pray or petition on behalf of one another in your individual responsibility. These are the methods to seek forgiveness of one's sins, rather than mistaking grace as a lifeline, we must understand that the basis is always the merit of Christ. We are able to  inherit this grace through his obedient life and sacrificial death on the cross for us. We are able to earn this forgiveness through this manner.	7	2023-06-28 17:59:06+05:30	2023-07-04 01:05:23+05:30	\N	811	893
2980	Man's fall into sin corrupted not only our souls but also our physical bodies as well. Human beings consist of two main parts, one being the human body, and the second being the soul. In Genesis 3:19, God placed a curse on mankind as a result of the sin committed by Adam and Eve, "By the sweat of your brow you will eat your food until you return to the ground, since from it you were taken; for dust you are and to the dust you will return." As a result of the sin of Adam and Eve, God cursed them to be mortal. Until then, they were not supposed to die. However, now the Bible shows us that God declared that they would eventually die and return to dust. This is a clear result of how man's fall into sin corrupted not only their souls, but their physical bodies too. \n\nGod did not create us to die, that was never his intention. Our salvation will never be complete, until Christ returns and redeems our bodies. Death is inevitable, it is a universal human experience that everyone must face at one point in time or another. However, the way to view death is that we are taken directly into the presence of Christ.\n\nAs all human beings descend from Adam and Eve, all human kind were born with sin and corruption, and continue to carry that curse. The corruption of sin results in physical hardship, sickness and eventually death. In Psalm 90:10, the Bible refers to how even the greatest of days are filled with trouble and sorrow, the years quickly pass and we are gone. The wonderful promise that we get to hold onto though is that God is our salvation and Jesus died for ours sins. This allows for both our souls and bodies to be redeemed.	8	2023-06-28 18:07:26+05:30	2023-07-04 01:05:23+05:30	\N	811	894
2981	The three stages of bodily redemption are composed of the things believers experience during our current life on earth, the second is the intermediate state of our bodies that begins with physical death, and the last is the new life of resurrection which begins when Christ returns. \n\nAccording to the Scripture we will experience these three stages quite differently. To begin with the the current life on earth, we refer to redemption on earth with the belief that once we believe in Christ, we are sanctified, or set apart for God and purified. He is able to continue to sanctify us and use our bodies in ways that honor him, resulting in us giving our bodies to God as a living sacrifice. This is experienced as a result of the Holy Spirit. The Holy Spirit's indwelling presence throughout our lives allows our bodies to continue to benefit and live a life pleasing to the Lord. \n\nThe second one we experience is the redemption of our bodies in the present life. The process of redemption continues even after we die, when are bodies are temporarily separated from our souls. This is the stage between life on earth and the lives we will have in resurrection, during this period is when our souls dwell with Christ in heaven. This is revealed in the Scripture in Matthew 17:3 and 2 Corinthians 5:6-8. Death frees the body from sin, while the body may be returning to dust and decaying, our bodies are in a state where they are incapable of any thought, action, or feeling whether it may be good or bad. Whether our bodies are buried or cremated, our bodies continue to  be a part of us. \n\nThe last experience is the new life of resurrection that begins when Christ returns. With the resurrection comes the relief that the consequences of sin will finally and forever be completely put away from us. Our bodies will receive a new and perfect life which allows for a glorious life that allows for perfected human beings. Just as Jesus body had gone through changes in resurrection, our bodies will also be changed and we will finally become the people that God had always intended for us to be.	9	2023-06-28 18:19:51+05:30	2023-07-04 01:05:23+05:30	\N	811	895
2982	During the week of creation, one of the things that is taught by Scripture is that when God, the Father was creating the world, God the Son was beside the Father. The Scripture also reveals that the Father created the world through his Son. The role of God the Father was that once the world was created, all of dominium and ownership would be given to Son to inherit and rule over it's entire creation. \n\nIn the book of Colossians, 1:6 we read, "For by him all things were created: things in heaven and on earth, visible and invisible, all things were created by him and for him." Therefore the passage implies that everything that God the Father had created was indeed for God the Son and created with the Son. The Scripture is very clear on the fact that God the Father was the overall designer of the world. He was able to create the world through his ways of what would please him. For example, after each creation, the Scripture states that "God saw that it was good" and he was pleased with what he had created. So for six days, God filled a void, shapeless, world without light to become full of creation, shape, light, sea, land, animals and vegetation. \n\nDuring the first three days of creation, God the father began to create and fill the world in ways that would be pleasing to him. He formed the world, and allowed there to be light. He separated the sky from the sea and the dry land from water. During the next three days, God the created the sun, moon and stars. He assigned the sun to rule the day and the moon to rule the night. He created the fish, the birds, and all of the animals that live on Earth. \n\nAt the end of the creation week, God the Father had completed his kingdom. He then appointed humankind to govern over the Earth so that they could bring him glory. God did this with the authority of keeping the God the Son as the heir of all things that was the king of all of the earth who would inherit, and rule over the entire creation.	9	2023-07-05 18:46:59+05