2984	The divine promises that made redemption certain can be traced back to the very beginning. If God has declared a promise, that promise remains as he does not ever change. The world may come and go, but the word of God will remain the same forever and ever. We are taught that God's promises are immutable and he will never break them. Regarding redemption, the divine promises that are made fall in line with the original plan of God. That man is to live on earth as it is the kingdom of heaven and the promises that are rooted between the Father and Son will come to pass. The covenant of redemption which is the promise to redeem fallen humanity will take place and secure redemption after the fall into sin. \n\nThere are several examples of promises or covenants that were made which ensure redemption is certain. The initial covenant of redemption is where the Father, Son and Holy Spirit promised to redeem fallen humanity, the covenant of Grace is the arrangement between the Father and Son and redeemed humanity. There were other examples of promises that ensured redemption such as the Adamic Covenant where redemption was offered to humanity when it was not deserved. The Noahic Covenant which promised to preserve humanity until the Son's redeeming work was fulfilled. The Abrahamic Covenant which promised that one of Abraham's descendants would be the Redeemer. \n\nThese examples provide us with the assurance that the promises made by God is eternal and unchangeable. There is no refuting any of the other examples mentioned in the Bible whether it be the Mosaic, Davidic, or New Covenant. These are all examples of how promises surrounding redemption and the covenant between God and mankind were fulfilled and continues to this day.	8	2023-07-05 19:06:51+05:30	2023-08-07 21:34:29+05:30	\N	812	763
