3027	While the author of Hebrew remains unknown, they used the Old Testament to support their theological views. For example within the book of Hebrews, there is a combination of either quotes or references to the Old Testament nearly 100 times. The historical facts of the Old Testament have been intertwined into the presentation of Christian faith into the book of Hebrews. \n\nSpecific examples that have been referenced in the book of Hebrews with context dating to the Old Testament include: referencing God's son (2 Samuel 7:14), angels as serving spirits (Psalm 104:4), lower than angels (Psalm 8:4-6), blessings of God's vindication (Isaiah 8:17), God as a consuming fire (Deut 4:24). \n\nThe author of Hebrews called attention to the fact that history had reached its last days in the times of Jesus. He did this through referencing the Old Testament as support and then a number of exhortations to continue and prosper in the Christian faith. This was done primarily due to the fact that the author had to challenge the teachings of the local community which was mainly Jewish. The one thing that remained sacred and in common was the Old Testament, and that is why we can see nearly 100 references or quotes from the Old Testament in the book of Hebrews.  In these scenarios, rather than noting just historical facts, the author spent more time on theological beliefs about God himself and the matters in close relation to God.	8	2023-10-25 06:11:01+05:30	2023-11-08 15:28:34+05:30	\N	839	781
3028	In the first century, Jewish communities often exalted angels as being powerful, glorious creatures who brought divine revelations to inferior human beings. While the outlooks of the community were rooted in biblical beliefs and passages, they gave excessive honor to angels. This raised a serious challenge to those who followed Christ. \n\nThe author of Hebrews taught his audience that Jesus is superior to angels. This was accomplished in five steps, the first of which the author wrote to the audience that they should follow Jesus because Jesus is God's supreme source of divine revelation. The author declared that God spoke through angels. The second step was through the explanation that Jesus is greater than angels because he alone is God's messianic Son. Thirdly, the author told his audience to heed and pay careful attention to the great message of salvation first announced by Jesus. Fourth, the book of Hebrews supports the belief in the supremacy of Christ by explaining that Jesus is now ruler over all the angels. And in the future all of the believers would reign over them with him. Lastly, the author demonstrated that Jesus is Abraham's descendant, and Jesus will share his glory with Abraham's descendants, not with the angels. \n\nThrough these five steps laid out in the book of Hebrews, the author was able to provide the honor that only Christ brings and how Jesus brought revelation that was far superior to any revelation given through any angel.	8	2023-10-25 06:20:02+05:30	2023-11-08 15:28:34+05:30	\N	839	782
3029	In order to discuss how the new covenant is superior to the old, we must first understand the reference for the 'new covenant'. The new covenant comes from Jeremiah 31:31, where the prophet predicted that God would grant a covenant of final renewal to Israel and Judah after Israel's exile in the final days. This was also referred to as the 'covenant of peace'. \n\nThe New Covenant is based on the ultimate sacrifice of Jesus Christ and providers eternal forgiveness of sins. The Old Covenant would require ongoing animal sacrifices and offerings that could not fully take away the sins of mankind. The New Covenant is focused more on a personal and direct relationship with God rather than a system of laws and rituals. The New Covenant is also not prohibited to just the Jewish people, but rather to people of all nations. \n\nThe New Covenant cannot fail per Hebrews 8:6 as it is founded on better promises. This offers the complete transformation of God's people and the the final eternal forgiveness of their sins. Jesus did away with sin once and for all because of his sacrifice and the shedding of bless. It was not merely sprinkle in a man made sanctuary, but He entered heaven by the sacrifice of himself, just as God had promised. \n\nJesus died as a ransom in order to set his people free from judgement, and the author clearly states that Jesus will return but not to bear sin again, rather to bring the fullness of salvation to those who wait for him. \n\nThe New Covenant is considered superior to the Old Covenant because it provides a more complete and personal relationship that is enduring with God through faith in Jesus Christ, which is opposed to the conditional, ritual based system of the old.	9	2023-10-25 06:30:59+05:30	2023-11-08 15:28:34+05:30	\N	839	783
