3047	The  majority of the interpreters believe that Paul was not the author of the book of Hebrews due to the fact that the author never revealed or identified himself. The other cause for this is that the book of Hebrews was never included in the Marcionite Canon and Muratorian Canon. Another fact to note from the writing style of the author of Hebrews is that it is different than Paul's letters, the style of Greek that is used in Hebrews is more sophisticated than the Greek that is used in Paul's other letters. There is also a different level of theological emphasis in Hebrews that is distinct from the other writings of Paul. \n\nHowever, there are three distinct reasons for the stance that is believed to identify Paul as not being the author of the book of Hebrews. The first is that is was Paul's practice to name himself in his epistles. He was concerned deeply around individuals forging his writing and spreading lies under his name. The second reason is subjects don't receive much emphasis if any in Paul's letters. The fact that Melchizedek was mentioned three times drew attention to the difference in styles compared to Paul's letters. The third reason is the way the writer of Hebrews distanced himself from the first generation of  Jesus' followers.	7	2023-11-22 19:26:10+05:30	2024-03-03 11:18:58+05:30	\N	848	776
3048	The five characteristics of Hebrew's original audience include  Jewish, Hellenistic, Immature, Persecuted, and Near Apostasy. \n\nFor the Jewish audience, Hebrews 1:1 make this clear, "In the past God spoke to our forefathers through the prophets at many time and in various ways." The Old Testament Israelites are referred to as "our forefathers" which are considered to be the ancestors of the author and his audience.\n\nThe Hellenistic audience, which interpreters believe have lived outside of Palestine. The content in Hebrews reveals that the audience was familiar with theological teachings that were more common among Jews who lived outside of Palestine than the traditional Jews who lived within Palestine. \n\nThe Immature audience which is known as the original audience of Hebrews was described in Hebrews 5:12, "Though by this time you ought to be teachers, you need someone to teach you the elementary truths of God's word all over again." This interprets to say that the audience has been listening to and exposed to the word of God over and over again for a long time, but they have yet not matured in the word. That is the emphasis the author gives when saying that this audience should have been teachers by now. \n\nThe Persecuted audience is addressed in Hebrews 10:32, "Remember those earlier days after you had received the light, when you stood your ground in a great contest in the face of suffering ... So do not throw away your confidence, it will be richly rewarded." In this passage, the audience mentioned is being praised for their strength and were told to not throw away their confidence. The author of Hebrews mentions this group was facing the threat of more persecution even in the future and this was addressed in Hebrews 12:3-4. \n\nLastly the Near Apostasy, this audience was near danger of turning away from Christ entirely. In Hebrews 10:26-27, it reads, "If we deliberately keep on sinning after we have have received knowledge of the truth, no sacrifice for sins is left, but only a fearful expectation of judgement and of raging fire that will consume the enemies of God." The author of Hebrews was not concerned about peccadillos or small sins, rather he was concerned more about those in danger of turning away from God.	8	2023-11-22 19:38:21+05:30	2024-03-03 11:18:58+05:30	\N	848	777
3049	The author of Hebrews used different literary devices and methods to reveal and express his exhortations to his audience. Frequency, for example allows us to understand the urgency of his message. The author exhorted to the audience using intensity and the hortatory subjunctive, a Greek verbal form used to urge or implore. At least 30 times these exhortations appear explicitly which would urge the audience often to "let us" do this or that. \n\nThe author also exhorted using imperatives, which is often translated as direct orders. Hebrews 12:12-16  reveals the importance of adopting new attitudes or actions. He did not want to simply inform the audience of theological doctrines, he wanted them to adopt different viewpoints and attitudes towards it all. \n\nThe author of Hebrews is described as highly rhetorical, meaning there was a persuasive or urgent debate mechanism in the first century. This is found more often in Hebrews than in any other book in the New Testament. This helped the author accomplish their purpose, containing a strong literary argument, using lots of different structural features to accomplish that, drawing readers in and convincing them of the argument that he is making. Some of these rhetorical devices included synkrisis, exempla, and qol wahomer.	8	2023-11-22 19:46:03+05:30	2024-03-03 11:18:58+05:30	\N	848	778
