3082	Archeological discoveries can give us insight into biblical covenants by helping us grasp how the faithful Israelites were able to understand that there were fundamental connections between God's covenant and his kingdom. There are three types of royal documents from the ancient Near East which often appear within the context of biblical covenants: first, royal and parity treaties. These are considered as the covenants which kings of somewhat equal status were able to acknowledge their relationships of each other. \n\nRoyal parity treaties in regards to divine covenants portray that although men would consider themselves of equal status to mutually negotiate the terms of covenants between, that was never the case when it came to humans and God. God never negotiated with a human being purely because they were never seen as his equal. The royal grants were writings that revealed how ancient kings would bestow land or special status towards their faithful servants. \n\nMuch of the ancient royal documents that have allowed for continued understanding of biblical divine covenants are suzerain-vassal treaties. These ancient writings offer insight into how the authors of the bible were able to understand and grasp the relationship between God's kingdom and his covenants. These were also international agreements that greater established kings would have with lesser established kings and their nations. This would offer additional context and insight into the relationship between God's kingdom and his covenants. We are able to see within the Old Testament of how the Pharaohs of Egypt and the kings of Hittites, Babylonians, Assyrians, and even of Israel would conquer and establish their territories by controlling the weaker kingdoms.	9	2023-12-01 07:11:34+05:30	2024-03-03 11:15:29+05:30	\N	851	987
