3181	The early Christian church almost unanimously believed that Luke, a physician and companion of Paul the Apostle, wrote both the Gospel of Luke and the book of Acts:\n\nPapyrus Bodemr XIV\nThis manuscript, which dates back to around 200 AD, contains the end of Gospel of Luke and uses the subscription "The Gospel According to Luke"\n\nAncient Sources\nIrenaeus, Tertullian, Clement of Alexandria, Origen, and the Muratorian Canon all believed Luke was the author.\n\nTheophilus\nBoth books are written to a man named Theophilus, and the book of Acts begins with "the former treatise have I made" which may refer to the Gospel of Luke.\n\nThe common authorship of the two books suggests that Acts was written after Luke probably between 75 and 95 CE. during a period of increasing pressure for followers of Christ to define themselves in relation to their Jewish roots. Roman historians have generally taken the basic historicity of Acts as granted.	7	2024-06-08 08:53:47+05:30	2024-06-19 05:14:24+05:30	\N	872	620
