3188	Five major redemptive covenants in the Old Testaments are as follows: \n\nAdam: after he had sinned, God extended mercy and salvation through proto-euangelion commonly referred to as the first gospel. This is where redemption was offered to humankind after the fall. Despite the redemption offered, it was made clear that there would be clear consequences for the sin that was committed. \n\nNoah: while the story is familiar of how God destroyed the world that Noah and his family lived in, there was a promise and covenant that was made, that until the end of the present order of creation God would spare such a harsh rebuke and not flood the earth again. \n\nAbraham: The covenant that was made with God and Abraham was that He would extend his redemption to the rest of humanity and establish His kingdom presence in the Promised Land and towards the ends of the Earth. This would included members from all nations on earth and the kingdom of God would not just consist of a single nation or people. \n\nMoses: While people would be exiled from the Promised Land and be scattered among the people and many nations, the mercy of God would not allow Him to abandon either Moses or his people forever, and there would come a time where God will fulfill his promises of blessings for his people and judgement against his enemies. \n\nDavid: with his kingdom rule and dynasty, the covenant with David was that his dynasty would rule over God's earthly kingdom forever. While the kingdom of God is a real place that is populated by real people, the expectation is that this dynasty would also be real and permanent just like each of God's words and promises.	8	2024-06-10 20:43:32+05:30	2024-06-19 05:15:15+05:30	\N	875	636
3189	The two great ages expected by first century Jewish theologians were "this age" and "the age to come". When referring to "this age", the characterization of sin, suffering, exile, and death were mentioned at the beginning of the fall and corresponds all the way to the age of redemption. The second being the "the age to come" when God's kingdom would fill the earth followed by forgiveness, righteousness, peace and eternal life. \n\nJesus and His apostles for the most part did agree on the way that Jewish theologians characterized these ages. They were teaching that Messiah would indeed end this age and time period of sin and death while ushering in a age to come with all it's blessings. In 1 Corinthians 2:6-8, 2 and 2 Corinthians 4:4 and 1 Timothy 6:17-10 we can see how Paul would distinguish between this present age of evil and the age to come with the return of Christ, the Messiah. \n\nWhile Jesus and the prophets did agree to many of the teachings that were set forth, it is important to note that not all expectations were met, for example he did not lead armies to victory over all their enemies, and he did not restore the reign of David in Jerusalem. And during his time after his resurrection, Jesus spent time explaining why he did not do everything as his followers had expected him to do.	8	2024-06-10 20:55:19+05:30	2024-06-19 05:15:15+05:30	\N	875	637
3190	Biblical hermeneutics is a common word that is used frequently within theology and biblical studies derived from the Greek terminology of 'hermes' the mythological messenger of the gods. The term itself is defined as to 'interpret' or to 'explain'. Therefore when defining what is biblical hermeneutics, the meaning is simply the study of interpreting the meaning and significance of Scripture. Friedrich Schleiermacher (1768-1834) is the father of modern hermeneutics. Biblical hermeneutics refers to the interpretation and or explanation of some type of message or communication.	5	2024-06-10 21:01:15+05:30	2024-06-19 05:16:36+05:30	\N	876	675
3191	The biblical roots of scientific hermeneutics can not be discounted or avoided simply because people living in biblical times were not modern scientists. Quite the contrary, they were sophisticated and had many accomplishments, and there are examples of biblical roots of scientific hermeneutics within the Bible. One notable example is the most influential figure in biblical interpretation of Origen of Alexandria. He lived from A.D. 185-253 and went far beyond scientific interpretations, and devoted himself to careful factual and rational analyses of the Bible. He crafted the Hexapla, a 6,000 page work consisting of more than 50 volumes which made a word-by-word comparison of various Hebrew and Greek version of the Old Testament. \nAnother example would be Augustine of Hippo who would focus on factual and rational analysis of the Bible which at time was often painstaking. Then came Thomas Aquinas, followed by the Renaissance period which analyzed the grammar and historical contexts of the biblical manuscripts. The Protestant Reformation dealt with a thorough analysis of the Bible's grammar and historical context.	7	2024-06-10 21:08:07+05:30	2024-06-19 05:16:36+05:30	\N	876	676
3192	The biblical roots of devotional hermeneutics are emphasized significantly as theologians and biblical scholars have encouraged followers of Christ to read the Scripture time and time again, in the presence of God in order to bring upon enlightenment and even supernatural experiences from God. This can be displayed in Scripture, in the passage of Hebrews 4:12, where the word of God is noted to be living and sharper than any two edged sword and can penetrate the soul. \nExamples of biblical roots of devotional hermeneutics include the Letter of Origen to Gregory where Origen told Gregory that he should devote himself to the divine reading or Lectio Divinia. Throughout the medieval period, Lectio Divinia was practiced in four steps: the reading of the scriptures, the silent ponderings of the content which was read, praying to God to grant enlightenment on what was read, and then waiting for the Spirit of God to grant intuitive and deeply emotional convictions of a passage's significance. \nCalvin had insisted that when you read the Scriptures, you should be so affected as though God himself were face to face with us. Therefore you should take the initiative to read from Scripture as an all consuming, intense emotional and humbling experience in God's presence.	7	2024-06-10 21:17:45+05:30	2024-06-19 05:16:36+05:30	\N	876	677
3193	Two important aspects of organic inspiration are that you need to prepare yourself for when you enter the presence of God whether that is reading or meditating before God. When you get ready for God's presences you are getting your heart ready to believe in God's words as being true. For example, if you believe in false concepts and since before the word of God, without an open heart and mind, you will not be ready to accept the words that you read and you must be willing to draw near to God to read through that behavioral preparation. Another is to seek God's nearness through emotional readiness and preparation. This includes all of our attitudes, preconceived notions, and the our feelings about God, human beings, and the rest of creation. Our emotions can have either a positive or negative affect on how we approach the presence of God and whether we are ready to hear from God. \n\nThe other aspect that you need to get ready for is the investigative piece. You have to look at the context of what the biblical authors intended with their writings and how God intends to bring that message to us. Paying attention to the detail and concepts that God and his authors intended for these writings to communicate with us must be closely tied to the organic acts of inspiration for us to be willing to accept the words we read. Lastly application, don't treat the word of God as a lifeless object, rather be willing to accept the word humbly and on an emotional level being able to apply that Scripture to our attitude and feelings as well.	8	2024-06-10 21:25:20+05:30	2024-06-19 05:16:36+05:30	\N	876	678
3195	The benefits that I have gained from an interpreter of Scripture has unfolded in a few different ways. For example, in my personal devotion, I always try to take the context of the writer and the audience. I then try to imagine the times in which this was written and understand the context and relate it to myself if it were me. I then try to take it in a forward thinking approach to present day and see how I can make it apply to my current day situation. The next thing I have gained is by being able to take conceptual teaching and translate them into modern day examples and be able to teach that back to my family, children, and youth group. For example, one common example is the story of Joseph and how he was abandoned by his family and taken as a prisoner, and then brought in a ruler and had the opportunity to fall into temptation. We can use that one story to describe many things we face on a day to day basis and I believe that once you are able to sit before God, in his presence and mediate, God will reveal many things to you. Another example is how I was able to learn from the life of Jonah, and many times we overlook the tiny worm that came and ate the shade that he was sitting under, but sometimes in our own lives, we overlook the creature, the worm that comes and ends up taking away the one thing that was protecting us and giving us cover. Being able to exegete the scripture, in a meaningful way is to look before the black and white text and allowing God to speak to you.	8	2024-06-10 21:37:26+05:30	2024-06-19 05:16:36+05:30	\N	876	680
3211	The theological basis for giving attention to the biblical writers in our exploration of the meaning of Scripture is done so in three aspects. First the basis is giving attention to the writer, second to the original audience, and lastly to the document itself. Jesus describes in Matthew 22:41-45 that David's authorship of Psalm 110 was being referred to. This led the audience and reader to be specifically in line with the fact that a human author was acknowledged as significant. There is incredible value in understanding the original context that a writer has to understand a specific part of the Scripture. \n\nSecond basis being the original audience, because looking back God would also allow for His Word to be delivered to His people in ways that suited their historical circumstance. God's words and ideas were revealed to his people in a way that would be understood by it's first audience. The words and ideas of Scripture were adapted to meet the needs of the current culture, technology, social structures and even the religious experiences of the time so that everyone who heard it would understand what was being said. Along every spectrum, the Scripture was written to suit the universal condition of humans. \n\nLastly, the detail given to the document requires a reader to go back and look at Scripture itself. While modern translations are great and represent one of the most important teaching ministries of the church, we must be careful and wise to not be dependent on a single word of phrase which may differ from one translation to another. There is organic inspiration and the doctrine of divine accomodation which are two important doctrines that emphasis the biblical doctrines in their respective ways. With organic inspiration, the Holy Spirit inspired humans to write scripture. Whereas with Divine Accomodation, everything in Scripture came from cultural conventions of it's day.	9	2024-06-28 18:15:12+05:30	2024-11-21 02:04:06+05:30	\N	877	681
