3218	The three types of biblical elaborations that are often used in Scripture are broken down into three parts, repetition of a facet of the original meaning, a clarification of things which were not entirely understood, and lastly an expansion of the meaning of a particular passage. The repetition of a facet is confirmed in Deuteronomy 5 where Moses is reminding the nation of the content which was within the Ten Commandments to Israel. This continued repetition enabled the people of God to remember the terms of his covenant. With clarification, we can see this example portrayed with the clarification of the commandment against murder described in Numbers 35. Moses clarifies between murder and other accidental manslaughter and the differences between the two. Lastly with the expansion of the meaning of a particular passage, an example is laid out for us in Matthew chapter 5, where Jesus criticized the rabbis of his day for incorrectly limiting the commandment's scope around murder. Jesus went on to explain the commandment that he had set out and went beyond just simple clarification. It did not just simply explain what was laid out, rather it brought additional clarification to the meaning of the commandment itself.	8	2024-06-29 18:16:48+05:30	2024-08-14 09:36:05+05:30	\N	878	688
