3196	The immutable character of God helps us to see that there will always be a vital connection that occurs between the original meaning and the modern application of Scripture. We as Christians believe in the same God as the audience of Scripture as God is never changing and immutable in his promises and covenants, He continues to fulfill everything that has been sworn. This is displayed in Scripture through various aspects, beginning with Covenant Promises. When a promise is made by the Creator of Heaven and Earth, there is a certain level of weight that comes with that promise, and it is immutable. If it were simply a man that were to make a person, we are flawed and can be free to change our minds, however the Scripture uses several examples, one for example in the context of Abraham where God made a covenant that Abraham's offspring would be as numerous as the stars in the sky.\n\nThere are certain instances where God did not make a promise, and rather his words are best understood to be either threats, or even curses, and also offers of blessings. While all of this has occurred in Scripture, we as mankind continue to live in this same world that continues to evolve and change just as the days in Scripture. The first audiences lived in times where the world was not constant and change was inevitable. The people also are relatable from the first audience to those of us in today's day and age. While we are all created in the image of God, we have all fallen into sin and sinful nature. We continue to also have division amongst us, and religious quarrels. There continues to be several classes of people existing both then and now. Therefore while we may feel that we live in modern times, we should note that many of the similarities exist from the days when Scripture was written to today's day and age, however one thing remains constant, and that is God and His promises.	9	2024-06-15 08:40:47+05:30	2024-06-19 05:17:14+05:30	\N	879	692
