3206	Two predominant first-century schools of thought concerning the resurrection of the dead were between the Pharisees and the Sadducees. The Pharisees believed that there would be a general resurrection of both the righteous and the wicked. While the Sadducees denied that a physical resurrection would take place for the dead. \n\nThis is relevant to note as the Pharisees rooted their belief on the entire Old Testament, whereas the Sadducees only accepted the five books written by Moses (Genesis through Deuteronomy). When looking at Scripture and the positions that Jesus, Paul, and the rest of the early church, the view that was taken was clearly that of the Pharisees. It is clearly taught in Scripture, in Isaiah 26:19, and Daniel 12:2 that there will be a general resurrection, and this will be for both the righteous and the wicked. \n\nEven while the Sadducees would argue and challenge this issue, Jesus responded in Mark 12:26-27 that the Sadducees had indeed misunderstood Moses and that Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob were to be alive as spirits and that they would eventually be resurrected.	8	2024-06-28 07:07:12+05:30	2024-11-21 02:03:27+05:30	\N	881	648
