3226	The Trinitarian formulas in the New Testament help us see the equality of the Holy Spirit with the other members of the Godhead by clear distinction within the Scripture. The Scriptures mentions God the Father, God the Son, and God the Holy Spirit as being equal members in the Trinity. One of these examples is portrayed in water baptism. When an individual is baptized, Scripture teaches us to baptize them in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. One name, three persons in one, and one God. When the book of Matthew ends, Scripture teaches us that Jesus ordered his disciples to go and make disciples of all nations and baptize them in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. He did not command them to baptize them in just the name of the Father, or just the name of the Son, or just the Holy Spirit, but giving equal rights and power among all three to do so. \n\nThe Great Commission and the commands given in 2 Corinthians are clear examples of how the Bible portrays the Trinitarian formulas describing the equality of the Holy Spirit among the other persons of the Triune God. With baptism, we have listed how the command is to baptize in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. And in 2 Corinithians, the command given is to depart in fellowship as 'the grace of our Lord Jesus Chris, the love of the God, and the fellowship of the Holy Spirit be with us all" which describes the three in one approach.	8	2024-08-05 07:18:27+05:30	2024-08-14 09:34:55+05:30	\N	882	931
