3234	The early church councils affirm the eternal procession of the Holy Spirit from the Father. We are not relating this to a  procession of how a being is created or conceived through birth. Rather the Spirit is eternal just like the Father and Son and there is no beginning or end to either. So when we say that the Spirit eternally proceeds from the Father we affirm the divinity of the Holy Spirit. The Council of Nicea and the Council of Constantinople defended this unique attribute of the Holy Spirit  by stating that the Holy Spirit is Lord and to be worshipped as the Father and the son. \n\nIn 381, the Council of Constantinople met and wrote two-thirds of the Nicene Creed and regarding the section of the Holy Spirit stated, "We believe in the Holy Spirit, and the Lord, the giver of life, who proceeds from the Father and the Son, and with the Father and the Son is worshiped and glorified." It reaffirms the divine status of the Holy Spirit and the divine attributes of the Spirit. The Spirit was not created, because if it was created, then it would not be of God, because God was not created, he was God eternal and so was His Son and His Spirit. While this may come with controversy, and theologians argue that the Spirit came or proceeded from the Father and the Son, the Spirit indeed proceeds from Christ and the Son and is taught in John 16, 20, and Galatians 4.	9	2024-08-07 06:47:13+05:30	2024-08-14 09:33:03+05:30	\N	884	939
