3235	Implications of the Council of Toledo for the doctrine of the Holy Spirit was that the Western Church added on very early to the ecumenical councils to describe the relationship of the Son to the Father, and more so the Son and Spirit relationship. The implications revolved mostly around the filioque where the argument revolved around the Father sending the Son, yet the Father and Son sending the Spirit. In Act 2, we can see the Day of Pentecost described where the the people exalted the Lord, and Jesus Christ ascends to the right hand of the Father and from the throne of authority, send the Spirit. With this depiction, the church was trying to isolate the roles and relationships between the Father, the Son and Holy Spirit. \n\nWhile some of the reasons for the division were complicated, the key fundamental is to understand that the Spirit's work is to bear witness to the Son and glorify the Father. With the council at Toledo in 589, the argument made was around the addition of "and the son" which was around the double procession of the Holy Spirit. This was done without consulting or seeking any theological input and caused a series of disputes that lasted for generations and centuries to come. And with this issues the division occurred between the East and West on the doctrine of the Trinity, speicifically around the issues of the filioque which concerned the double procession of the Spirit.	9	2024-08-07 06:53:48+05:30	2024-08-14 09:33:03+05:30	\N	884	940
