3237	Three reasons that critical scholars challenge the authorship of James is because the writer opens up chapter 1 by saying that he is a "servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ." While the writer assumed that this would be sufficient and carry enough authority, this also created reason for confusion to challenge the authorship of the book. Critical scholars have challenged the authorship by stating that there was another James but not the one who indeed wrote the book, the brother of Jesus or son of Zebedee. The second reason is because the author is aware of Hellenistic or Greek culture and James was a Jew from Palestine. However in the time of Jesus, many of the Jews from Palestine had a decent awareness and understanding of Hellenistic and Greek culture. Pseudonyimity, where the written works is assigned to someone else other than the actual author was brought up as there was no mention of the author to Jesus in the writing, and the inaccurate assumption of inaccuracies that the epistles don't match James' theological outlooks in the New Testament. \nEven with these arguments and reasons set forth by critical scholars, the argument is weak at best. The arguments of James being the author and brother of Jesus are actually much stronger than it not being evident. And due to this compelling fact, evangelical scholars affirm that James was indeed the author of the book, and the brother of Jesus.	8	2024-08-09 06:47:50+05:30	2024-08-14 09:31:58+05:30	\N	885	771
