3242	Neither Luke or the book of Acts clearly mentions the name of the author for the book of Acts, however there are two types of evidence, both explicit and implicit that suggest that one person wrote the book of Acts. The explicit evidence suggests that both Luke and Acts are both dedicated to Theophilus, and while Acts does refer to a former book, evidence suggests that the third gospel is what is being mentioned. The implicit evidence is that both of these books (both Luke and the book of Acts) have similarities between them which is inclusive of the orderly account, compositional structure, chronological length, the themes that are mentioned, the similar story lines and the commonalities of a redemptive historical vision, which incorporates the purpose and beliefs along with the author. The fact that there is also a connection between the two books and they are both addressed to Theophilus highly suggests the evidence that it is the same writer.	8	2024-08-19 06:54:04+05:30	2024-11-21 02:02:14+05:30	\N	886	619
3243	While there are explicit and implicit evidence that the writer of Luke and Acts are the same, there is also historical evidence that stems from the early church affirming that the same person wrote these two books. The Muratorian fragment (AD 170-180) confirms that Luke is the author of the third gospel, also known as the book of Luke and the book of Acts. The Anti-Macrionite Prologue which was an introduction to the third gospel, also describes the author of the book of Acts as being Luke. \nThe early church leaders, Irenaeus (AD 130-202) stated, "Luke also, the companion of Paul, recorded in a book the gospel preached by him." This is later affirmed by Clement of Alexandria, Tertullian and Eusebius. While all these confirmations are there, there no single indication in the literature from the early church that anyone other than Luke was the author of the third gospel and the book of Acts. Eusbius, the great church historian wrote around AD 323 that Luke was the author of the book of Acts, and while he was never designated as an apostle, the early church passed on what it had received as the truth, that Luke wrote both of these books.	9	2024-08-19 06:59:58+05:30	2024-11-21 02:02:14+05:30	\N	886	620
