3391	God's authority over creation as absolute, exclusive, and exhaustive help explain just how complete His rule is. First let's discuss how God's authority is absolute. This means that there is no limits to His power or control. He has made everything by His words including everything in heaven and on earth. Because of this, everything belongs to Him. Nothing can stop God from doing what He has planned. This was very clear in the scriptures. You can see many examples of where He clam the storms, raise the dead, and even changed the hearts of kings. God authority is not shared with anyone and no one can overrule Him. Second we see that God's authority is exclusive. This means that He has the right to rule creation. There is no one that can claim to be the Lord of the universe. There are many other false gods, spirits, and idols but they have no authority. Most if not all of these so called gods are still under God's power. Last but not least you see that God's authority is exhaustive. This means that it covers every part of creation. There is no place, no situation, no moment in time that is outside of His control. From the largest galaxy to the smallest detail of our life we can have assurance that God is in charge of it all. Even if things happen to us that may feel as if they are random or accidental, they are all under God's sovereign will. With all of these truths in minds, we can be at peace knowing that everything is in God's hands. This should also give us the confidence that his plans will always come to pass.	0	2025-09-18 07:42:08+05:30	2025-09-18 07:42:08+05:30	\N	913	875
3392	Whenever the Bible uses the term or call Jesus the "Son of God" it means that Jesus has a very special and very unique relationship with God. You can see in different parts of the Bible when the words "Son of God" is used, it also refers to angels, kings, or even believers. For these examples, it just means that they belong to God or that they were chosen for something special or important. However, when the title is used for Jesus it's different. It means so much more. Jesus is not just close to God but rather He is actually God's eternal Son. He has always existed with God the Father and the Holy Spirit. That makes Jesus equal with God. He has the same power, wisdom, love, and holiness as God the Father. The scriptures goes further and shows us this in two distinct ways. The first is that Jesus is fully God, the same as the Father and the Spirit. Secondly, it explains that Jesus took on a role where He chose to obey the father to save the world. Don't misunderstand this by thinking that it makes Jesus less than God, on the contrary it shows the Father, Son, and Spirit work together but have different roles.	0	2025-09-27 07:14:01+05:30	2025-09-27 07:14:01+05:30	\N	914	876
3393	Whenever the Bible calls Jesus "Lord" it is showing that He is truly God. In the Greek language, the word "Kurios" is used in reference to "Lord." This means master, ruler, or someone with authority. However, when you look at the Old Testament whenever the Jewish scholars translated God's name "Yahweh" into Greek, they used the same word "Kurios." So what this tells us is that when the New Testament calls Jesus "Lord" it is not just for the sake of saying that he is a respected teacher or even a leader but rather that He is equal to God. Calling Jesus "Lord" shows that He has authority over all people and all creation. It means that He rules as King and is worthy of worship like God the Father. We pray to Jesus and worship Him because we believe that He is divine. If Jesus was only a man then it would be wrong to worship Him, but because He is God, this makes it right. The title of "Lord" also shows that Jesus is the one who saves His people. You can see in the Old Testament that God is the one who redeems His people from sin as well as from slavery. In the New Testament you can see that Jesus takes on this role. By calling Him "Lord" the writers of the Bible makes it clear that Jesus is the same God who has the power to forgive sins as well as bring salvation.	0	2025-09-27 07:27:08+05:30	2025-09-27 07:27:08+05:30	\N	914	877
3394	This life of Jesus shows many clear signs of how He was truly human. This was not the case of God just appearing in disguise. Let's first look at His birth. Yes He was conceived by the Holy Spirit but He was born as a baby by the Virgin Mary.  Even though His birth was a miracle, He still grew inside Mary's womb just like every other baby. He did not just magically appear but rather he went through the normal process of being born. Secondly, Jesus had a real human body. He grew up from that baby to an adult. He ate food, got tired, and even felt pain. He could bleed, suffer, and eventually die. All of which are the same things that normal humans go through. The Apostles Creed even mentions that He was crucified, died and was buried. All of these experiences are only possible if He has a real physical body. Next you can see that Jesus had a human soul. Just like everyone else, Jesus had emotions, thoughts, and a spirit. While Jesus was on the cross He prayed to God the father and gave up His spirit. this shows that His inner life was truly human as well. Last looking at his resurrection, this proves His humanity. When He rose from the dead, it just wasn't a spirit that was floating away but rather it was His real human body that was raised and reunited with His soul. You can even see where he showed his disciples his wounds and even at food with them after rising from the dead. This proves that He was a real man. All of these experiences, the birth, body, soul, suffering, death, and resurrection, shows that Jesus was fully human.	0	2025-09-27 07:39:29+05:30	2025-09-27 07:39:29+05:30	\N	914	878
3395	Often times there are many who misunderstand the word "Christ" and think that it is the last name of Jesus. This is not the case. The word "Christ" is a title. In the Old Testament, the Hebrew word Messiah and the Greek word Christos both mean anointed one. This title was given to people who were chosen by God for a special job. You can see throughout scriptures that kings, priests and prophets were sometimes anointed with oil to show that God had set them apart to lead, serve, and guide His people. The Old Testament also promised a future great king who is a descendant of King David. This king would be the "Anointed One" or the Messiah. This messiah would rule with justice, save God's people and bring peace to the world. Israel waited and hoped for this Messiah for hundreds of years. Jesus fulfills this office in every way. He is the true Christ and the Messiah that the Old Testament points to. As a descendant of David, Jesus is the rightful King. As a priest, Jesus offered Himself as the perfect sacrifice to pay for our sins and still represents us before God. As a prophet, Jesus revealed God's truth very clearly by teaching people about God's Kingdom and how to live in it. Jesus fulfilled all of these roles, prophet, priest, and king. This shows us that Jesus is the Christ. He is not just another leader or another teacher but rather He is the one that God has promised to send, the Messiah.	0	2025-09-27 07:52:41+05:30	2025-09-27 07:52:41+05:30	\N	914	879
3396	Jesus is unique because he has two natures. You see that he is fully human and he is fully divine at the same time. This idea is called hypostatic union. This means that in one person Jesus has both a human nature and a divine nature. The human nature of Jesus was just like us in many ways. He had a real body that had the same types of experiences that we do as well. For example he could get hungry, tired, and feel pain. He had a mind that could learn and grow and he also experienced emotions like job, sadness, and compassion. He suffered and died as well. All of which humans can do. This shows us that Jesus was truly one of us and that He shared in our struggles and because of this He is able to relate and understand what we are going through. Now at the same time He is also divine in nature. This means that He is God. He has all the attributes of God. He is eternal, all powerful, holy, and perfect. Because He had all of these attributes, he could do things that no other person was able to do. Examples of this are found throughout scripture where He preformed miracles, forgave sins, and rose from the dead. What is important is that both of these natures are not mixed together. Jesus is not half of a God or half of a man but rather he is fully God and fully man at the same time. His human nature and His divine nature remain distinct and are united perfectly in one person. This means that Jesus can bridge the gap between God and humanity. As God, Jesus has the power to save us at the same time, as man He can represent us before God.	0	2025-09-27 08:09:32+05:30	2025-09-27 08:09:32+05:30	\N	914	880
3397	The Apostles' Creed affirms the Holy Spirit's divinity in an indirect manner. You can see in the Apostles' Creed where it says, "I believe in the Holy Spirit." Even though it may appear brief it has a very powerful meaning. The Apostles Creed is written in three main sections. It covers God the Father, Jesus the Son, and the Holy Spirit. This Trinitatian structure shows that the Holy Spirit is placed on the same level as the Father and the Son. By doing so, the Apostles' Creed treats the Holy Spirit as fully divine and not as a lesser being or anything of that nature. The Creed also mentions that Jesus was conceived by the Holy Spirit. This can be found in the Gospel specifically in Luke where the angel tells Mary the power of the Most High will come upon her. That power mentioned is God's alone. When the Holy Spirit performs this miracle, it shows that He shares God's divine power and nature. The Bible itself also calls the Spirit by divine names like "Spirit of God" and "Spirit of Christ." You can see that this credits the Holy Spirit with divine works such as creating the world, inspiring Scripture and renewing believers' hearts. The Spirit's divinity is not only shown in two main ways by first its Trinitarian structure which places Him equally with the Father and Son and by describing His role in conception of Jesus, an act only God could perform. Together these truths affirm that the Holy Spirit is not just a helper or some kind of influence but rather is truly God Himself.	0	2025-10-05 07:45:47+05:30	2025-10-05 07:45:47+05:30	\N	915	881
3398	The Apostles' Creed teaches us that the Holy Spirit is fully God. This belief comes directly from the Bible. You see the Apostolic Creed by itself is not meant to replace the Word of God but rather what it does is summarizes what the Bible already says about the Holy Spirit's divine nature. The Word shows the Spirit's divinity through His names, characteristics, and actions. You can see throughout Scripture the different names of the Holy Spirit. The Bible reveals who He is. He is called the Spirit of God, the Spirit of the Lord, the Spirit of Christ, and the Spirit of the Living God. These titles show that the spirit is not separate from God but one with Him. You can also see an example in the Book of Acts where Peter was telling Ananias that lying the Holy Spirit is the same as lying to God. This makes it very clear that the Holy Spirit is God. Next the Bible gives the Holy Spirit divine qualities that only God can have. You see throughout Scripture where it mentions that He is eternal, meaning He has no beginning or end. He is all knowing meaning he understands even the deepest thoughts of God. He is also all powerful and present everywhere. The Holy Spirit's work also reveal His divinity. He took part in the creation of the world, giving life to all living things, inspiring the writers of the Bible, and changing people's hearts by bringing them to faith in Jesus. These are examples of thing that God can only do.	0	2025-10-05 08:00:51+05:30	2025-10-05 08:00:51+05:30	\N	915	882
3399	The Bible shows us clearly that the Holy Spirit is not just a spiritual force or some kind of an energy but rather a real person with a mind, a personal will, and real emotions. He speaks, as well as teaches and guides and shows love. This shows that He has personal qualities that only a living being can have. These traits reveal that the Holy Spirit is a divine person and not just a power or symbol of God's presence. An example of this can be found in 1 Corinthians 2:10-11 where the Word says the Spirit "searches all things, even the deep things of God." This helps us see that the Holy Spirit has intelligence and understanding. This means that He knows and understands the thoughts of God perfectly which no created being could ever do. He also teaches believers spiritual truth reminding them of what Jesus said. This shows actions of a thinking person where the Spirit has knowledge and communicates it to others. You can also see where the Spirit also has a will. In 1 Corinthians 12:11 it goes on to say that the Spirit gives different spiritual gifts to believers "just as He wills." This means that the Holy Spirit makes decisions based on the purpose and wisdom He has which goes on to prove that He acts intentionally. Another example you can find is in Ephesians 4:30 where the Holy Spirit is showing emotions. Here we can see where Christians are warned not to "grieve the Holy Spirit." This shows that the Holy Spirit can feel sorrow when the people sin or reject God's ways. On the other hand you can see in Romans 15:30 where Paul speaks of the "love of the Spirit." This shows that the Spirit feels and expresses love towards believers. Lastly were can see where the Spirit takes personal actions as He guides people, intercedes for them in prayer, speaks to believers, and calls people to serve. Again these actions are that only a person can accomplish and not that of some being of energy or power.	0	2025-10-05 08:17:49+05:30	2025-10-05 08:17:49+05:30	\N	915	883
3400	The Bible teaches that God is one in essence but exists in three persons, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. These three persons are equal in power, glory, and divinity. Even though they are equal they are distinct from one another in their roles and in their relationships. The Holy Spirit is fully God just like the Father and the Son but He is also a unique person within the Trinity. You can see specifically that the Holy Spirit is distinct in His identity. As stated clearly in John 14:16-17, the Father is not the Son, the Son is not the Spirit, and the Spirit is not the Father. Jesus said that the Father would send another Helper. He was referring to the Holy Spirit as the another person. The Spirit is the one who will continue the work of Jesus among His believers. Next you can see that the Spirit is distinct in His roles and in His functions. You can see in the Scripture that each person of the Trinity carries out specific works. The Father plans and sends while the Son accomplishes redemption through His life, death and resurrection. The Holy Spirit now has a role that applies that salvation to the believers by giving them faith, transforming their hearts, and guiding them in truth. In addition to this the Spirit intercedes for believers by speaking to God the Father on their behalf. This clearly shows the interaction between distinct persons. Lastly we can see that the Spirit is distinct in relationship. In the "Economic Trinity" where it describes how the persons of God work tother, the Spirit carries out the will of the Father and of the Son however in the "Ontological Trinity" all three are equal in being and in glory.	0	2025-10-05 08:32:17+05:30	2025-10-05 08:32:17+05:30	\N	915	884
3401	The relationship of the Holy Spirit to that of the Father and the Son is shown in unity and cooperation within the Trinity. The Trinity means that God exists as one divine being in three equal and eternal persons. These persons are as the Father the Son, and the Holy Spirit. Each of these persons is fully God while still sharing the same divine nature but yet they are still distinct in their roles and relationships. Looking into their relationships, you can see that the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit are completely equal in power and in glory but they relate in different ways. Starting with the Father, He is often seen as the source or the origin. He sends the Son into the world and later sends the Spirit. The Son in turn willingly obeys the Father and accomplishes the work of salvation through His life, death, and His resurrection, The Holy Spirit's role here is to carry out and apply the will of the Father and the work of the Son. The Spirit brings God's presence to believers and gives them a new spiritual life and even guides them in the truth. You can see in John 15:26 where Jesus describes this relationship where "When the Helper comes, whom I will send to you from the Father, the Spirit of truth who proceeds from the Father, He will testify about Me." This shows us that the Spirit comes from both the Father as well as the Son. Not only that but it also shows that the Spirit works to glorify them as well. You can also see in Romans 8:9-11 where it explains that the Spirit of God and the Spirit of Christ dwells in believers. This shows the unity and the harmony that is between all three persons. Even though the Holy Spirit is called the third person of the Trinity, it does not mean that He is any less important. Rather on the other hand it shows the traditional order in how God's work is revealed. The Father is usually mentioned first as the sender, next is the Son as the Savior, and then comes the Holy Spirit as the one who applies God's power and presence to believers. The relationship here to the Father and the Son is that of quarry divinity and shared purpose with a distinct role.	0	2025-10-05 08:49:20+05:30	2025-10-05 08:49:20+05:30	\N	915	885
